hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4201","And as Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, from Malik. And what Malik said therein was worthier with us than what al-Layth said therein; for it is with the Arabs of what they mentioned in their poems, and of what their women boasted. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted to us, from Abu Ubayd, he said: Abu Ubaydah and al-Yazidi and al-Asma'i and others said: Al-ghayl is that he have intercourse with his wife while she is suckling. He said: And the Arabs say to a man praising him: His mother neither bore him with menstruation, and of them are those who say: with weakness, nor suckled him with ghayl, nor delivered him breech, nor made him weep intensely at night. So their saying: she did not bear him with menstruation, means: she did not bear him upon menstruation. And their saying: nor suckled him with ghayl, they mean: that she be had intercourse with while suckling. And nor delivered him breech, they mean: that his legs come out before his hands in birth; it is said thereof: mutin for the woman who delivered him thus, and for the child: mutan. And their saying: nor made him weep intensely at night, and some say: nor made him spend the night upon intense weeping, for it is severity of weeping. So that indicated in al-ghayl what Malik said therein. And there was narrated, in what was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in his permitting intercourse with the suckling woman."
"4202","3671 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Ayyash ibn Abbas informed me, he said: Abu al-Nadr informed me, from Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, that Usamah ibn Zayd informed his father Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I practice coitus interruptus with my wife. He said: Why? He said: I fear for the child. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If it is for that, then no; it never harmed Persia and Rome. Abu Ubayd said, in what Ali permitted to us: As for his saying, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): It overtakes the horseman and throws him off, he says: it defeats him and dashes him after he became a man who rode horses. And from Allah Almighty we ask success."
"4203","577 - Chapter: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning dyeing the hair: of dislike and of permission. Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned in what preceded from us of this book of ours the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, concerning the ten things which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to dislike; and in them: altering grey hair. And the best that presented itself to us therein is that we found from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3672 - What Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah and Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Christians do not dye, so oppose them."
"4204","3673 - And what Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us; and Bahar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, he informed him, and he did not mention Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof."
"4205","3674 - And what Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah and Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like thereof."
"4206","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Hurayth narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah — and he did not mention Sulayman — from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he said: ""So differ from them and dye."""
"4207","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Asadi narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah — and he did not mention Sulayman — from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""The Jews and the Christians do not dye, so differ from them."""
"4208","Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Awza'i saying: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. These reports contain the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing that the Jews and the Christians did not dye, so we understood thereby that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at first upon what they were upon, because of what we mentioned from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in matters in which he had not been commanded anything, loved to agree with the People of the Book in what they held, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained upon that until Allah Almighty introduced into his law what contradicts it, namely dyeing, so he commanded it and commanded differing from the Jews and the Christians in their abandoning it, and we understood thereby that all that is reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding the use of dye is later than that. Among what is reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that:"
"4209","Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Change grey hair and do not imitate the Jews."""
"4210","Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Janab al-Missisi narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"4211","'Ali ibn Ma'bad and Abu Umayyah narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Kunasah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from al-Zubayr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Change grey hair and do not imitate the People of the Book."" Abu Ja'far said: The hadith of 'Urwah here became confused for us in its chain: Abu Mu'awiyah reported it from Hisham, from his father, from 'A'ishah; 'Isa ibn Yunus reported it from Hisham, from his father, from Ibn 'Umar; and Ibn Kunasah reported it from Hisham, from his brother 'Uthman, from his father, from al-Zubayr — and this is severe confusion. Then we returned to what is reported from others on it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4212","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Ajlah, from Ibn Buraydah, from Abu al-Aswad, from Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4213","Abu Umayyah also narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from Ibn Buraydah, from Abu al-Aswad al-Dili, from Abu Dharr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The best thing with which grey hair is changed is henna and katam."" Abu Ja'far said: This came soundly, with no confusion in it."
"4214","Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: Abu Quhafah was brought on the day of the conquest of Makkah, his head and beard white like a thaghamah, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Change this with something, and avoid black."""
"4215","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd, son-in-law of 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa, narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn 'Abbad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from Asma', she said: On the day of the conquest, Abu Quhafah was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his head and beard as if a thaghamah. He said: ""Change him, and keep him away from black."" Abu Ja'far said: This also came soundly, with no confusion in it."
"4216","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad — meaning Ibn Sirin — he said: Anas was asked: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dye? He said: He had seen only some grey, and he minimized it."
"4217","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: Anas was asked about the dye of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had hardly greyed except slightly, but Abu Bakr and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) dyed with henna and katam. He said: Abu Bakr brought Abu Quhafah on the day of the conquest of Makkah to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Had you left the old man in his house, we would have come to him,"" as an honour to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: His head and beard were white like a thaghamah, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Change them, and keep them away from black."""
"4218","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated to us, from Makhul, from Musa ibn Anas ibn Malik, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not reach such grey as to dye it, but Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) dyed his beard and head with henna and katam until his hair became deep red. This also came soundly, with no confusion in it. In these reports is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command to dye, and in the hadith of Anas is his informing that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not dye. So we looked: was anything contrary to that reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or not?"
"4219","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Iyad ibn Laqit al-Sadusi narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Abu Rimthah, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a stain of henna on his head."
"4220","Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Iyad ibn Laqit, from Abu Rimthah, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with grey overcoming him, and he had changed it with henna. Abu Ja'far said: In what we reported from Abu Rimthah here is what contradicts what we reported from Anas ibn Malik, and one who affirms something is worthier than one who denies it — besides, the hadith of Anas ibn Malik only minimizes the grey of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among that is what we mentioned earlier in this chapter, and also:"
"4221","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn 'Iyad informed me, from Rabi'ah, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died with not twenty white hairs in his head and beard. It is possible that his grey (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was this number, and that he dyed it with a dye that others besides Anas saw and Anas did not see; and it is possible that from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) there was no dyeing with henna, but he yellowed it, and the like of that is hidden — especially from one who had in his heart for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) such veneration, reverence and respect that he would not scrutinize him — so the like of this would be hidden from him."
"4222","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: I said to Anas: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dye? He said: He had seen only little grey, and he mentioned nothing else; but Abu Bakr and 'Umar dyed with henna and katam. This has been reported of what 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) practiced of dyeing, and contrary to that has also been reported about his position."
"4223","Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, Malik ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Sayf, and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us, he said: Thabit ibn al-'Ajlan narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu 'Amir al-Ansari saying: I saw Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) changing with henna and katam, and I saw 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) not changing his grey at all, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Whoever greys a grey hair in Islam, it is a light for him on the Day of Resurrection, so I do not love to change my grey."" Except that 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman said in his hadith: ""So I do not love to change my light."" He said: In this, from 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), is abandoning the changing of grey for what was told from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it, and that in our view — and Allah knows best — is what he was upon at first; then he later came to know that that does not prevent dyeing, so he dyed. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4224","Chapter 578: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on yellowing the beard — of dislike, of permissibility, of approval of it and preferring it over anything else. Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned in the hadith of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the ten things he disliked, among them yellowness, which is a form of changing grey hair. We mentioned on changing grey with henna and katam what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, and in that is evidence that the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dislike of yellowness was only because the People of the Book did not do it, so he was upon what they were upon in that; then he commanded differing from them, so he also dyed with yellowness. Likewise he disliked it as the People of the Book disliked it until he commanded differing from them, so he dyed yellow, and it was reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about it: Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from 'Ubayd ibn Jurayj, that he said to 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, I saw you dyeing yellow. He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dyeing with it, so I love to dye with it."
"4225","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abdah ibn 'Abd al-Rahim informed me, he said: 'Amr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Rawwad informed us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wore Sibti sandals and yellowed his beard with wars and saffron, and Ibn 'Umar did that."
"4226","Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hakim informed us, he said: Abu Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ubayd ibn Jurayj, he said: I saw Ibn 'Umar yellowing his beard, and he was spoken to about it, so he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) yellowing his beard. Abu Ja'far said: In this too is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) use of yellowness, and it is also reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) approving it and preferring it over others."
"4227","Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Wahb al-Qurashi, from the two sons of Tawus, from their father Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: A man passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) having dyed his beard with henna, and he said: ""How fine this is!"" Then another man passed after him having dyed with henna and katam, and he said: ""This is finer than the first."" And a man passed having dyed yellow, and he said: ""This is finer than all of these."" And Tawus used to dye yellow."
"4228","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn 'Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Humayd narrated to me — so he said — then he mentioned the like with its chain, except that he did not mention: and Tawus used to dye, and except that he said, in place of what is in al-Rabi's hadith from the two sons of Tawus: the sons of Tawus narrated to me, from their father."
"4229","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Talhah, then he mentioned with his chain the like, except that he did not mention in it: and Tawus used to dye. In this hadith is the preferring of yellowness over the other things with which grey is changed. All the things with which grey is changed — redness and yellowness — reports have come permitting them. As for changing with black, we mentioned in the story of Abu Quhafah the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command to them to keep him away from black. So we looked into the reason for which he disliked black."
"4230","Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, raised, he said: ""There will be a people at the end of time who dye black like the crops of doves; they will not smell the fragrance of Paradise."" We understood thereby that the dislike was only for that — because they are the deeds of blameworthy people — not because it is in itself unlawful. And some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dyed black. Among them: 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir."
"4231","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Amr ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Lahi'ah, from Abu 'Ushshanah, he said: 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir used to dye black, and he said... And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: I said to Ibn Lahi'ah: Did Abu 'Ushshanah narrate this hadith to you? Then he mentioned this hadith? He said: I did not hear it from Abu 'Ushshanah, but al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated it to me, from Abu 'Ushshanah. Abu Ja'far said: Ibn Abi Dawud said to us: al-Layth ibn Sa'd heard nothing from Abu 'Ushshanah except this hadith, and Ibn Lahi'ah heard nothing from al-Layth except this hadith."
"4232","And it is reported from al-Husayn ibn 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) also that he dyed black. As Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shaybah al-Juddi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I entered upon al-Husayn ibn 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) wearing a garment of khazz, cupping in Ramadan, and he had dyed black."
"4233","And as Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn 'Awn informed us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, he said: al-'Ayzaar ibn Hurayth narrated to us, he said: I saw on al-Husayn ibn 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) a cloak of khazz, and he had dyed his beard and head with henna and katam. In this hadith is evidence that dyeing black in itself was disliked only out of fear of what we mentioned — imitating blameworthy people — not because it is in itself unlawful. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4234","Chapter 580: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the dislike of withdrawing water from its place. We mentioned in the hadith of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Ibn Mas'ud, the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) dislike, among the things he disliked: withdrawing water from its place. And we reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book that he said about withdrawal: ""It is hidden infanticide."" The sense of that in our view — and Allah knows best — may be that he was confirming the People of the Book in what they said agreeing with that, until Allah Almighty informed him of their lie in it, so he said about it to whom he addressed: ""The Jews lied."" And we mentioned that earlier in this book. And a people held that the sperm of the man itself contains a spirit, and preventing it from the womb and diverting it elsewhere is destroying that spirit. Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned earlier in this book that 'Ali ibn Abi Talib had said to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): In the Book of Allah is what refutes that, and he recited to him His Almighty saying: ""And We created man from an extraction of clay,"" up to His saying: ""Then We produced him as another creation."" 'Umar marvelled at that and rewarded 'Ali for it. And we reported from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) also the like. Then we examined it and found in the Book of Allah Almighty what outwardly refutes that, namely His Almighty saying: ""And He began the creation of man from clay, then He made his progeny from an extraction of despised water, then He fashioned him and breathed into him of His spirit."" So He informed us that His breathing the spirit into him is only after fashioning him, and his fashioning is only in the wombs of women. As Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: al-Minhal ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr on His Almighty saying: ""We created you, then We fashioned you."" He said: We created you in the loins of men, then We fashioned you in the wombs of women."
"4235","And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from al-Minhal ibn 'Amr, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn 'Abbas on His Almighty saying: ""And We created you, then We fashioned you."" He said: They were created in the back of Adam, then fashioned in wombs. We understood thereby that the breathing of the spirit is only after fashioning, and in that is evidence invalidating the saying of one who said about the sperm what we mentioned. And in the hadith of 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud — which we shall mention later in this book where it is more fitting than this place, from the hadith of al-A'mash and Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud — is mention of the breathing of the spirit after the fashioning of the sperm, after it becomes a clot, then becomes a lump. So a speaker said: What then is the meaning of what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on withdrawal?"
"4236","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhayriz al-Jumahi informed me that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri informed him that while he was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man of the Ansar came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, we take captives and love the price, so what do you think about withdrawal? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do you really do that? It is no harm upon you not to do that, for there is no soul that Allah Almighty has decreed to come forth except that it comes forth."""
"4237","Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban narrated to me that Ibn Muhayriz narrated to him that Abu Sa'id narrated to him that some people spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about withdrawal, concerning the raid on Banu al-Mustaliq, when they took captives and disliked that they bear children from them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""It is no harm upon you not to withdraw, for Allah Almighty has decreed what He creates until the Day of Resurrection."""
"4238","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Rabi'ah ibn Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, and he mentioned with his chain the like."
"4239","Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"4240","Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Mutarrif, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Khaybar, we took captives and used to withdraw from them. Some of us said to others: Do you do this while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is beside you and you do not ask him? So they asked him about it, and he said: ""Not from all water is the child; when Allah Almighty wills to create a thing, nothing prevents Him, so it is no harm upon you not to withdraw."""
"4241","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq al-Sabi'i, he said: I heard Abu al-Waddak narrating from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: When we took the captives of Khaybar, we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about withdrawal. He said: ""Not from all water is the child, and when Allah wills to create a thing, nothing prevents Him."" This speaker said: If withdrawal is permissible, how can it be said in these reports that Allah Almighty, when He wills to create a thing, nothing prevents Him — while creation is only from the sperm that reaches the womb, so if it does not reach it, it is impossible for there to be a decree preventing a child. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: with His subtle power, Allah Almighty may, when He has decreed a child from a sperm, convey to the womb whatever He wills of it despite the withdrawal of its owner, so from what He conveys is the child decreed to be from it. It may be conveyed whole to the womb, though His decree preceded that no child be from it, so no child is from it. The child is only from what He decreed it be from, whether with withdrawal or without. Withdrawal may occur, yet by His subtle power Allah conveys of that withdrawn water to the womb what the child's formation is from. Thus every created one is only by Allah Almighty's prior decree, not by the sperm itself; and one for whom Allah did not decree a child from it, none is. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave withdrawal no effect in that, permitted it to whoever wishes, and did not forbid it — except that he informed him that it does not prevent a decree of Allah Almighty if it preceded. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4242","Chapter 581: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a man cursing his brother. Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Dharr narrated to us, from al-'Ayzaar ibn Jarwal, that he said: Among them was a man called Abu 'Umayr, a friend of Ibn Mas'ud. Ibn Mas'ud came to his house and did not find him among his family; he sought permission from his family and entered upon them and asked them for drink. The woman sent the servant to the neighbours seeking drink, then deemed her slow and cursed her. 'Abd Allah went out and sat at the side of the house, and Abu 'Umayr came and said: May Allah have mercy on you, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman — is one like you kept waiting? Why did you not enter upon your brother's daughter, greet her and take some drink? He said: I did — I entered upon them, greeted and asked them for drink; either they had no drink or they withheld what they had, so the woman sent the servant to the neighbours seeking drink, then deemed her slow and cursed her. And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""When the curse is directed, it heads to the one it was directed to; if it finds a way against him or a course into him, it enters him, otherwise it cries out to its Lord Almighty and says: O Lord, Your servant So-and-so directed me to So-and-so, and I found no way against him nor course in him, so what do You command me? It is said to it: Return whence you came."" So I feared the servant was excused, and the curse would return and I would be its path — that is what made me leave. Al-Kaysani did not mention in this hadith between Ibn Mas'ud and al-'Ayzaar anyone, and al-'Ayzaar is an old man, so it is possible he narrated this hadith taking it from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, and possible that between them in it is another who narrated it to him from him. So we examined that."
"4243","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Dharr informed us, he said: al-'Ayzaar ibn Jarwal narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu 'Umayr — a friend of 'Abd Allah — narrating from 'Abd Allah, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""When the curse is directed to someone it heads; if it finds a way against him or a course in him, it enters upon him, otherwise it returns to its Lord Almighty and says: O Lord, So-and-so directed me to So-and-so, and I found no way against him nor course in him, so what do You command me? He says: Return whence you came."" We understood thereby that al-'Ayzaar took this hadith from this Abu 'Umayr, from 'Abd Allah. In this hadith: when a man curses a man, if the cursed one deserves it, his curse courses in him; if otherwise, it returns to the one it came from and courses in him. A speaker said: You reported earlier in this book, on the woman who cursed her camel and the man who cursed his camel, his command that those two camels not accompany them, because they became cursed, and because the curse of the curser is a supplication against the one he cursed — and it may coincide with an hour from Allah Almighty in which His giving is attained. Yet this hadith indicates that the two who cursed their she-camels coincided with such an hour from Allah Almighty, so their two she-camels came to expulsion and distancing, though they bear no sin, while the curse did not return to the curser to course in him when it found no course in the two cursed she-camels — and this is severe contradiction. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: cursing things that bear no sins and no accountability means supplicating curse upon them, so that supplication is turned back upon its author as punishment — he is barred from benefiting from what he cursed, and that harms him; the cursed thing itself suffers no harm, rather it is relieved of the use it performed before. Cursing a man is cursing one who is accountable and in whom may be blameworthy traits by which he is cursed; so one who curses him is not excessive, for Allah Almighty cursed the wrongdoers and said in His Book: ""Those — Allah curses them, and the curser curse them,"" and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed in his qunut in prayer those he cursed, saying: ""O Allah, curse Lihyan, Ri'l, Dhakwan, and 'Usayyah which disobeyed Allah and His Messenger."" That caused their perishing until none remained. If the cursed one is otherwise — for his curser insulted him beyond what anyone is insulted with — he deserves punishment for insulting him, so Allah Almighty made his punishment the curse's return upon him and coursing in him, until he is in the state he wanted the one he cursed to be in by cursing him. We seek refuge in Allah from that, and ask Him for success."
"4244","Chapter 582: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: ""Whoever is gladdened by his good deed and grieved by his evil deed is a believer."" Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Suqah informed us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab addressed the people at al-Jabiyah, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us addressing, in this station of mine among you, saying: ""Be good to my Companions, then those who follow them, then lying spreads, until a man begins with testimony before he is asked it, and with an oath before he is asked it. Whoever among you wants the midst of Paradise, let him keep to the community, for Satan is with the one, and is farther from the two. Let none of you be alone with a woman, for Satan is the third of them. Whoever is gladdened by his good deed and grieved by his evil deed is a believer."" Thus Muhammad ibn 'Ali narrated this hadith to us, saying in it: the midst (buhbuhah) of Paradise. He said: And 'Abd Allah said, and others said — as if meaning other than Muhammad ibn Suqah —: the midst (bahbahah) of Paradise."
"4245","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdah ibn Sulayman in Egypt narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Suqah informed us, then he mentioned with his chain the like, except that he said: the midst (bahbahah) of Paradise."
"4246","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mukhtar, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Whoever is grieved by his evil deed and gladdened by his good deed is a believer."" Abu Ja'far said: Thus Hammad reported this hadith, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mukhtar, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Ibn al-Zubayr, without mentioning anyone between them. And Abu 'Awanah reported it likewise."
"4247","Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, then he mentioned the like, except that he said: ""Be good to my Companions."" And Qaza'ah ibn Suwayd al-Bahili also reported it likewise."
"4248","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, he said: Qaza'ah ibn Suwayd al-Bahili narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab addressed us, then he mentioned the like. And Ma'mar ibn Rashid also reported it likewise."
"4249","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Rafi' informed us, from 'Abd al-Razzaq, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) stood at al-Jabiyah addressing, then he mentioned the like. And Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq also reported it likewise, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr."
"4250","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Tamim and Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan informed us, they said: Hajjaj — namely Ibn Muhammad — narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Umar, then he mentioned the like. And al-Husayn ibn Waqid also reported it likewise, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, adding the hearing of 'Abd al-Malik from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr."
"4251","As Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr addressing, he said: I heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) addressing, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressing, then he mentioned the like exactly. And Shayban al-Nahwi also reported it, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, inserting between him and Ibn al-Zubayr an unnamed man."
"4252","Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban — namely al-Nahwi — narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from a man who heard 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab addressed in al-Sham, then he mentioned the like. Except that we found this hadith from the narration of 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Amr al-Raqqi, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, naming the man between him and Ibn al-Zubayr in this hadith — that he is Mujahid."
"4253","As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Mujahid, from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed the people saying: ""O people, whoever wants the midst of Paradise, let him keep to the community, for Satan is with the solitary, and is farther from the two..."" And he did not mention the rest of the hadith. It is possible that what 'Abd al-Malik had, from Mujahid, from Abu al-Zubayr, from 'Umar, is only what is in the hadith specifically, and what he had of the rest of this hadith is from Mujahid, or from another, from Ibn al-Zubayr — and Allah knows best the truth of the matter. Then we found Isra'il ibn Yunus reporting this hadith from 'Abd al-Malik, from Jabir ibn Samurah, not from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr."
"4254","As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr narrated to us, he said: Jabir ibn Samurah narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed us at al-Jabiyah, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us in my station among you today, saying: ""Do good to my Companions, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then lying spreads, until a man testifies to testimony without being asked it, and a man swears an oath without being asked to swear. Whoever is gladdened by the midst of Paradise, let him keep to the community, for Satan is with the solitary, and is farther from the two. Let no man be alone with a woman, for Satan is the third of them. Whoever is gladdened by his good deed and grieved by his evil deed is a believer."" And Jarir ibn Hazim also reported it likewise, from 'Abd al-Malik."
"4255","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us — (h) — and as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr narrated to us, he said: Jabir ibn Samurah narrated to us, he said: 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among us, then he mentioned the like. Then we found Abu al-Muhayyah Yahya ibn Ya'la al-Taymi reporting this hadith from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Qabisah ibn Jabir."
"4256","As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Muhayyah Yahya ibn Ya'la narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Qabisah ibn Jabir, he said: 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) addressed us, then he mentioned this hadith. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this hadith to grasp what is in it of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Whoever is gladdened by his good deed and grieved by his evil deed is a believer,"" Allah willing. His saying ""whoever is gladdened by his good deed"" may mean: whoever is gladdened by his good deed as he hopes for Allah Almighty's acceptance of it from him; and his saying ""whoever is grieved by his evil deed"" as he fears Allah Almighty's punishment upon him for it — out of faith. For whoever hopes from Allah Almighty the like of what he hoped, and fears from Him the like of what he feared, upon the praiseworthy states with which Allah Almighty described the praiseworthy among His creation by His saying: ""Those whom they call seek the means to their Lord, which of them is nearest, and hope for His mercy and fear His punishment."" Whoever is thus in hope from Allah and fear of Him is a believer. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4257","Chapter 583: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from his Lord Almighty, on His servant who committed a sin, confessed it, and asked Him to forgive him. 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya informed us, from Ishaq ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi 'Amrah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a man committed a sin and said: Lord, I committed a sin — or did a sin — so forgive it. Blessed and Exalted said: My servant committed a sin and knew he has a Lord who forgives sin and seizes for it; I have forgiven My servant. Then he did another sin — or said: committed another sin — and said: Lord, I did a sin, so forgive it for me. Blessed and Exalted said: My servant knew he has a Lord who forgives sin and seizes for it; I have forgiven My servant. Then he did another sin — or committed another sin — and said: Lord, I did a sin, so forgive it for me. Blessed and Exalted said: My servant knew he has a Lord who forgives sin and seizes for it; I have forgiven My servant. Then he did another sin — or said: committed another sin — and said: Lord, I did a sin, so forgive it. He said: My servant knew he has a Lord who forgives sin and seizes for it; I call you to witness that I have forgiven My servant, so let him do what he wills. We examined this hadith, and the best we arrived at of what it allows — and Allah knows best — is that the servant, by his sins being a sin, and that Allah Almighty knew it from him, and that He seizes him with punishment for it if He wills and pardons him if He wills, is faith from him in Him. And it is understood that when he fears His punishment, Majestic and Mighty, for that sin and hopes for His forgiveness for it, he is among those gladdened by his good deed and grieved by his evil deed, so he enters thereby into the meaning in the hadith we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, and deserves faith thereby. And his knowing that Allah Almighty knew what was from him is contrary to others who think Allah Almighty is unaware of what is from them — deserving disbelief thereby — among those Allah Almighty mentioned in His Book by His saying: ""You did not hide lest your hearing, your sight and your skins testify against you, but you thought Allah does not know much of what you do."" Then He followed that with His saying: ""And that thought of yours which you thought of your Lord ruined you, so you became among the losers."" So the man we mentioned in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which we reported at the head of this chapter is the opposite of one of that description. Whoever enters into this verse is a disbeliever, deserving the Fire; whoever enters into that hadith is a believer, deserving from his Lord Almighty by His bounty upon him what He mentioned by His bounty to him in that hadith. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4258","Chapter 584: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: ""When the slave girl fornicates and is not fortified, flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then if she fornicates, sell her even for a rope."" Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, from Abu Hurayrah and Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the slave girl when she fornicates and is not fortified. He said: ""If she fornicates, flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then sell her even for a rope."" Malik said: Ibn Shihab said: I do not know whether after the third or the fourth. And al-dafir is the rope."
"4259","'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, from Zayd and Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the slave girl fornicating before being fortified. He said: ""If she fornicates, flog her; if she fornicates, flog her; if she fornicates, flog her."" And he said at the fourth or the third: ""If she fornicates, sell her, even for the rope."""
"4260","Al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, Abu Hurayrah, and Shibl, they said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man came to him and said: My slave girl fornicated. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Flog her; if she fornicates, flog her; if she fornicates, flog her; if she fornicates, sell her even for a rope."" And he did not mention in his hadith ""and not fortified."""
"4261","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Harrani informed us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shihab, that 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah informed him that Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) and Zayd ibn Khalid (may Allah be pleased with him) informed him that they heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about the slave girl fornicating, not fortified. He said: ""Flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then sell her even for a rope, after the third or the fourth."""
"4262","And Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Naysaburi informed us, he said: Ayyub — meaning Ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal — narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr — meaning Ibn Abi Uways — narrated to me, from Sulayman — meaning Ibn Bilal — he said: Yahya — meaning Ibn Sa'id — said, and Ibn Shihab informed me that 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to him that Abu Hurayrah and Zayd ibn Khalid (may Allah be pleased with them) narrated to him that they heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about the slave girl when she fornicates, not fortified. He said: ""Flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then sell her even for a rope, after the third or the fourth."" And al-dafir is the rope. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his command on the slave girl when she fornicates, not fortified, as we mentioned from him. A speaker said: In this hadith is evidence that when she fornicates, not fortified, that flogging is not obligatory upon her, for the flogging mentioned in this hadith is only mentioned for her fornication before fortification — and in that is evidence that her ruling after fortification differs; otherwise mentioning fortification would have no meaning. And it is reported on that from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas:"
"4263","As 'Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi 'Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: There is no cutting upon slaves nor upon the people of the land at all — meaning the people of the dhimmah. Thus Ibn 'Uyaynah reported it from 'Amr as the saying of Ibn 'Abbas, while Musa ibn Dawud reported it, from al-Thawri, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4264","As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""There is no cutting upon the runaway slave when he steals, nor upon the dhimmi."" This speaker said: The Book of Allah Almighty indicates that, mentioning His Almighty saying: ""And fortified women except what your right hands possess"" — namely captive women — then He said: ""When they are fortified, if they commit indecency, upon them is half of what is upon fortified women of punishment."" This word is among what the readers differed in: some read it with fath — among them 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud."
"4265","As Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar narrated to us, he said: al-Khaffaf narrated to us, from Sa'id — namely Ibn Abi 'Arubah — from Abu Ma'shar, from al-Nakha'i, that Ma'qil ibn Muqarrin asked Ibn Mas'ud, saying: My slave girl fornicated. He said: Flog her fifty. He said: She is not fortified. He said: Is she not a Muslim? He said: Yes. He said: Her Islam is her fortification."
"4266","And Ahmad ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: Khalaf narrated to us, he said: al-Khaffaf narrated to us, from Aban al-'Attar, from 'Asim, from Zirr: ""When they are fortified"" — he says: when they embrace Islam; and he did not mention Ibn Mas'ud."
"4267","And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, from Ma'qil ibn Muqarrin, from Ibn Mas'ud, then he mentioned the hadith before this hadith, and said at the fourth: sell her. Khalaf said: Likewise al-A'mash, 'Asim and Hamzah read it, and some read it with damm: ""when they are fortified"" — among those who read it thus: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas."
"4268","As Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Khalaf narrated to us, he said: al-Khaffaf narrated to us, from Harun al-A'war, from Aban ibn Taghlib, from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas: ""when they are fortified"" — meaning by marriage. And among those who read it thus: Nafi' and Abu 'Amr ibn al-'Ala'. This speaker said: In that is evidence that the slave girl, when she fornicates or does what obligates a hadd upon others — theft and the like — before the fortification in this verse, no punishment for that fornication or otherwise is obligatory upon her. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: In the hadith we reported at the head of this chapter, from Abu Hurayrah and Zayd, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the slave girl when she fornicates, not fortified, and he commanded flogging her — and in his commanding her flogging is evidence of the obligation of punishment for fornication upon her; otherwise he would not have commanded flogging her. This speaker said: As for his commanding her flogging, it is as you mentioned — upon discipline, not the hadd; the proof is that he did not mention a hadd in it, but only mentioned flogging. He said: This hadith is also reported from other than Abu Hurayrah and other than Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani likewise, without mention of a hadd in it."
"4269","And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, that Shibl ibn Hamid al-Muzani informed him that 'Abd Allah ibn Malik al-Uwaysi informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of the slave girl: ""If she fornicates, flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then if she fornicates, flog her; then if she fornicates, sell her even for a rope"" — and al-dafir is the rope — at the third or the fourth. And Zayd ibn Khalid, the Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him the like. Thus Yunus told us, from Ibn Wahb in the hadith: Shibl ibn Hamid — while it is Ibn Khulayd; that 'Abd Allah ibn Malik al-Uwaysi — while it is al-Awsi."
"4270","And likewise Fahd narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah, that Shibl ibn Khulayd al-Muzani informed him that 'Abd Allah ibn Malik al-Awsi informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like exactly."
"4271","And likewise Ibn Abi Dawud narrated it to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: 'Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, then he mentioned with his chain the like exactly. This speaker said: What is in these reports of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding the flogging of the slave girl when she fornicates is only discipline, not a hadd. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: We found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reported with a fixed measure of flogging in it — and that is only in the hadd, for disciplines are upon the measures of offences, and offences differ, so disciplines differ in them. Intending a measure of flogging is proof that the hadd, not discipline, was meant. And what is reported containing mention of the measure in flogging:"
"4272","As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed me, he said: Abu al-Jawwab informed me, he said: 'Ammar ibn Ruzayq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: My slave girl fornicated, and her fornication became clear. He said: ""Flog her fifty."" Then he came to him saying: She returned, and her fornication became clear. He said: ""Flog her fifty."" Then he came to him saying: She returned, and her fornication became clear. He said: ""Sell her, even for a rope of hair."""
"4273","And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim — meaning Ibn Warah — narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Musa — namely Ibn A'yan — narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a man came to him saying: My slave girl fornicated. He said: ""Flog her fifty."" He said: If she returns? He said: ""Then return."" He said: If she returns? He said: ""Then sell her even for a rope, at the third or the fourth"" — and al-dafir is the rope. Abu Ja'far said: The fixing in this hadith indicates that that flogging is a hadd, not otherwise. And we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on it what is clearer than this."
"4274","As al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi, Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us — thus Rabi' said; as for Muhammad, he said: Shu'ayb informed us; as for Bahr, he said: it was read to Shu'ayb — then they all agreed, saying: from al-Layth, he informed him, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that he heard him saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""When the slave girl of one of you fornicates, let him flog her the hadd and not rebuke her."" He said that three times, then said at the third or the fourth: ""Then let him sell her even for a rope."""
"4275","And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — and he did not mention Abu Sa'id al-Maqburi."
"4276","And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me, from Makhul, from 'Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"4277","And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id and Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid informed us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When the slave girl of one of you fornicates and her fornication becomes clear, let him flog her the hadd and not rebuke"" — three times. Qutaybah added: ""And if she fornicates, sell her even for a rope."" And the wording is Muhammad's."
"4278","And as Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like. Sufyan said: By his saying ""rebuke"" he means: reproach. Abu Ja'far said: The proof stood for us against our opponent on the flogging we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports — that it is the hadd, not discipline. And in that is evidence that the hadd is upon the slave girl in her fornication even without the intended fortification in the verse mentioned about it. And this meaning was strengthened:"
"4279","As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-'Abdi and Musa ibn Mas'ud — meaning Abu Hudhayfah — narrated to us, they said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from 'Abd al-A'la al-Tha'labi, from Maysarah Abu Jamilah al-Tuhawi, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A slave girl of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fornicated, and he commanded me to carry out the hadd upon her, but she had not dried of her blood nor become pure. I said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): She has not dried of her blood nor become pure. He said: ""When she is pure, carry out the hadd upon her."" And he said: ""Carry out the hudud upon what your right hands possess."" The speaker we mentioned said: It is also possible that that slave girl had been fortified before that, either by marriage or by Islam. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: It may allow what he mentioned, except that what is in this hadith of his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""Carry out the hudud upon what your right hands possess"" — without mention of fortification — is proof that the hudud are obligatory upon what our right hands possess without stipulating fortification or anything else in them. A speaker said: What is the meaning of his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying ""not fortified"" in the hadiths you reported? Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: It may be that what was revealed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he said that saying on the punishments of slave girls when they fornicate was upon their ruling when not fortified before that. And it was understood that the punishment of the fortified in fornication is harsher than the unfortified therein, for the unfortified freeman is flogged therein, while the fortified among them is stoned — and stoning is harsher than flogging. So the ruling from Allah Almighty which He informed His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of, until the mentioned answer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports on the punishment of the slave girl when she fornicates, was on fornication from her before fortification. Then Allah Almighty clarified that her ruling after fortification is as her ruling before fortification in that — out of lightening from Him and mercy — saying: ""When they are fortified, if they commit indecency, upon them is half of what is upon fortified women of punishment"" — meaning fortified free women. And that stipulation from Him Almighty before that is like His stipulation in His saying: ""And when you travel in the land, there is no blame upon you to shorten the prayer, if you fear those who disbelieve may afflict you."" That was upon lifting blame and permitting shortening when fearing the affliction of those who disbelieve; then Allah Almighty bestowed upon His servants what He mentioned in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer to 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) when he asked him about it, saying to him: ""A charity Allah has bestowed upon you, so accept His charity."""
"4280","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us — (h) — and as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, they said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi 'Ammar narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Babah, from Ya'la ibn Umayyah, he said: I said to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): Allah Almighty only said: ""that you shorten the prayer, if you fear."" He said: I marvelled at what you marvelled at, so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: ""A charity Allah has bestowed upon you, so accept it."" Abu Ja'far said: That is, He Almighty carried out for you what He bestowed upon you when you fear those who disbelieve may afflict you, of shortening the prayer, even if you are safe from their affliction. Likewise what He Almighty informed His Messenger of on the hadd of slave girls in fornication before fortification of what He informed him — then the awaited in their hadd therein after fortification was what is harsher than that — so He Almighty bestowed upon them and had mercy, making it after fortification as it is before fortification, with no increase upon them therein nor harshness. A speaker said: It may be that when He Almighty returned them to half of what is upon fortified women, and what is upon fortified women therein is stoning — and stoning has no half — that all of what is obligatory upon the fortified woman is obligatory upon them, as He Almighty said: ""The thief, male and female, cut their hands."" Then He said of slaves: ""When they are fortified, if they commit indecency, upon them is half of what is upon fortified women of punishment."" And cutting, having no attainable half, was obligatory in its entirety upon slaves — likewise stoning, having no attainable half, is obligatory in its entirety upon slaves. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: Consensus bars this, for there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge on the married Muslim slave girl who fornicates — that there is no stoning upon her. And in their consensus is evidence that Allah Almighty did not mean by slaves therein half of the stoning which has no half, but meant half of the flogging which has a known half, upon what is in the reports we reported. And in what we mentioned is established the equality of the ruling of slaves in punishments for indecencies before fortification and after fortification. And in what we mentioned from Ibn 'Abbas — that there is no hadd upon the people of the land in theft — by his interpreting Allah Almighty's saying on slave girls: ""When they are fortified, if they commit indecency, upon them is half of what is upon fortified women of punishment"" as that hudud are only obligatory upon those fortified, not others — that is refuted by the hadith of 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) which we reported in this chapter, and what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in stoning the two Jews when they fornicated, which we shall mention in its place later in this book, Allah Almighty willing. And we ask Allah for success."
"4281","Chapter 585: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the khutbah for 'Id — must people sit for it and listen as is obligatory in the khutbah for Jumu'ah, or not? Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa al-Sinani narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: I attended the 'Id with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); when he prayed, he said: ""We shall give a khutbah; whoever loves to sit for the khutbah, let him sit, and whoever loves to return, let him return."" Abu Ja'far said: We understood from the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting whoever of those praying with him wishes to leave before his khutbah after it that the khutbah for 'Id is not like the khutbah for Jumu'ah in sitting for it, listening to it, and abandoning idle talk therein until it ends; that is permissible in the 'Id khutbah and forbidden in the Jumu'ah khutbah. That in our view — and Allah knows best — is because the Jumu'ah khutbah is an admonition, and people must listen to admonition, as He Almighty said: ""Call to the way of your Lord with wisdom and good admonition."" When he is commanded to admonish them, they are commanded to listen to it and attend to it until it takes the effect Allah Almighty intended thereby from them. And thereby — and Allah knows best — the prayer after it, namely Jumu'ah, was made bound to it, so it suffices only after it precedes it. The 'Id khutbah is not so, for it is not an admonition they are admonished with, obliging them to listen and be silent; rather it is teaching them what is addressed to them therein — among that what they learn therein on the day of Fitr of the sadaqat al-fitr: the kinds it is from, the measure of each kind, the time they pay it in, and whom among people they give it to. And among that on the day of sacrifice: his commanding them to sacrifice — what they sacrifice therein, the kinds they sacrifice from, what they use therein of sacrificial slaughter, the times they do it in, what blemished animals are unfit, and what those blemishes barring it are. And that is what many people dispense with for their knowledge of it, and one ignorant of it takes it from other than the one addressing them — so it differs from the Jumu'ah khutbah for these meanings by which they differ. And the 'Id khutbah was made like the Hajj khutbah in which the imam teaches people what they do in their Hajj and what they avoid therein — and there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge on latitude for people in staying away from it and abandoning listening to it. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4282","Chapter 586: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on entering places whose people Allah Almighty was angry with — of prohibition and of permission. Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud, author of al-Tayalisah, narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Awsat al-Bajali narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Kabshah al-Anmari of Anmar Ghatafan, from his father, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the raid of Tabuk; people hastened to the people of al-Hijr, entering upon them. It was called among people: prayer in congregation. We reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) holding his camel, and he said: ""Why do you enter upon a people Allah Almighty is angry with?!"" A man called to him: We marvel at them, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Shall I inform you of something more marvellous: a man of yourselves informing you of what was before you and what will be after you — so be upright and correct, for Allah Almighty does not care at all for your punishment. Then will come a people who do not defend anything from themselves."" Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) uncovering to people the meaning for which they entered upon the people Allah Almighty was angry with, and some saying to him that it was from them out of marvel, and the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to them thereupon what is in this hadith. In that is evidence that he did not praise their entering upon them for that. So it is possible their entering upon them is in all cases not permitted to them, and possible it is not permitted to them for marvelling, but permitted for otherwise. So we considered that."
"4283","Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab — while mentioning al-Hijr, dwellings of Thamud — he said: Salim ibn 'Abd Allah said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar said: We passed with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over al-Hijr, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: ""Do not enter the dwellings of those who wronged except weeping, lest what befell them befall you."" Then he urged on and hastened until he left it behind."
"4284","Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the companions of al-Hijr: ""Do not enter upon these punished ones except weeping; if you are not weeping, do not enter upon them, lest what befell them befall you."""
"4285","Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"4286","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting people to enter upon them weeping — for in that is a lesson for them, and caution against opposing Allah Almighty's command, lest what befell them befall them at that. It became clear by what we mentioned, by Allah Almighty's praise and blessing, that what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in each of these two kinds in these reports is other than what is in the other kind, and that each is not contradictory to the other. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4287","Chapter 587: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the valley they passed in the raid of Tabuk — that it is a cursed valley. Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sa'igh and Fahd ibn Sulayman together narrated to us, they said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan said to me: Ask 'Abd Allah ibn Qudamah ibn Sakhr al-'Uqayli about this hadith. He said: I met him at the door of the governorate and mentioned it to him, and he said: Abu Dharr claimed that they were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the raid of Tabuk, and they came upon a valley. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: ""O people, you are in a cursed valley."" So he rode his horse and pressed on, and the people pressed on. Then he said: ""Whoever kneaded dough, let him feed it to his camel; and whoever cooked a pot, let him overturn it."""
"4288","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Muhammad and Fahd, from Muslim, from Hammad, with its chain and text."
"4289","Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hasan saying: 'Abd Allah ibn Qudamah al-Sa'di narrated to me — he said: and al-Sa'di was a truthful man — that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon the dwellings of Thamud and said: ""Go out, go out, for it is a cursed valley"" — fearing you do not go out until such-and-such befalls you."
"4290","'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like. And it is reported from Sabrah al-Juhani, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on his commanding people on what they kneaded with the water of that valley, the like of what is reported from him in the hadith of Abu Dharr which we reported."
"4291","As Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Harmalah ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted at al-Hijr, he said to his companions: ""Whoever made food of this water, let him cast it away."" Among them were those who kneaded dough and those who prepared hays, and they cast it away."
"4292","And as Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah al-Juhani narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrating from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he alighted at al-Hijr, said to those with him: ""Whoever of you kneaded dough or prepared hays of this water, let him cast it away."""
"4293","And as Abu Muhammad Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Ma'bad ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sabrah ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah narrated to me, he said: My uncle Harmalah ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Rabi' ibn Sabrah narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted at al-Hijr, he said to his companions: ""Whoever made food of this water, let him cast it away."" Among them were those who kneaded dough and those who prepared hays, and they cast it away. Abu Ja'far said: That in our view — and Allah knows best — may be that when Allah Almighty was angry with the people of that valley, among His punishing them was making their water what harms them and harms their likes among the accountable — as punishment for the things for which He was angry with the people of that valley, and fear for others lest it be punishment for their past sins, for they are all sinners, though their sins differ and punishments differ. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them on what they kneaded with that water not to eat it, and permitted them to feed their camels which bear no accountability nor sins. Then we considered his hastening in that valley until he passed it: that in our view — and Allah knows best — was for them to follow him, hastening as he hastens until they leave that valley, fearing for them lest they be seized there for their sins as those before them of that valley were seized there for their sins. Then we considered the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) describing that valley with curse: that in our view — and Allah knows best — was intending thereby the people of the valley from whom was what He Almighty was angry with them for, so he cursed them for that. And he mentioned the valley with that curse while intending its people, not it — as He Almighty said: ""And Allah struck the example of a town, safe and secure, its provision coming to it abundantly from every place, then it disbelieved in Allah's blessings, so Allah made it taste the garment of hunger and fear for what they did"" — for the town did nothing; rather its people did what they were destroyed for. Then He followed it with what indicates His intending them thereby, not their town, by His Almighty saying: ""And there came to them a messenger from them, but they denied him"" — meaning thereby His messenger to them (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And as He Almighty said, quoting its sayers: ""And ask the town we were in, and the caravan we came in"" — meaning the people of the town and the people of the caravan. Likewise his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying of that valley ""this is a cursed valley"" is upon its people, not the valley itself. And Allah knows best, and Him we ask for success."
"4294","Chapter 588: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on what was from him at the grave of Abu Righal, and his informing people that he is of Thamud, and that the sanctuary barred from him what befell the rest of Thamud besides him until he left it and vengeance overtook him and he perished. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Bujayr ibn Abi Bujayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr, that he heard him saying: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and they passed by the grave of Abu Righal. He said: ""This is the grave of Abu Righal, father of Thaqif. He was a man of Thamud, and his dwelling was in the sanctuary. When Allah Almighty destroyed his people with what He destroyed them with, his place in the sanctuary barred him. He left until when he reached here, he died, and was buried with a branch of gold, so we hastened and extracted it."" Abu Ja'far said: After hearing this hadith from Ibn Abi Dawud, I looked in my book and did not find mention of Isma'il ibn Umayyah in it, so something entered my heart from it. I mentioned it to Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb al-Nasa'i, and he said to me: It is as you memorized. I said to him: From whom did you take it? He said: From Abu Hafs — meaning 'Amr ibn 'Ali — from al-Riyahi. I said to him: 'Amr ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab? He said: Yes, from Yazid."
"4295","'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Zubalah al-Madini Abu al-Hasan and Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrating from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Bujayr ibn Abi Bujayr, he said: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, when we went out to al-Ta'if and passed by a grave: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""This is the grave of Abu Righal, father of Thaqif. He was of Thamud, and this sanctuary defended him. When he left, vengeance befell him in this place, and he was buried in it. The sign of that is that he was buried with a branch of gold; if you dig it up, you will find it with him."" So the people hastened and extracted the branch with him. Abu Ja'far said: We examined this hadith and found in it the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing people that Abu Righal was of Thamud, and that Allah Almighty's sanctuary barred from him the vengeance that befell others of Thamud. And we understood that the dwellings of Thamud were not in the sanctuary, but in what is besides it of what is mentioned in the two chapters we mentioned before this chapter. And it is possible he took refuge in the sanctuary and entered it, so it barred him from what befell others of Thamud. A speaker said: In the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud among the two hadiths we reported in this chapter, his dwelling was in the sanctuary. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: It is possible his dwelling was in the sanctuary, and he was with Thamud in the places they were in upon their disobedience of Allah Almighty and departure from His command. When Allah Almighty's threat came to them and he feared it would reach him in the place he was in, he took refuge in his dwelling in the sanctuary and entered the sanctuary for that, so it barred him. And it is reported from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on the story of Abu Righal also what agrees with what is in the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud of what we mentioned."
"4296","As Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by al-Hijr and said: ""Do not ask for signs, for the people of Salih asked, and it came from this pass and went out from this pass — meaning the she-camel. They defied their Lord's command and hamstrung her. She drank their water one day and they drank her milk one day. Then a thunderbolt seized them, stilling all under heaven's surface among them except one man in Allah's sanctuary. When he left, what befell his people befell him."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, who is he? He said: ""Abu Righal, and he was buried here."""
"4297","And as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Khuthaym narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with its meaning, except that he said: except a man in Allah's sanctuary, whom Allah Almighty's sanctuary barred from Allah's punishment."
"4298","And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi 'Abbad narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Uthman, from Ibn Sabit, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said while in al-Hijr: ""These are the people of Salih; Allah Almighty destroyed them except a man in Allah Almighty's sanctuary whom Allah barred from Allah's punishment."" It was said: O Messenger of Allah, who is he? He said: ""Abu Righal."" Abu Ja'far said: If the sanctuary barred in the pre-Islamic age from punishments entailing loss of lives, in Islam it bars the like even more. And that is strengthened by what is reported from Ibn 'Abbas and Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) on one who incurs a hadd outside the sanctuary then takes refuge in the sanctuary."
"4299","As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he said: Whoever incurs a hadd in the sanctuary, it is carried out upon him; if he incurs it outside the sanctuary then enters the sanctuary, he is not spoken to, nor sat with, nor traded with, until he leaves the sanctuary, then the hadd is carried out upon him."
"4300","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, the like."
"4301","And as Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Abbas, the like."
"4302","And as Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Malik informed us, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, on one who commits an offence outside the sanctuary then takes refuge in the sanctuary: he is not spoken to, nor traded with, nor harmed, until he leaves the sanctuary; when he leaves the sanctuary, he is seized and what is upon him carried out. And what he commits in the sanctuary is carried out upon him of whatever he commits therein."
"4303","As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: 'Ata' narrated to me that Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas said on Allah Almighty's saying: ""And whoever enters it is safe"": A man incurs the hadd then enters it — he is not traded with, nor sat with, nor sheltered, nor spoken to, until he leaves it; then he is followed, seized, and the hadd carried out upon him. He said: And 'Ata' said to me: If he slanders in it, or steals, the hadd is carried out upon him; if he does that outside it then takes refuge — meaning in it — it is not carried out upon him."
"4304","And as Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to me, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj informed us, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: Had I found the killer of 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) in the sanctuary, I would not disturb him. If a speaker says: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr opposed them in that, and this was from him:"
"4305","He mentioned what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik, from 'Ata', he said: Sa'id, freedman of Mu'awiyah, and companions of his in al-Ta'if fortified themselves in a fortress, and were brought down from it and taken to 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr in Makkah. He sent to 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, saying: What is your view on these men? He said: I see you free them, for they are safe since you brought them into the sanctuary. He said: No, we take them out of the sanctuary, then kill them. He said: Why not before you brought them in? So Ibn al-Zubayr took them out and crucified them. Ibn 'Abbas said: Had I met my father's killer in the sanctuary, I would not disturb him until he leaves it. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: Ibn al-Zubayr did not thereby oppose Ibn 'Abbas that the sanctuary had sheltered the people brought into it from the punishment upon them; rather he did not bar taking them out of it to carry out upon them outside it. So in his view that they are not punished while in it, he agreed with Ibn 'Abbas; in his saying they are taken out of it to elsewhere, he opposed him. And what Ibn 'Abbas said is worthier in our view, for the verse requires it, namely Allah Almighty's saying: ""And whoever enters it is safe."" And those men had entered it, so were safe by entering it. And it is possible Ibn al-Zubayr did not make their return to the sanctuary safety for them, because their entering it was not by their choice — rather by others' act upon them — for their entering it by choice seeking safety therein from what they feared, while others' bringing them into it contains no seeking from them of safety therein from what they feared, so that entering did not secure them from what they feared. Thus the meaning of the disagreement returns to what is undisputed, by what was from Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas. A speaker said: His Almighty saying ""And whoever enters it is safe"" is only upon game, not otherwise. Our answer to him: This saying of his is severe ignorance of language, for had the matter been as he mentioned, it would be ""and what enters it is safe,"" for ""man"" (whoever) is only for the children of Adam, and for others in its place is ""ma"" (what), as He Almighty said: ""And what the beast eats except what you slaughter, and what is sacrificed on altars"" — with like examples in the Qur'an too long to mention. And ""man"" is used for the children of Adam, as His Almighty saying: ""And whoever disbelieves, I shall grant him enjoyment a little, then compel him to the punishment of the Fire."" And as His saying: ""And whoever does that meets sin."" And as His saying: ""Whoever of you commits a clear indecency."" And many likes — except that ""ma"" may come for the children of Adam in place of ""man,"" among that His Almighty saying: ""And fortified women except what your right hands possess."" And among that His Exalted saying: ""And the parent and what he begot"" — in the meaning: and whom he begot. So ""ma"" may be used for the children of Adam in place of ""man,"" though rarely; but they never use for other than the children of Adam ""man"" in place of ""ma"" in any case. Since ""man"" is for the children of Adam, not others, His Almighty saying ""And whoever enters it is safe"" is upon the children of Adam, not others. And this saying we mentioned from Ibn 'Abbas and Ibn 'Umar was said after them by Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, Muhammad and Zufar."
"4306","As Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub informed us, from Abu Hanifah, with that, and he did not relate any disagreement in it."
"4307","And Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Ziyad, from Zufar, with the like. He said: And Abu Yusuf said: The sanctuary does not shelter a wrongdoer. And the saying in our view on that was what Abu Hanifah, Zufar and Muhammad said — with which Abu Yusuf agreed with them in Muhammad's narration — by what preceded them therein of what we mentioned from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, and what 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr agreed with them in of what he agreed with them, and we know of no one among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disagreeing with them. And the Qur'an came down in their language, and they are the knowers of what they were addressed with therein. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4308","Chapter 589: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying on the crescent: ""If it is obscured for you, then estimate for it."" Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, then estimate for it."""
"4309","Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to me that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the like."
"4310","Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Nafi', from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned Ramadan, saying: ""Do not fast until you see the crescent, nor break fast until you see it; if it is obscured for you, then estimate for it."""
"4311","And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The month is twenty-nine; do not fast until you see the crescent, nor break fast until you see it; if it is obscured for you, then estimate for it."" Abu Ja'far said: Thus Yunus informed us this hadith."
"4312","And al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The month is twenty-nine; do not fast until you see the crescent, nor break fast until you see it; if it is obscured for you, complete the number thirty."" So Ibn Wahb and al-Shafi'i differed over Malik in this hadith; each reported it from him as we mentioned of his reporting it from him. So we sought it from others' reporting it from him — how is it?"
"4313","We found Yazid ibn Sinan narrating to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""The month is twenty-nine nights; do not fast until you see the crescent, nor break fast until you see it; if it is obscured for you, then estimate for it."" So what al-Qa'nabi reported from Malik agreed with what Ibn Wahb reported from him, and differed from what al-Shafi'i reported from him. Two are worthier of preservation than one — especially as what the two reported from Malik agrees with what Salim and Nafi' reported from Ibn 'Umar. Abu Ja'far said: We examined his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying ""then estimate for it"" — what is his intent thereby? The best we heard on that — and Allah knows best — is that Allah Almighty said in His Book: ""And the moon, We measured it stations until it returns like the old palm-stalk."" So He Almighty informed that He measured it stations it runs upon. That is that He Almighty runs it making what runs each night until it sets one station — six-sevenths of an hour — for the stations of the night are fourteen stations, and its hours fourteen hours, so each station is six-sevenths of an hour. It runs thus until twenty-eight nights, concealed; if the month is thirty, it is concealed two nights, and if twenty-nine, concealed one night. So what is commanded in this hadith of Ibn 'Umar, when it is obscured for us, then rises: we look at its setting — if for one station, we know it is of its night; if for two stations, we know it is of two nights. We understand thereby that between them is a day, and that upon us is making up that day if of Ramadan. And this consideration is what people are not equal in; only few of those who learn it, and it is hidden from most. Then that was returned to what they are all equal in, none preceding another in its knowledge, by what is reported from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) abrogating that, namely his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: ""If it is obscured for you, count thirty."""
"4314","As al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar — he heard Muhammad ibn Jubayr or Ibn Hunayn — Abu Ja'far said: and the correct is Ibn Hunayn — saying: He heard Ibn 'Abbas marvelling at one who anticipates the month, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you see it, fast; and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, complete the number thirty."""
"4315","And as 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, that Muhammad ibn Hunayn informed him that he heard Ibn 'Abbas saying, then he mentioned the like."
"4316","And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: 'Amr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Hunayn, from Ibn 'Abbas, then he mentioned the like."
"4317","And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us — (h). And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, then they agreed, saying: from Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: We saw the crescent of Ramadan, so we sent a man to Ibn 'Abbas and asked him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Allah Almighty has extended it for its sighting; if it is obscured for you, complete the number."""
"4318","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi informed us — (h). And as al-Hasan ibn Bakr al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah informed us, then they agreed, saying: Hatim ibn Abi Saghirhah informed us, from Simak ibn Harb, from 'Ikrimah, he said: I heard Ibn 'Abbas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Fast by its sighting, and break fast by its sighting; if cloud, darkness or haze comes between you and it, complete the number."""
"4319","And as al-Hasan ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur and 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq informed us, they said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid informed us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""Do not anticipate the month until you see the crescent, or complete the number before it; then fast until you see the crescent, or complete the number."""
"4320","And as Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"4321","And as Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from some Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"4322","And as al-Hasan ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, fast thirty."""
"4323","And as Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: 'Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"4324","And as al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"4325","And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"4326","'Ali ibn Ma'bad and al-Hasan ibn Bakr narrated to us — 'Ali said: Rawh narrated to us, and al-Hasan said: Rawh informed us — he said: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed us: he heard Jabir saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, count thirty days"" — except that 'Ali did not say ""days."""
"4327","Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Humayd al-Ru'asi narrated to us, from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Adi ibn Hatim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: ""When Ramadan comes, fast thirty, unless you see the crescent before that."""
"4328","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, he said: I heard a man saying: O Messenger of Allah, what of the day disputed over — one group says of Sha'ban, another says of Ramadan? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, count thirty."" These reports abrogate the first reports, and people proceeded upon them. And what confirms what we reported from Ibn 'Umar, of the hadith of 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, that it is as 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb and al-Qa'nabi reported it from Malik — as we mentioned from them from him — is Isma'il ibn Ja'far's agreement with them, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, on that."
"4329","As Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, that he heard Ibn 'Umar saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, then estimate for it."" And we also found, from the hadith of Ayyub, from Nafi', upon this meaning."
"4330","Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like. And we found it from Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi's narration, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, likewise."
"4331","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like. Abu Ja'far said: In what we mentioned is evidence that the reality of what Ibn 'Umar narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter — with no disagreement upon him therein — is as Salim and Nafi' reported it from him, and as those who reported it from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar reported it agreeing therewith, not opposing it. And we ask Allah for success."
"4332","Chapter 590: Explanation of the difficulty in what al-Nu'man ibn Bashir al-Ansari reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the time he prayed 'Isha at night — which time is it? Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Abu Bishr, from Bashir ibn Thabit, from Habib ibn Salim, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: I am the most knowledgeable of people of the time of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) 'Isha prayer; he prayed it by the measure of the moon's setting on the fourth night. Yazid said: I said to Shu'bah: Hushaym narrated to us of the third night. He said: Likewise? I said: Yes. He said: Or the third night."
"4333","And Abu al-Darda' Hashim ibn Muhammad al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr narrated to us, from Habib ibn Salim — and he did not mention Bashir ibn Thabit — from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I am the most knowledgeable of people of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) time for 'Isha; he prayed it by the measure of the setting of the third night of the month."
"4334","And 'Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr informed us, from Habib ibn Salim, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: I am the most knowledgeable of people — or as the most knowledgeable of people — of the time of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer for the last 'Isha, then he mentioned the like. So we examined the reality of this hadith's chain: is it as Shu'bah reported it, or as Hushaym reported it?"
"4335","We found Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi narrating to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Bashir ibn Thabit al-Ansari, from Habib ibn Salim, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: By Allah, I am the most knowledgeable of people of the time of this prayer, the last 'Isha prayer; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed it at the moon's setting on the third night."
"4336","We found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrating to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like."
"4337","And we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrating to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Qudamah informed me, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Raqabah, from Ja'far ibn Iyas, from Habib ibn Salim, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, then he mentioned the like. So Raqabah agreed with Hushaym in omitting Bashir ibn Thabit in this hadith's chain, while Abu 'Awanah agreed with Shu'bah in inserting him in its chain. All these reports agreed that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the last 'Isha prayer delaying it, for its time enters before that time in which he prayed it. And what we mentioned of that is indicated by:"
"4338","As Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the last 'Isha. Abu Ja'far said: That — and Allah knows best — was his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking the excellent time of its time, as he prayed other prayers in their best times. Among that: he prayed Zuhr in winter days hastening it, and in summer days delaying it; and Maghrib throughout hastening it — these two prayers agreed upon in the two hours in which he prayed them of their times. As for Fajr and 'Asr, they differ in the two hours in which he prayed them of their times, so he did not cite the two hours in which he prayed them. Likewise the hour in which he prayed the last 'Isha — that was because it is the excellent hour of its time, and Allah knows best. Then we considered the hour in which he prayed it — which of the night's hours it is. We found his praying it at the moon's setting on the third to be upon the setting of three stations of the night's stations — that of its hours is two and a half hours and half a seventh of an hour. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4339","Chapter 591: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the causes of love and the causes of hatred in people's hearts. Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-'Ala' ibn al-Musayyib, that Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to him, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""When Allah Almighty loves a servant, He says to Jibril: I love So-and-so, so love him. Jibril loves him, and says to the people of heaven: Allah Almighty loves So-and-so, so love him. The people of heaven love him, and acceptance is placed for him."" Al-'Ala' said: I said: What is acceptance? He said: Affection from people."
"4340","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: ""When Allah Almighty loves the servant, He says to Jibril: I have loved So-and-so, so love him. Jibril loves him, then calls in heaven: Allah has loved So-and-so, so love him. The people of heaven love him, then acceptance is placed for him on earth."" Malik said: And I deem it only that he said the like on hatred."
"4341","And 'Ali ibn Ma'bad and 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, he said: We were at 'Arafah when 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz passed, in charge of the season, and people stood looking at him. I said to my father: Father, I deem Allah Almighty loves 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz. He said: Why? I said: For his love in people's hearts. He said: By your father, my son — I heard Abu Hurayrah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""When Allah Almighty loves a servant, He says: O Jibril, I love So-and-so, so love him. Jibril calls in the heavens: Allah Almighty loves So-and-so, so love him. His love is cast upon the people of earth, so they love him. And when He hates a servant, He says: O Jibril, I hate So-and-so, so hate him. Hatred for him is placed on earth."""
"4342","Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih — meaning from his father — from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""When Allah Almighty loves a servant, He calls Jibril and says: O Jibril, I love So-and-so, so love him. Jibril loves him, and calls in heaven: Allah Almighty loves So-and-so, so love him. The people of heaven love him, and acceptance is placed for him on earth. And when He hates a servant, the like."""
"4343","And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like. Abu Ja'far said: All these reports are reported from Suhayl, from his father. Rawh opposed their narrators therein, inserting between Suhayl and his father al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim."
"4344","As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from al-Qa'qa', from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like. Ibn Abi Dawud said to us: Thus Rawh says, from Suhayl, from al-Qa'qa', from Abu Salih — and none says this but him. A speaker said: These reports indicate that the love and hatred occurring in people's hearts involve no acquisition from them — they are in their hearts without their choice, by what they cannot repel — like what their souls whisper to them which they cannot expel. That merits them no praise for its praiseworthy nor blame for its blameworthy. Yet you reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts that."
"4345","He mentioned what the two Rabi's narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi and al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Azdi, they said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: ""A man visited a brother of his in another town, so Allah Almighty stationed an angel on his path. When he came upon him, he said to him: Where do you intend? He said: I visit a brother of mine in this town. He said: Has he a favour upon you that you tend? He said: No, I loved him for Allah Almighty. He said: I am Allah's messenger to you — Allah has loved you as you loved him."""
"4346","And as al-Rabi' al-Muradi alone narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Hassan, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), the like."
"4347","And as Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Sulaym, he said: I heard 'Amr ibn Maymun, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""Whoever is gladdened to find the taste of faith, let him love a man loving him only for Allah Almighty."" He said: This is praised upon, and that is only by his acquiring it. What is in the first section of this chapter negates his having acquisition therein — two contradictory meanings. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: The matter is not as he thought, and there is no contradiction in anything the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, for what he said is revelation revealed, which Allah Almighty undertook therein. Rather the meaning of the first hadiths — and Allah knows best — is that the mentioned love from Allah Almighty for whom He loves among His servants is after what was from them for which He loved them, as He Almighty said in His Book: ""Say: If you love Allah, follow me; Allah will love you and forgive your sins."" So His loving them was by their following His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — in his life and after his death. When they follow him, they become His allies, so He casts love for them in His servants' hearts, and they love them by their choice — so He rewards them for that. Like what He casts in their hearts of faith, as He Almighty said: ""But Allah made faith beloved to you and adorned it in your hearts, and made disbelief, defiance and disobedience hateful to you; those are the rightly guided — bounty from Allah and blessing."" So He informed of His favour therein upon them of what He cast in their hearts until from them is what He praises them upon, rewarding and recompensing them. Likewise love for Allah Almighty's allies by His making them beloved to whom He makes them beloved among His servants — so they love them by their choice and by acquiring their love — so He rewards them for that. Likewise whom He hates among His servants for departing from His Messenger and defying His command — He hates him for that, for he became His enemy — so He casts hatred for him in the hearts of whom He wills among His servants, and they hate him by acquiring that — so they are rewarded for hating him. Thus it became clear, by Allah's praise and blessing, all we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter has no contradiction, nor opposition of parts. And we ask Allah Almighty for success."
"4348","Chapter 592: Explanation of the difficulty in what is reported from Abu Ma'mar, from Ibn Mas'ud, of what they said in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lifetime in the tashahhud in prayer: ""Peace upon you, O Prophet, and Allah's mercy and blessings"" — and that after the Prophet they said: ""Peace upon the Prophet."" Al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Kufi al-Hibri Abu 'Abd Allah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sayf ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: 'Abd Allah ibn Sakhbarah Abu Ma'mar narrated to me, he said: I heard Ibn Mas'ud saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught me the tashahhud, my hand between his hands, as he teaches a surah of the Qur'an: ""Greetings to Allah, and prayers, and good words. Peace upon you, O Prophet, and Allah's mercy and blessings. Peace upon us and upon Allah's righteous servants. I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness Muhammad is His servant and Messenger"" — while he was among us. When he passed away, we said: ""Peace upon the Prophet."" A speaker said: This hadith is rejected, for it requires tashahhud after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what people generally tashahhud contrary to, for they tashahhud saying in their tashahhud ""Peace upon you, O Prophet, and Allah's mercy and blessings"" after his death as they tashahhuded in his life. Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: We rejected of that the like of what he rejected. He said: Whence came this opposition to what people hold — from Abu Ma'mar, a man of lofty rank, accepted in narration, or from below him among this hadith's narrators? Our answer to him, by Allah Almighty's success and help: We uncovered that and found it from below him among this hadith's narrators."
"4349","As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-'Absi narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, from Mujahid, from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud — and he did not mention Abu Ma'mar in his hadith — he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us the tashahhud in prayer as he teaches us a surah of the Qur'an, then he mentioned the tashahhud in the first hadith. He said: When he passed away, they said: ""Peace upon the Prophet."" So what we mentioned indicates that this addition opposing what people hold was from below Abu Ma'mar. Abu Ja'far said: Among what repels this hadith from being acted upon, and requires holding to what people hold in their prayers of their tashahhud with which they tashahhud therein:"
"4350","That Abu 'Isa Musa ibn 'Isa al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn 'Ali al-Ju'fi narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, he said: 'Alqamah took my hand and narrated to me that 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud took his hand, then taught him the tashahhud, and he mentioned the tashahhud in the hadith we reported — and he did not mention therein the addition in it over people's tashahhud."
"4351","3800 - And that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym and Abu Ghassan narrated to us, and the wording is that of Abu Nu'aym, they both said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar, and he said: So when you do that or complete this, your prayer has been completed; if you wish to stand then stand, and if you wish to sit then sit."
"4352","3801 - And that al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar."
"4353","3802 - And that Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I came to al-Aswad ibn Yazid and said: Abu al-Ahwas has added in the khutbah of the prayer: ""wa al-mubarakat."" He said: Go to him and tell him: al-Aswad forbids you, and says: Alqamah learned them from Abdullah just as one learns a surah of the Qur'an; Abdullah counted them on his hand, then he mentioned the tashahhud of Abdullah. So it is negated that the addition in the first hadith is from Abdullah, and it is established that it is from Mujahid. And among what proves the corruption of that, and the obligation of the latter apart from it which the people follow in their prayers, is that Ibn Umar, Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, Jabir ibn Abdillah, and others among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated the tashahhud from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without contradicting what they followed of it during his life and after his death, and we have mentioned that in its chapter in our book Sharh Ma'ani al-Athar. And among what also confirmed that is that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, after the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught the people the tashahhud in the same way."
"4354","3803 - As Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Abu al-Siddiq al-Naji, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, used to teach us the tashahhud upon the minbar just as children are taught in the kuttab, then he mentioned the tashahhud of Ibn Mas'ud exactly the same. And that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, taught the people the tashahhud while he was upon the minbar."
"4355","3804 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Malik ibn Anas informed me, that Ibn Shihab narrated to them both, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, that he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, teaching the people the tashahhud upon the minbar, saying: Say: al-Tahiyyat belongs to Allah, al-Zakiyat belongs to Allah, al-Salawat belongs to Allah; peace be upon you O Prophet and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. This is how Yunus dictated it to us."
"4356","3805 - And he narrated it to us in the Muwatta of Malik, from Ibn Wahb, from Malik, that Ibn Shihab narrated to him, from Urwah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, that he heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, while upon the minbar teaching the people the tashahhud, saying: Say: al-Tahiyyat belongs to Allah, al-Zakiyat al-Tayyibat, al-Salawat belongs to Allah; peace be upon you O Prophet and the mercy of Allah; peace be upon you, O righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. So someone said: How is it permissible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) be addressed after his death with the same address as he was addressed in his life? So our answer to him in that, by the guidance of Allah Almighty and His aid, was: Abu Ubayd mentioned from Ibn Uyaynah that among what Allah honored His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with is that he be greeted after his death just as he was greeted in his life, and this was good, and one of those who derived from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this a good meaning."
"4357","3806 - And it is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to the graveyard and said: Peace be upon you, O abode of a believing people, and we, if Allah wills, shall join you; I wish I had seen our brothers. They said: O Messenger of Allah, are we not your brothers? He said: Rather you are my Companions, and my brothers are those who will come afterwards, and I am their forerunner at the Hawd."
"4358","3807 - And it is what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar. He said: So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) greeted the people of the graveyard though they were dead, just as he used to greet them while they were alive, and when that is permissible regarding the people of the graveyard, it is more permissible regarding the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and this is a good meaning, and from Allah we ask guidance. And there has been narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding what falls under this meaning, something like what was narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in it."
"4359","3808 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah ibn Qa'nab narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Namir, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Whenever it was her night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he would go out at the end of the night to al-Baqi' and say: Peace be upon you, O abode of a believing people; there has come to you what you were promised, deferred until tomorrow, and we, if Allah wills, shall join you; O Allah, forgive the people of Baqi' al-Gharqad."
"4360","3809 - And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Sharik ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Namir, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar, except that he said: And there has come to you what you were promised, and Allah is the Granter of success."
"4361","593 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him is charity for every day, and whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him is the like of it as charity for every day. 3810 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him is charity for every day. Then I heard him saying: For every day the like of it as charity. He said: So I said to him: I heard you saying: for him is charity for every day, then you have now said: for him is for every day the like of it as charity, so he said: When the debt has not yet fallen due, for him is charity for every day, but when the debt falls due and he grants him respite, then for him is the like of it as charity for every day. Abu Ja'far said: So it is possible that the one asked about what he was asked in this hadith is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and possible that it is someone below him among the narrators of this hadith, so we examined that."
"4362","3811 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him is charity for every day. He said: And I heard him saying: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him is the like of it as charity for every day. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, you said: charity for every day, then you said: for him is for every day the like of it as charity, so he said: Charity for every day so long as the debt has not fallen due, but when the debt falls due, if he grants him respite after it becomes due, then for him is the like of it as charity for every day. So by this hadith we established that the one asked in it is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then we reflected on his answer to his questioner, and we found it is known to us to be about debts from loans, not about other debts such as prices of sales and the like; because debts from prices of sales and the like are equal, while loans are substitutes for other things in which there is no praise for their people for which they are rewarded. Loan funds are wealth whose owners volunteer by lending them to whom they lend, so he may use them for his own benefit, so they are praised for that and rewarded for it. It is possible that such waiting is until the period to which the loan bound the lender, as those of the people of Madinah who say that say, among them Malik ibn Anas, so his reward in that is what Allah Almighty grants him; then when the debt falls due to him and he grants respite to the one who owes it, his reward in that is above his first reward. If this is the reality of this hadith, there is established in it what those people say about loans: that terms are established in them as they are established in other things. And it is possible that the reward for that is not for an obligatory term upon the lender, but for a term promised by the one to whom he lent his wealth, and though a promise is not enforced by judgment, the Shariah obliges fulfilling it, and praises who fulfills it and blames breach of it; so the lender has promised a term for which he has reward for fulfilling it, and the Shariah prevents him from breaking his promise in that. Then when that term expires, that promise leaves him, and the Shariah permits him to demand his debt; then if he grants respite after that to the one who owes it, his reward for that is greater than his reward for it before that. And this is a good interpretation, and it is what accords with the principles of Abu Hanifah and his companions, and al-Shafi'i, and Allah knows best what its ruling was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among these two interpretations or others beyond our knowledge, and from Allah we ask guidance."
"4363","594 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship and remits it, Allah Almighty will shade him in His shade on a day when there is no shade but His shade. 3812 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Ayyub, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, that he was demanding a man for a debt, and he hid from him, so he said: What drove you to that? He said: Hardship, so he made him swear to that, and he swore, so he called for his document and gave it to him, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever grants delay to one in hardship, or remits it from him, Allah will save him from the distresses of the Day of Resurrection."
"4364","3813 - And Yunus narrated it to us another time from Ibn Wahb, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, and he did not mention Ayyub in it."
"4365","3814 - And Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Khidash narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, that his father pursued a debtor of his, and he hid from him, then he found him, and he said: I am in hardship, he said: By Allah? He said: By Allah. So Abu Qatadah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever is pleased that Allah Almighty save him from the distresses of the Day of Resurrection, let him grant respite to one in hardship, or remit it for him."
"4366","3815 - And al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Mujahid al-Madini Abu Hazrah narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn al-Walid ibn Ubadah ibn al-Samit, from Abu al-Yasar - Abu Ja'far said: And Abu al-Yasar is Ka'b ibn Amr - he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship or remits it for him, Allah Almighty will shade him in His shade."
"4367","3816 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr and Sa'id ibn Bishr al-Azdi narrated to us, they said: Mahdi ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar."
"4368","3817 - And what Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Za'idah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Rib'i ibn Hirash, from Abu al-Yasar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship or remits it from him, Allah will shade him in His shade on a day when there is no shade but His shade."
"4369","3818 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, from Ubaydillah ibn Amr, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever aids a warrior in the path of Allah, or a debtor in his hardship, or a mukatab regarding his manumission price, Allah Almighty will shade him in His shade on a day when there is no shade but His shade."
"4370","3819 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr, judge of Kirman, narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Abdullah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, that Sahl narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar."
"4371","3820 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed us, he said: Yazid ibn al-Had informed me, from Mu'attib, freedman of Asma daughter of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, that he heard Abu Qatadah al-Sulami saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, or remits it for him, Allah will shade him in the shade of His Throne."
"4372","3821 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiyah, from Hanzalah ibn Qays, from Abu al-Yasar al-Badri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever loves that Allah Almighty shade him in His shade - and he pointed with his hand above his eyebrows - let him grant respite to one in hardship or remit it for him."
"4373","3822 - Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, from Qays ibn Sa'd, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar was demanding payment from a man, and he hid from him, so he called out: Do you detain me and hide from me? He said: I did that only because I do not find what to pay you, he said: By Allah? He said: By Allah. So he took his document and erased it, then said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah Almighty will shade a man on a day when there is no shade but His shade who grants delay to one in hardship until his ease, or erases it from him."
"4374","3823 - And Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from Ibn Shihab, that Ubaydillah ibn Abdillah narrated to him, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A man used to lend to people, and he used to say to his servant: When you come upon one in hardship, overlook him, that Allah Almighty may overlook us; so he met Allah Almighty and He overlooked him."
"4375","3824 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar."
"4376","3825 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, he said: al-Zubaydi narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Abdillah ibn Utbah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar. Abu Ja'far said: So the shade mentioned in these reports could mean actual shade from things by whose like the children of Adam are harmed in this world, like the sun, so he is shaded from its like on the Day of Resurrection by what Allah Almighty shades him with of His shade besides which there is no shade that day; and His saying 'in His shade' could mean: in His protection or in His covering, and whoever is in Allah's protection or covering is shielded from hateful things. Like what is said in this world: So-and-so is in the shade of so-and-so, meaning in his protection, and in his sufficing him the things others seek with toil and effort and activity. So someone said: What reward is there for one who grants respite to one in hardship, when had he demanded him he would not have reached anything from him; reward is only for one who leaves what he can take, while what he is unable to take, it is reasoned he has no reward for leaving. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and aid: Hardship may be utter lack with which nothing can be reached, and may be scantiness with which something can be reached but if taken from the debtor it burdens him and exposes him and harms him; hardship comprises both, though they differ in it, so one of them is destitute by it and the other is not destitute by it; every destitute one is in hardship, but not every one in hardship is destitute. So it is possible that the one in hardship meant in these reports is the one in hardship who has something which if taken from him burdens him and exposes him and harms him; so whoever grants respite to one in such condition for what he owes, has preferred him over himself, and deserves what those who prefer others over themselves deserve, and is among the people of the promise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in these reports. So it became clear, by Allah's praise, that there is nothing impossible in what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, and from Allah we ask guidance."
"4377","595 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one killed in battle, of which we know with certainty that he meant, when he is hired for a campaign, that he is the hireling until the last drop of his blood. 3826 - Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us, he said: Duhaym ibn al-Yatim narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to me, from Abu Salamah Sulayman ibn Sulaym, from Yahya ibn Jabir, from the nephew of Abu Ayyub, he said: Abu Ayyub wrote to me: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Cities will be opened for you, and levies will be imposed upon you which a man among you will dislike, so he will want to escape them, and come to the tribes offering himself to them, saying: Who will I suffice such-and-such levy for; verily that one is the hireling until the last drop of his blood."
"4378","3827 - And Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, from Abu Salamah Sulayman ibn Sulaym al-Kinani - meaning Kinanah of Kalb - from Yahya ibn Jabir al-Ta'i, from the nephew of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: Abu Ayyub al-Ansari wrote to me: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned something similar. So we reflected on this hadith, and understood it to mean hiring oneself out to go forth in battle on behalf of those who hire; and in that is what necessitates the reward in that battle belongs to the hirer. And we have mentioned in this chapter earlier in this book of ours, in the hadith of Shufayy al-Asbahi, that the hirer has the reward of hiring and the reward of the warrior, and in that was what negated any reward for the warrior for that, since he fought only for compensation for his fighting from the hire he took for it; so when he is killed in that, he is killed as a hireling in something for which he has no reward from his Lord Almighty, since his reward in it was the hire he took from whom he took it so his fighting be for him with what he takes of that hire, and from Allah Almighty we ask guidance."
"4379","596 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the ruling of what is between the khutbah on Friday and entering the prayer: is it a place of speech or a place of silence? 3828 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq and Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn al-Harith al-Baghandi narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ziyad ibn Kulayb, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Qartha', from Salman, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know what Friday is? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best, then he said: Do you know what Friday is? I said: Allah Almighty and His Messenger know best, he said: I said on the third or fourth: It is the day in which your father was gathered, he said: But I will inform you of the news of Friday: There is no Muslim who purifies himself, then walks to the mosque, then remains silent until the imam completes his prayer, except it is expiation for what is between it and the Friday before it, so long as murder is avoided. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is encouragement to be silent between the khutbah for Friday and the Friday prayer, and a people went to that, among them Abu Hanifah; and most of the people of knowledge opposed them in that, among them Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, who saw no harm in speech between the khutbah and the Friday prayer. So we examined what was narrated in this chapter besides this hadith."
"4380","3829 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Munqidh al-Usfuri narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us (h), and we found Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Farrukh al-Ubulli narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, then they both concurred, saying: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes descend from the minbar after the prayer had been called, and a man would intercept him and speak to him at length, then he would advance to the prayer. So in this hadith was the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between the khutbah for Friday and the Friday prayer. So we reflected whether that contradicts the first hadith or not. And we found it possible that what is in the first hadith is upon what is better and more rewarding, not that it is like silence during the Friday khutbah; because silence during the Friday khutbah is obligatory, and speech in it is idle, while silence between the khutbah and the Friday prayer is not so, nor has it the obligatoriness of silence during the khutbah; rather it is encouraged, and leaving it is permissible. And the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it is facilitation for the people, though other than it is better, just as he performed ablution once, while ablution twice is better, and ablution three times is better than both; so he left the better and used what is below it, to inform his nation (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it is permissible for them, not prohibited. So by authenticating these two hadiths there is established what we mentioned in authenticating them."
"4381","So someone said: There has been narrated regarding what the people followed in this matter in the time of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Tha'labah ibn Abi Malik al-Qurazi informed me, that the imam's sitting upon the minbar cuts off speech, and his speech cuts off speech, and he said: They used to converse when Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sat upon the minbar until the mu'adhdhin fell silent; then when Umar stood upon the minbar, no one spoke until he completed both his khutbahs; then when Umar descended from the minbar, having completed his khutbahs, they spoke. He said: So this proves that what they were all upon in that was speech. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and aid: That is also possible upon the latitude we mentioned, not upon other than it, so the people follow them, though other than what they did of that is better and greater in reward, and from Allah we ask guidance."
"4382","597 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who commits intercourse with an animal. 3830 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whom you find upon an animal, kill him, and kill the animal with him. So it was said to Ibn Abbas: What is the matter of the animal? He said: I heard nothing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, but I see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked that its meat be eaten or benefited from, after that deed was done with it."
"4383","3831 - And Ibn Abi Dawud and Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad ibn Zubalah al-Madini narrated to us, they said: Ishaq ibn Muhammad al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail - meaning Ibn Abi Habibah al-Ashhali - narrated to us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever commits intercourse with an animal, kill him and kill it. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined these two hadiths, and found the hadith of Yusuf goes back to Amr ibn Abi Amr, a man whose narration has been criticized without dropping it; and we found the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud and Ibn Zubalah goes back to Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Abi Habibah, a man abandoned in hadith according to all the people of hadith. Then we considered these two hadiths and found them both referred to Ibn Abbas, and we have found from Ibn Abbas through sound chains what repels the ruling mentioned therein."
"4384","As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hujr informed us, he said: Ali ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Nu'man - meaning Abu Hanifah - from Asim, from Abu Razin, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: There is no hadd upon one who comes to an animal."
"4385","And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Razin, from Ibn Abbas, something similar."
"4386","And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isra'il, Abu Bakr, Abu al-Ahwas, and Sharik narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Abu Razin, from Ibn Abbas, something similar."
"4387","And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Razin, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, something similar."
"4388","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu Razin, from Ibn Abbas, something similar. Abu Ja'far said: So what we narrated from Ibn Abbas of these hadiths is better in isnad from him than the first two hadiths. And the first two hadiths are either sound or unsound; if unsound, we are spared speech about them, and if sound, then Ibn Abbas did not say after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts what he had learned from him contradicting it, except after the abrogation was established with him. And in that is what proves the fall of the first two hadiths and the obligation to abandon them. And in this is sufficiency and proof in repelling them. But we also want to repel them by what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) earlier in this book of ours, by which proof is established from him, that the blood of a Muslim man is not lawful except by one of three: disbelief after faith, or adultery after chastity, or killing a soul without a soul. And in that is what repels killing for other than these three things, unless proof stands of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joining another to them, so it is joined; and the prohibition is to kill a soul for other than them or other than what he joined to them, and we did not find that. So therein was what repels that one be killed for other than them, and with Allah is success."
"4389","598 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who commits intercourse with a mahram relative of his. 3832 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: al-Farwi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever commits intercourse with a mahram, kill him. And in what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, of the fall of the narration of Ibrahim ibn Ismail according to the people of hadith, and of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited in the hadith we mentioned therein of killing for other than the three things we mentioned therein, is what spares us speech in this chapter, and what necessitates referring whoever refuses that to the hadd Allah Almighty mentioned in His Book and upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding adultery, and from Allah Almighty we ask guidance."
"4390","599 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one found doing the deed of the people of Lut. 3833 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi' informed us, from Asim ibn Umar, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The one who does the deed of the people of Lut, stone the upper and the lower, stone them both together."
"4391","3834 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whom you find doing the deed of the people of Lut, kill the doer and the one done to. Abu Ja'far said: So in what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah is the obligation of stoning, without distinction between the ruling of one who did that having been chaste, and his ruling not having been chaste. So it is possible that is specific to one who does this deed, distinguishing him from the adulterer. So we examined that: whether it was narrated explained as such or not."
"4392","So we found Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym narrated to me, that Mujahid and Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrated to them both, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to say about a virgin found upon sodomy: that he is stoned, whether chaste or not. So by that we established that his ruling according to Ibn Abbas was stoning. And it is possible that was with him from taking it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and possible he said it by opinion. And we found what was narrated in the hadith of Amr ibn Abi Amr in the command to kill him may mean by stoning, so agreeing with the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, and may mean by other than stoning, so repelled by what we mentioned of established proof from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) repelling killing for other than the three mentioned things. Except that since there entered in this chapter what entered of which we found nothing but these two hadiths, we looked at what the people of knowledge said about that."
"4393","So we found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: The hadd of the sodomite is the hadd of the adulterer. So in this is distinction between the hadd of the virgin and other than the virgin in that. And this hadith is from Ata, one of the companions of Ibn Abbas; so it is possible he said it thus from taking it from Ibn Abbas, and possible otherwise."
"4394","And we found Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, and Khalid, from al-Hasan, they said: The hadd of the sodomite is the hadd of the adulterer."
"4395","And we found Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Hashim narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah and Sufyan, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, something similar. Abu Ja'far said: And we found in this chapter nothing but what we narrated therein. And when the hadd of the chaste in that must be referred to the hadd of the adulterer, the hadd of the virgin in it must be referred to the hadd of the adulterer. And we found them also not differing on the obligation of ghusl from it, even without emission, just as ghusl is obligated from it when intercourse is in the front; and when that is so in what we mentioned, it must be the same in what we described of the obligation of the hadd and of the distinction between the chaste state in it and the non-chaste state. So if someone says: We have seen this occur from a man to a woman in her behind, and it does not obligate mahr upon him if doubt entered in what was from him to her, as would be upon him had he come to her in her front; and when it must differ in mahr from its ruling in the front, it must differ in the hadd. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and aid: What was mentioned of that in mahr is as mentioned, and what we mentioned of ghusl in that is as we mentioned, and ghusl is intermediate between them; so it must be referred to the more similar of the two. And we found the hadd among the rights of Allah Almighty, and ghusl among the rights of Allah Almighty, and mahr among the rights of humans; so Allah's right of the hadd is more similar to His right of ghusl than His right in the hadd is to the human rights of mahr. And this is the saying of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan both. And we have mentioned in this chapter the hadith of Ibn Abbas from the hadith of Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Darawardi, and Sulayman ibn Bilal agreed with him, narrating it from Amr likewise."
"4396","3835 - As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, freedman of al-Muttalib, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you find him doing the deed of the people of Lut, kill him. And from Allah we ask guidance."
"4397","600 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: The shade of the believer on the Day of Resurrection is his charity. 3836 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Harmalah ibn Imran al-Tujibi narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The shade of the believer on the Day of Resurrection is his charity."
"4398","3837 - And al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun saying: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Marthad ibn Abdillah al-Yazani, who was among the best people of Egypt, and he would not leave the mosque except with charity in his sleeve; sometimes a cake would come out with him, sometimes an onion; so I would say to him: This soils your garment, and he would say: One of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The shade of the believer on the Day of Resurrection is his charity. So we reflected on this hadith, and its sense with us - and Allah knows best - is that what is meant is the reward of his charity. And the shade in that is like the shade mentioned in the hadith we mentioned about one who grants respite to one in hardship or remits it, earlier in this book of ours, and with Allah is success."
"4399","601 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the obligatory tawaf upon the qarin of umrah and hajj: is it one tawaf or two tawafs? 3838 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Aishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: When you return to Makkah, your tawaf for your hajj suffices you for your hajj and your umrah. Abu Ja'far said: Thus we found this hadith from the narration of Abdullah ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Aishah. And we found it from the narration of others from Ata, from Aishah, with wording contrary to these wordings; they are Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, Hajjaj ibn Artah, and Habib al-Mu'allim, who is Habib ibn Abi Baqiyyah."
"4400","3839 - As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, and Abd al-Malik informed us, from Ata, from Aishah, that she said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, will all your family return with a hajj and an umrah but me? He said: Depart, for it suffices you. Hajjaj said in his hadith, from Ata: So she persisted upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he ordered her to go out to al-Tan'im and enter ihram from it for umrah, and sent with her her brother Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr; so she entered ihram from it for umrah, then came, circumambulated, ran, shortened, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed on her behalf. Abd al-Malik said, from Ata: He sacrificed a cow on her behalf."
"4401","3840 - And as Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi informed me, from Habib al-Mu'allim, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: Jabir ibn Abdillah, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to me, that Aishah menstruated, and she performed all the rites, except that she did not circumambulate the House; then when she became pure and performed ifadah, she said: O Messenger of Allah, will they go forth with a hajj and an umrah while I go forth with hajj? So he ordered Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr to go out with her to al-Tan'im, and she performed umrah after the hajj in Dhu al-Hijjah. Abu Ja'far said: So in the hadith of Abdullah ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Aishah, is what indicates she had remained in the sanctity of the umrah for which she had entered ihram until she exited from it, and from the hajj for which she had entered ihram at one time. And in that also is what indicated that the tawaf from her was for the hajj and for the umrah, as the tawaf of the qarin is in his hajj and umrah for both. Except that the phrase in the hadith of Ibn Abi Najih attributed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said to her: Your tawaf for your hajj suffices you for your hajj and your umrah, is remote to hearts to be from the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); because if the hajj has a tawaf other than the tawaf of the umrah, it is for it not for the umrah, and if the tawaf is for both together, it is not permissible to attribute it to the hajj apart from the umrah, nor to the umrah apart from the hajj, and Allah knows best the reality of the matter in that. And in the hadith of Abd al-Malik and al-Hajjaj, from Ata, that Aishah said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Will all your family return with a hajj and an umrah but me? He said: So in that is what indicated she was not then in an umrah, but was in a hajj with no umrah with it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny that of her saying. So in that is what indicated she was not then in an umrah, so it is impossible that the tawaf from her sufficed her for an umrah in which she was not yet. So disagreement occurred upon Ata in this hadith from Aishah as we mentioned, so both narrations from him are equivalent, neither more worthy than the other except by an indication from besides them. Then this Habib al-Mu'allim narrated from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, in the story of Aishah what indicates what Abd al-Malik and Hajjaj narrated from Ata from Aishah, and contradicts what Ibn Abi Najih narrated from Ata from Aishah. Then we returned to what was narrated in that from other than the hadith of Ata to establish the reality of the matter in that meaning, if Allah wills."
"4402","3841 - So we found Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed me. 3842 - And we found al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me (h), and we found Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: My father and Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, they said: al-Layth informed us, then they all concurred, saying: From Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Aishah and found her weeping, and said: What is the matter with you? She said: My matter is that I menstruated, and the people have exited ihram while I have not, and I have not circumambulated the House, and the people are now going to the hajj. He said: This is a matter Allah Almighty decreed upon the daughters of Adam, so bathe, then enter ihram for hajj. So she did, and stood the standings, until when she became pure she circumambulated the Ka'bah and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then he said: You have exited from your hajj and your umrah together. So she said: O Messenger of Allah, I feel in myself that I did not circumambulate the House until I performed hajj. He said: Go, O Abd al-Rahman, and perform umrah with her from al-Tan'im, and that was on the night of al-Hasbah."
"4403","3843 - And we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn al-Haytham ibn al-Jahm al-Abdi al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that Jabir ibn Abdillah says, then he mentioned something similar. Abu Ja'far said: So in this report is that Aishah's exit was from her umrah and from her hajj together, and that supports what Ibn Abi Najih narrated from Ata from her in this story. And what is in the hadith of Habib al-Mu'allim, from Ata, from Jabir, in her story indicates the opposite; because in it is her address to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not deny: Will they go forth with a hajj and an umrah while I go forth with hajj? So in that is what indicated she was in a hajj with no umrah with it; for had she been in an umrah and a hajj, she and others would have been equal in that, and they would not have excelled her in anything, nor would she have needed an umrah after the hajj, after the umrah and hajj that were from her. Then we looked at this story of Aishah from other than the hadith of Jabir, how it was. And we found al-Aswad ibn Yazid narrated from her therein."
"4404","3844 - What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Aishah, she said: We went out seeing nothing but the hajj; then when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Makkah he circumambulated the House and did not exit ihram, and he had the hady with him; then she menstruated. She said: So we completed our rites of our hajj; then when it was the night of al-Hasbah, the night of departure, she said: O Messenger of Allah, will all your companions return with a hajj and an umrah while I return with a hajj? He said: Had you not circumambulated the House on the nights we arrived? He said: So go with your brother to al-Tan'im and enter ihram for umrah, then your appointment is such-and-such."
"4405","3845 - And what Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, something similar, with addition: Had you not circumambulated on the nights we arrived? I said: No."
"4406","3846 - And what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar."
"4407","3847 - And what Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, then he mentioned something similar. Abu Ja'far said: So in this report is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Aishah: Had you not circumambulated on the nights we arrived? And her informing him that she had not circumambulated. So its sense with us - and Allah knows best - is that had she circumambulated on the nights they arrived, the umrah would have been completed for her; and since she had not circumambulated then, she was otherwise in his ordering her to perform umrah from al-Tan'im, so she have an umrah with the hajj that became hers. And in his ordering her to perform umrah is what indicated she had exited the first umrah before that; because it is not permissible according to all the people of knowledge that an umrah enter upon an umrah. And if one did that he would be doing wrong, then they differ on what is obligatory upon him: a group of them say: It does not bind him and he is in the ruling of one who did not enter ihram for it, and it is the saying of Muhammad ibn al-Hasan and al-Shafi'i, and that was narrated from Ata ibn Abi Rabah."
"4408","Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata. And a group of them say: It has bound him, so when he acts in the first he becomes abandoning this one for which he entered ihram, and upon him for abandoning it is blood and an umrah in its place, and among those who said that is Abu Hanifah."
"4409","Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated it to us, from Ali ibn Ma'bad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah. And a group of them say: When he entered ihram for it, it bound him, and he was then abandoning it, and upon him is blood for abandoning it and an umrah in its place, and among those who said that is Abu Yusuf."
"4410","Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated it to us, from Ali, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad mentioned to us in this narration of his, from Ali, from Muhammad, that it is also the saying of Muhammad."
"4411","And as for the latter saying of Muhammad we mentioned before this, Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated it to us, from his father, from Muhammad. And since entering an umrah upon an umrah is not praiseworthy according to all of them, it is impossible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) order Aishah with what has no praise in it. So that proved she had exited her umrah by abandoning tawaf for it on the nights they arrived, either by heading to Arafah intending hajj, as a group of the people of knowledge say, among them Abu Hanifah in one of his two sayings: Whoever enters ihram for umrah while in a hajj, or was in an umrah and a hajj, then heads to Arafah without circumambulating for his umrah, that he thereby abandons his umrah, and upon him for abandoning it is blood and an umrah in its place."
"4412","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated that of his saying to us, from his father, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah. And a group say: He is not abandoning it until he stands at Arafah after midday, then he is abandoning it, and upon him for abandoning it is blood and an umrah in its place, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah contrary to his other saying."
"4413","Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated it to us of his saying, from Ali, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, from him. So Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was abandoning her umrah by one of two matters: either by heading to Arafah for her hajj, or by standing at Arafah for her hajj, and Allah Almighty knows best by which of that it was. So thereby it is impossible if she was a qarinah, and it is established she was performing hajj singly with no umrah with it, since she had exited her umrah before that by what she exited by. So someone said: We have found in the hadith of Jabir what indicates she was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a qarinah; because in it is his sacrificing a cow on her behalf, and that is only for a sacrifice due upon her in what she was in, namely her combining hajj with umrah."
"4414","3848 - And he mentioned what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed a cow on behalf of Aishah in his hajj. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and aid: It is possible the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sacrificed on her behalf what he sacrificed for her abandoning the umrah and exiting from it before its completion, as those of the people of knowledge we mentioned say about an umrah when abandoned before completion: upon its abandoner is blood. And when the hadith bears what we mentioned, there is in it no proof for you of what you mentioned. Then we looked at this story of Aishah from her from other than the narration of al-Aswad from her, and we found Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated it from her with what also necessitates her exit from that umrah before heading to Arafah and before her ihram for hajj."
"4415","3849 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed us, from his father, from Aishah, she said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) coinciding with the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah; then he mounted me behind him on the day of Arafah while I was menstruating, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave your umrah, undo your hair, comb, and recite talbiyah for hajj. Then when it was the night of al-Batha she became pure, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and he went with her to al-Tan'im, and she recited talbiyah for umrah as restitution for her umrah."
"4416","3850 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, they said: Uthman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from Urwah, from Aishah, she said: I was among those who entered ihram for umrah - meaning with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his hajj - then I menstruated, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me, and ordered me to undo my head, comb, and leave my umrah."
"4417","3851 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We arrived at Makkah while I was menstruating, and I did not circumambulate the House nor between al-Safa and al-Marwah, so I complained of that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Undo your hair, comb, enter ihram for hajj, and leave the umrah. So I did; then when we completed the hajj, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and I performed umrah, and he said: This is in place of your umrah."
"4418","3852 - As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar and Malik informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar, except he did not say: He said: This is in place of your umrah. So in what we narrated is what indicated what we mentioned of her exit from the umrah she was in before entering the hajj for which she entered ihram. And in that is what indicated that at the time of her tawaf she was in a hajj with no umrah with it. Then we looked at her story also from other than the hadith of al-Aswad and Urwah, how it was. And we found al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated therein also what indicated that, except he differed from them in something of his hadith which when stood upon becomes clear what it is, then agreed with them in the rest which we needed to bring for its sake."
"4419","3853 - As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), mentioning nothing but the hajj; then when we came to Sarif, I menstruated, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me while I was weeping, and said: What makes you weep? So I said: I wish I had not performed hajj this year. He said: Perhaps you are menstruating? I said: Yes. He said: This is a matter Allah decreed upon the daughters of Adam, so do what the pilgrim does, except do not circumambulate the House. Then when we came to Makkah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Make it an umrah; so the people did, except whoever had the hady with him. So the hady was with him, and with Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and those of means. Then they entered ihram for hajj; then when it was the day of sacrifice I became pure, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me, and I performed ifadah, until when it was the night of al-Hasbah, I said: O Messenger of Allah, will the people return with a hajj and an umrah while I return with a hajj? So he ordered Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and he mounted me behind him, until we came to al-Tan'im, and I entered ihram for umrah as recompense for the people's umrah they performed. So in this report is what indicated her exit from the first umrah which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the people in their hajj they were in - and Aishah was among them - to make it an umrah. So in that also is what indicated she was not at the time of her tawaf in an umrah with the hajj. Then we looked whether others also agreed with them upon that, and we found Ibn Abi Mulaykah agreed with them upon that."
"4420","3854 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Nafi' ibn Umar, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Aishah, then he mentioned something similar to the hadith of Bakkar and Ibn Khuzaymah, from Uthman al-Mu'adhdhin, from Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, which we mentioned earlier in this chapter. And in what we mentioned is what repels what Ibn Abi Najih narrated from Ata from Aishah in her story; because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not order her to undo her hair while in the sanctity of an umrah, for in that is what sheds her hair, nor order her to comb, especially as combing is mostly with perfume or with what is prohibited in ihram besides it. And in it is what is more indicative than this, namely his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): This is in place of your umrah, or: This is restitution for your umrah. And a thing is not in place of a thing, nor restitution for it, unless that thing was contracted before it. Then we returned to seeking the ruling in that from other than the hadith of Aishah and other than her story we mentioned."
"4421","3855 - So we found al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Nafi', from Abdullah ibn Umar, who intended hajj in the year al-Hajjaj descended upon Ibn al-Zubayr; so it was said to him: The people will fight among themselves, and we fear they will block you. So he said: You have in the Messenger of Allah a good example; then I shall do as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did: I call you to witness that I have obligated an umrah. Then he went out until when he was at the back of al-Bayda, he said: The matter of hajj and umrah is but one; I call you to witness that I have obligated a hajj with my umrah. And he gifted a hady he bought at Qudayd, and went on reciting talbiyah for both together, until he arrived at Makkah, and circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and did no more than that; he did not sacrifice, nor shave, nor shorten, nor exit from anything prohibited to him, until the day of sacrifice came, when he sacrificed and shaved. And he held that he had discharged the tawaf of hajj and umrah by his first tawaf, and likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. Thus al-Rabi' narrated it to us from Shu'ayb from al-Layth."
"4422","3856 - As for Yazid ibn Sinan, he narrated it to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to me, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Nafi' narrated to me, and he mentioned something similar, with addition: And he said: Likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. And these two differ; because what is in Shu'ayb's narration of his saying, 'and likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did,' could be from the saying of Nafi', so reverting to disconnection, while what is in Abu Salih's hadith informs that it is from the speech of Ibn Umar, restoring it to connection. So someone said: In this is what indicates that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) circumambulated for his umrah and his hajj a single tawaf. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and aid: Salim narrated from Ibn Umar what informs that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that hajj of his was a mutamatti', not a qarin."
"4423","3857 - As Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim informed me, that Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu' in the Farewell Hajj, with umrah unto hajj, and gifted, driving the hady from Dhu al-Hulayfah. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began and entered ihram for umrah, then entered ihram for hajj, and the people performed tamattu' with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with umrah unto hajj. Abu Ja'far said: So this informs that the tawaf of the umrah was before the tawaf of the hajj; because tamattu' is done thus, and because the ihram of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the hajj was only after he had circumambulated for the hajj which turned into an umrah."
"4424","3858 - As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, he said: We entered upon Jabir ibn Abdillah and asked him about the hajj of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained nine years without performing hajj, then announced among the people in the tenth that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) will perform hajj. So many people came to Madinah seeking to follow the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So we went out until when we came to Dhu al-Hulayfah, he entered ihram with tawhid, and the people entered ihram with what they enter ihram with, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not object to them in anything, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kept to his talbiyah. Jabir said: We intended nothing but the hajj; we did not know the umrah, until when we were at the end of the tawaf at al-Marwah, he said: Had I faced of my affair what I have passed, I would not have driven the hady, and I would have made it an umrah. So whoever has no hady with him, let him exit ihram and make it an umrah. So the people exited ihram and shortened, except the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and whoever had the hady with him. So in that is what indicated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had circumambulated the tawaf which reverted to an umrah before that; so his umrah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had been circumambulated for then. And we understood thereby that the tawaf he circumambulated after returning to Mina was a tawaf for his hajj not for his umrah; because the mutamatti' circumambulates before going out to Mina for his umrah, or for his umrah and his hajj according to what is differed therein - no tawaf for his umrah other than that tawaf - then the tawaf he circumambulates after returning from Mina is for his hajj not his umrah. So it is impossible that Ibn Umar meant by his saying, 'and likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did,' that he had circumambulated a single tawaf for his umrah and his hajj; because that tawaf from him was from him for his hajj, since his umrah had been circumambulated for once. And the umrah has but one tawaf, while the hajj has two tawafs: a tawaf upon arrival at Makkah, and a tawaf after returning from Mina. So this speaker said: There has been narrated from Aishah what indicated that the qarin circumambulates for his hajj and his umrah a single tawaf, not two tawafs, and that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who combined hajj and umrah likewise circumambulated."
"4425","3859 - And he mentioned what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the Farewell Hajj, and we entered ihram for umrah. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever has a hady with him, let him enter ihram for hajj with the umrah, then not exit until he exits from both together. So I arrived at Makkah menstruating, not having circumambulated the House nor between al-Safa and al-Marwah, so I complained of that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Undo your head, comb, enter ihram for hajj, and leave the umrah. Then when we completed the hajj, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr to al-Tan'im, and I performed umrah, and he said: This is in place of your umrah. She said: So those who entered ihram for umrah circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then exited ihram, then circumambulated another tawaf after returning from Mina for their hajj. As for those who combined hajj and umrah, they circumambulated for both a single tawaf. He said: So this is Aishah informing in this hadith that those who combined hajj and umrah circumambulated for both a single tawaf. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and aid: It has been narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu' in that hajj of his."
"4426","3860 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah informed me that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his tamattu' with umrah unto hajj, and the people performing tamattu' with him, like what Salim ibn Abdillah informed me about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning his hadith we mentioned in that earlier in this chapter. And when he was therein a mutamatti', his tawaf for his umrah is only upon his arrival, and his tawaf after returning from Mina is only for his hajj apart from his umrah. So thereby it is possible that Aishah's saying, 'they circumambulated for both a single tawaf,' means: a single tawaf for the ihram they were in, that tawaf being for the hajj not for the umrah. And among what established that the tawaf of the qarin is two tawafs is that Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that hajj of his, and his madhhab in the tawaf of the qarin is that it is two tawafs."
"4427","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim or Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu Nasr, he said: I entered ihram for hajj, and I met Ali, and said to him: I entered ihram for hajj; can I add an umrah to it? He said: No; had you entered ihram for umrah, then wanted to join the hajj to it, you would have joined it. He said: I said to him: How do I do if I want that? He said: Pour upon yourself a waterskin of water, then enter ihram for both together, and circumambulate for each one of them a tawaf."
"4428","And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Mansur informed me, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu Nasr al-Sulami, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, something similar. Abu Dawud said: Qays said: Mansur said: So I mentioned that to Mujahid, and he said: I used to give people fatwa only with a single tawaf, but now no."
"4429","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman - meaning al-A'mash - from Ibrahim with his isnad something similar."
"4430","And as Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Malik, from Abu Nasr, something similar. Mansur said: So I mentioned that to Mujahid, and he said: I would not give people fatwa except with a single tawaf, but now no."
"4431","And as Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ata narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim and Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Udhaynah, he said: I asked Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned something similar. He said: So thereby we understood that this Abu Nasr is Abd al-Rahman ibn Udhaynah. So it is impossible that Ali order contrary to what they did with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there has been narrated from Ibn Mas'ud something similar, and he was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his hajj."
"4432","As Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from al-Hakam, from Ziyad ibn Malik, from Ali and Abdullah, they said: The qarin circumambulates two tawafs and runs two sa'ys. Abu Ja'far said: And when there is no tawaf for the umrah but the tawaf of arrival, and the tawaf of the hajj for arrival is not the tawaf for it after returning from Mina - because the tawaf for it after returning from Mina is the obligatory one, and the tawaf for the umrah which is obligatory therein is the tawaf upon arrival - so their places differ, we understood thereby that whoever combines hajj and umrah has combined two ihrams, the tawaf for each one of them in a time other than the time of the other tawaf. So we understood thereby that they are two tawafs, not one tawaf, and with Allah Almighty is success."
"4433","602 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to us, and he is the truthful, the believed, and what in it is from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and what in it is from the speech of Ibn Mas'ud. 3861 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with something like his hadith."
"4434","Which Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris and Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, saying in it: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris and Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-A'mash, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud narrated to us with something like his hadith."
"4435","Which Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us. He said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us from al-A'mash, he said: I heard Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: I heard Abdullah saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is the truthful, the believed, narrated to us: The creation of one of you is gathered in his mother's belly forty days and forty nights as blood, then is a clot like that, then is a lump like that, then an angel is sent to him and ordered to write his provision, his term, and wretched or happy. So by Allah, one of you does the deeds of the people of Paradise until there is between him and it but a cubit, then it overcomes him, and he does the deeds of the people of Hell, and enters Hell. And one of you does the deeds of the people of Hell until there is between him and it but a cubit, then it overcomes him, and he does the deeds of the people of Paradise, and enters Paradise."
"4436","3862 - And Yazid and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah and my father both narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Ibn Mas'ud, except that my father did not raise it. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned something similar."
"4437","3863 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: I heard Zayd ibn Wahb saying: I heard Ibn Mas'ud saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is the truthful, the believed, narrated to us."
"4438","3864 - And Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb al-Juhani narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is the truthful, the believed, narrated to us, then he mentioned something similar, except that he said: So the Book that preceded overcomes him, in both places of it, and he did not say: So it overcomes him."
"4439","3865 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Mas'ud saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is the truthful, the believed, narrated to us, then he mentioned something similar, until he ended to: And wretched or happy. So he said after that: Then the spirit is breathed into him. Zuhayr said: And I think he said: And one of you does the deed of the people of Paradise, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"4440","3866 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakha'i narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is the truthful, the believed, narrated to us: One of you is gathered in his mother's belly forty days, then is a clot like that, then is a lump like that, then the angel is sent to him with four words, and writes his deed, his term, his provision, and wretched or happy; then the spirit is breathed into him. Then one of you does the deed of the people of Hell until there is between him and it but a cubit, then the Book overcomes him, and he does the deed of the people of Paradise, and enters Paradise. And a man does the deed of the people of Paradise until there is between him and it but a cubit, then the Book overcomes him, and he does the deed of the people of Hell, and enters Hell. Abu Ja'far said: Thus al-A'mash narrated this hadith from Zayd. And Salamah ibn Kuhayl also narrated it from Zayd."
"4441","3867 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is the truthful, the believed, narrated to us: The creation of one of you is in his mother's belly forty days, then is a clot like that, then is a lump like that, then the spirit is breathed into him, then Allah Almighty sends an angel with four words, and writes his term, his provision, and whether he is happy or wretched. And a man does the deed of the people of Paradise until there is between him and it but a cubit, then the preceding Book overtakes him, and he does the deed of the people of Hell, and enters Hell. And a man does the deed of the people of Hell, then the preceding Book overtakes him, and he does the deed of the people of Paradise, and enters Paradise. Abu Ja'far said: So this agreed with what al-A'mash narrated from Zayd."
"4442","3868 - And Ismail ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he is the truthful, the believed, narrated to us, then he mentioned something similar, up to his saying: And wretched or happy. So he said after that: Abdullah said: By the One in whose Hand is the soul of Abdullah, a man does the deed of the people of Paradise, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. So in this was attribution of what is in it of a man's doing the deed of the people of Paradise to its end, to the speech of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud thereby, removing it from the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith."
"4443","3869 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned something similar, except that he said after his saying: And wretched or happy: So by the One in whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, then he mentioned the rest. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is what indicated that this speech to the end of this hadith is from the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not from the speech of Ibn Mas'ud; because that oath could not be from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud as therein, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was then dead; for one swears only by the souls of the living, not by the souls of the dead. And we have found this hadith from the narration of Jarir ibn Hazim, from al-A'mash, with what indicates that this speech is from the speech of Ibn Mas'ud, not from the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4444","3870 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Mihran, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The drop is in the womb forty nights as a drop, forty nights as a clot, forty nights as a lump, then an angel is sent to him and ordered with four words: With his provision, his term, and wretched or happy. So by the One in whose Hand is the soul of Ibn Mas'ud, a man does the deed of... then he mentioned something similar. So thereby we understood that this speech is from the speech of Ibn Mas'ud, not from the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And in whatever sense this speech in reality is - from the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or from the speech of Ibn Mas'ud - it is true; because Ibn Mas'ud is trusted in what he said of that, if he said it, and because we know he did not say that by opinion, for its like is not said by opinion, and he said it only by divine instruction, and divine instruction is only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Rather in the hadith is what indicates his taking it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); because in it is from the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He is ordered to write his provision, his term, and wretched or happy. And wretchedness and happiness are the meaning in the rest of this hadith disputed whether from the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or from the speech of Ibn Mas'ud. So if from the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is from his speech; and if not from his speech but from the speech of Ibn Mas'ud by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instructing him thereof, it is likewise; and if by his deriving it from the wretchedness and happiness mentioned therein, it is as he took it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also by instruction. Abu Ja'far said: And in this hadith is a meaning we found only in the two narrations of Zuhayr and Hafs from al-A'mash, and in the narration of Bakkar from Abu Ahmad from Fitr from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, namely: Then the spirit is breathed into him."
"4445","And that is among what was narrated therein from Abu al-Aliyah. Ismail ibn Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Rabi' ibn Anas, from Abu al-Aliyah, about His saying Almighty: And those of you who die and leave wives, they shall wait by themselves four months and ten. He said: I said: Why were these ten joined to the four months? He said: Because the spirit is breathed into him in these ten."
"4446","3871 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, they said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim ibn Abdillah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the drop falls in the womb - or he said: When the drop is created in the womb - the angel of wombs says while turning away: O Lord, what shall I write? So Allah Almighty decrees to him His command. So he says: Male or female? So Allah Almighty decrees to him His command. So he says: Wretched or happy? So Allah Almighty decrees to him His command. So he writes what he will meet, even the stumble he stumbles."
"4447","3872 - As Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Yunus ibn Yazid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hunaydah, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar."
"4448","3873 - And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hunaydah, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar. And Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar also."
"4449","3874 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: al-Zubayr ibn Abdillah narrated to me, he said: Ja'far ibn Mus'ab narrated to me, he said: I heard Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrating from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah Almighty, when He wills to create the creation, sends an angel, and he enters the womb, and says: O Lord, what? So He says: A boy or a girl, or what Allah Almighty wills to create in the womb. So he says: O Lord, wretched or happy? So He says: Wretched or happy. So he says: O Lord, what is his provision? So He says: Such-and-such. So he says: O Lord, what is his term? So He says: Such-and-such. He said: So he says: O Lord, what is his character? What are his traits? He said: So there is nothing but is created with him in the womb. And Hudhayfah ibn Asid al-Ghifari narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar also, of what we mentioned earlier in this book of ours, so we were spared repeating it here, and from Allah Almighty we ask guidance."
"4450","603 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what Iyad ibn Himar narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Allah Almighty said: I created My servants all as hunafa, and the devils came to them and diverted them from their religion, forbidding to them what I made lawful for them, and commanding them to associate with Me what I sent no authority for. 3875 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Imran al-Sadusi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif ibn Abdillah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Iyad ibn Himar, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying in his khutbah: Allah Almighty commanded me to teach you what you were ignorant of of your religion this day of yours; and every wealth I grant My servant is lawful for him; and I created My servants all as hunafa, and the devils came to them and diverted them from their religion, forbidding to them what I made lawful for them, and commanding them to associate with Me what I sent no authority for."
"4451","3876 - And Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar."
"4452","3877 - And Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us (h), and as Yazid and Ahmad ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, they said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, then they all concurred, saying: Qatadah narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Ziyad, Yazid brother of Mutarrif, and two other men whose names Hammam forgot, narrated to me, that Mutarrif narrated to them, that Iyad ibn Himar narrated to him, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying in his khutbah, then they mentioned something similar."
"4453","3878 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Abdillah ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me - Abu Ja'far said: And the father of his father here is Harun ibn Abi Isa, who narrated from Muhammad ibn Ishaq - he said: And Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Yahya ibn Jabir, from Abd al-Rahman ibn A'idh al-Azdi, he said: And Abd al-Rahman was among the bearers of knowledge, seeking it from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the companions of his Companions, that he narrated to him, from Iyad ibn Himar al-Mujashi'i, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people one day: Shall I not narrate to you what Allah Almighty narrated to me in the Book? Allah Almighty created Adam and his sons as hunafa, Muslims, and gave them wealth lawful with no prohibition in it; whoever wished acquired and whoever wished tilled. Then they made of what Allah Almighty gave them lawful and unlawful, and worshipped the taghuts. So Allah Almighty commanded me to come to them and clarify to them what He fashioned them upon. So I said to my Lord Almighty, addressing Him: Quraysh will crush my head as bread is crushed. So He said to me: Carry it out and I shall support you, spend and I shall spend upon you, fight with those who obey you those who disobey you; for I shall place with every army ten times their like of angels, breathe terror into the breasts of your enemy, give you a Book water does not erase, reminding you of it sleeping and waking. So support me; and these Quraysh have bloodied my face and taken my family, and I challenge them. If I overcome them, they will come to what I called them to, willingly or unwillingly; and if they overcome me, know that I am upon nothing and call you to nothing. He said: And Makhul used to resemble the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn A'idh from Iyad ibn Himar. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined this hadith to establish what is meant therein, if Allah wills. And we found hanaf in the speech of the Arabs is inclination; hence one with a foot inclining to a side is called ahnaf. And the plural of hanif is hunafa. So therefore it was said in this hadith: They are created hunafa, meaning: inclining to what they were created for, namely what Allah Almighty mentioned in His saying: I created jinn and men only to worship Me. And they were thereby hunafa. And in His creating them was that He wrote some of them happy and some wretched, according to the reports mentioned in the chapter before this chapter. And the wretched among them is whoever obeyed the devils in what they called him to, as in this hadith of Iyad; and the happy is whoever opposed them and held to what Allah Almighty created him for of worshipping Him, leaving inclination to other than Him. And there has been narrated from Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the interpretation of this verse."
"4454","What al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Azdi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, about His saying Almighty: I created jinn and men only to worship Me. He said: Upon what I created them upon of My obedience and My disobedience, and My wretchedness and My happiness. Abu Ja'far said: And in that of Ibn Abbas's interpretation was what indicated that Allah Almighty's creating His servants is upon what He wrote in them of His obedience and His disobedience, His wretchedness and His happiness; they do not exit from that to other than it, though their happy deeds were by their choice thereof, and their deeds contrary to that were by their choice thereof. So their happiness was by their praiseworthy deeds from them, and their wretchedness for their blameworthy deeds from them; and all that is of what Allah Almighty decreed in them that they will do those deeds and be happy thereby or wretched thereby. So this hadith of Iyad and the hadiths we mentioned before it in the chapter before this chapter revert to one meaning, some confirming others, none contradicting others, and from Allah Almighty we ask guidance."
"4455","604 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his delivering the inheritance of one who died in his time to his lower freedman whom he had freed. 3879 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us (h), and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, then they concurred, saying: He said: Amr ibn Dinar informed us, from Awsajah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man died leaving no heir but a boy of his whom he had freed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Has he anyone? They said: No, except a boy of his whom he had freed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned his inheritance to the boy."
"4456","3880 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud - meaning al-Harrani - narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, that a man died, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek an heir for him; and they found no heir for him, so he delivered his inheritance to the one who had freed him, from below. I said: Who narrated to you? He said: Awsajah, from Ibn Abbas."
"4457","3881 - And Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman al-Khuza'i al-A'war narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Zayd and Wuhayb ibn Khalid, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Awsajah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man freed a slave of his, then the manumitter died leaving only the manumitted; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned his inheritance to the manumitted."
"4458","3882 - And al-Qasim ibn Abdillah ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ubaydillah Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'id ibn Hassan al-Makhzumi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Awsajah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), leaving no heir but a slave he had freed; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave him his inheritance."
"4459","3883 - And al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed us, from Awsajah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they said: O Messenger of Allah, he has no heir, though the deceased left a freedman he had freed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Give him his wealth. So someone said: How was it permissible for you to abandon a hadith like this, narrated by those imams you narrated it from, from Amr ibn Dinar, with none narrating from others from them contradicting it, while analogy necessitates it; for we found no one inheriting by a meaning but being inherited by it. Of that, we saw kin inherit one another thereby, and we saw spouses inherit one another thereby, husbands inheriting wives thereby and wives inheriting husbands thereby. And when that is so, wala is likewise: when the upper freedman inherits thereby the lower freedman, the lower freedman inherits thereby the upper freedman. So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and aid: Had we been left with analogy, analogy would be as he mentioned. But we were not left with analogy therein, since the scholars around whom fatwa revolves in the lands - of the notables of Hijaz, the notables of Iraq, and besides them of the notables of the rest of the lands - did not accept this hadith by receiving it nor act by it. So that was from them an exclusion of it from the acted-upon reports, and from the accepted reports. And that indicated from them that they abandoned it, either because they found no mention of Awsajah, to whom it reverts, in other than this hadith, or they abandoned it for a meaning they stood upon therein with which its use is impermissible. Then we examined it ourselves and found therein things preventing the lower freedman being an heir of the upper freedman by right of the upper freedman's manumitting him. Of that is what is in the hadith of Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb thereof, namely: So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek an heir for him; and they found no heir for him. So that proved the lower freedman was not an heir to him, and that the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivering his estate to him was like what belongs to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to do with wealth having no deserver, then his granting it to whom he sees fit to grant it. And of that is what is in the hadith of Ali ibn Shaybah and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah thereof, namely: 'And he left no heir but a boy of his whom he had freed.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Has he anyone? They said: No, except a boy of his whom he had freed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) assigned his inheritance to the boy. So it is possible that was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a meaning between him and the boy of kinship, by which he was his agnate or his kin, so he delivered his inheritance to him for that, not for the wala the deceased was upon. And possible he delivered it to him by a wala the manumitted had upon the one who freed him, by which each of them was a patron of his fellow - namely that the manumitter, after manumitting, owned the manumitted one's father, who was a slave, and freed him; so thereby he became his patron and his father's patron, so the manumitter and the manumitted each became a patron of his fellow. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereby delivered the deceased's inheritance to his lower freedman, because he is an upper patron to him. And what bears of interpretation we mentioned, none of its possibilities is more worthy of it than its contrary possibilities, except by proof thereof, either from a written verse, a transmitted sunnah, or consensus of scholars upon it; and all that is absent in what necessitates this meaning. Rather what we found of the scholars being upon contrary to it necessitates it be a deviant saying not to be accepted from its sayer, and the saying of the generality of scholars is proof against him, not his saying proof against them nor opposing their sayings; for they are the successors who took it from the predecessors before them, and likewise those before them were successors to the predecessors before them. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said about the successors."
"4460","3884 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Ruzayq Abu Abdillah al-Alhani, from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This knowledge shall be borne by the upright of every generation; they repel from it the distortion of extremists, the false claims of the nullifiers, and the interpretation of the ignorant. Abu Ja'far said: And the successors are those we mentioned from whom knowledge is taken and to whose sayings it is referred, not others from whom knowledge is not taken nor referred to their saying therein, for their deviance by which they deviated, and their singling out by which they singled out, and from Allah Almighty we ask guidance."
"4461","605 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his saying: Whoever lends two loans shall have the reward of one of them had he given it as charity. 3885 - al-Qasim ibn Abdillah - meaning Ibn Mahdi - narrated to us, he said: Abu Abdillah Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, residing in Basrah, narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: I read to Fudayl ibn Maysarah, from Abu Hariz - Abu Ja'far said: And his name is Abdullah ibn al-Husayn - that Ibrahim narrated to him, that al-Aswad ibn Yazid used to borrow from a freedman of Nakha', a trader; when his stipend came out he would repay him. And his stipend came out, so al-Aswad said to him: If you wish, delay from us, for there have been claims upon us in this stipend. So the trader said to him: I shall not do it. So al-Aswad paid him five hundred dirhams in cash; when he took possession, the trader said to him: Here, take it. So al-Aswad said to him: I asked you and you refused. The trader said: I heard you narrating from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Whoever lends two loans shall have the like of the reward of one of them had he given it as charity. Al-Mu'tamir deemed it sound and accepted it. So someone said: You narrated to us earlier in this book of yours the hadith of Buraydah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him is charity for every day, and whoever grants respite to one in hardship, for him is the like of it as charity for every day, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about that, and said to him: Before its due, charity for every day; then when it falls due and he grants him respite therewith, for him is the like of it as charity for every day. So is this hadith of Ibn Mas'ud contrary to this hadith of Buraydah? So our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance and aid: It is not contrary to it; because the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud is about reward for the loan itself, while the hadith of Buraydah is about reward by the loan after the loan, in granting respite therewith, after the lender has upon the borrower by his lending him his wealth, and after it becomes a debt due to him upon him. And from Allah we ask guidance."
"4462","606 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is meant by Allah Almighty's saying: And when your Lord took from the children of Adam, from their backs, their offspring, to His saying: Will You destroy us for what the nullifiers did. 3886 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik ibn Anas informed him, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, that Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn al-Khattab informed him, from Muslim ibn Yasar al-Juhani, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about this verse: And when your Lord took from the children of Adam, from their backs, their offspring, the verse to His saying: Heedless. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah created Adam, then wiped his back with His right and extracted from him offspring, and said: I created these for Paradise, and by the deed of the people of Paradise they shall act. Then He wiped his back and extracted from him offspring, and said: I created these for Hell, and by the deed of the people of Hell they shall act. So a man said: O Messenger of Allah, then for what is action? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah creates the servant for Paradise, He employs him with the deed of the people of Paradise until he dies upon a deed of the deeds of the people of Paradise, and admits him thereby to Paradise. And when He creates the servant for Hell, He employs him with the deed of the people of Hell until he dies upon a deed of the deeds of the people of Hell, and admits him thereby to Hell. Abu Ja'far said: And this hadith was disconnected, because Muslim ibn Yasar al-Juhani did not meet Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. So we looked at who he took it from, from Umar."
"4463","3887 - So we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid, meaning his father, narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Muslim ibn Yasar al-Juhani, from Nu'aym ibn Rabi'ah al-Azdi; Muslim said: I asked Nu'aym ibn Rabi'ah about this verse: And when your Lord took from the children of Adam, from their backs, their offspring. So he said: I was with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man came to him and asked him about it. So he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When Allah Almighty created Adam... then he mentioned the rest of the hadith similar to what is in the hadith of Yunus. Abu Ja'far said: So thereby we established that the one he took it from, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, is Nu'aym ibn Rabi'ah al-Azdi. So this hadith reverted to connected isnad, except that we need the one who connects it to be of those whose addition is fit to be accepted, from the one who disconnected it. And this Yazid ibn Sinan is not of those in this station, nor of those whose addition in the hadith upon Malik ibn Anas is fit for us to accept, for the grandeur of Malik's rank therein, and this Yazid's falling short of him therein. So we sought it from the narration of another whose addition upon Malik therein is fit for us to accept."
"4464","3888 - So we found Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Wahb ibn Abi Karimah al-Jazari Abu al-Mu'afa informed me, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahim - namely Khalid ibn Abi Yazid - narrated to me, he said: Zayd - meaning Ibn Abi Unaysah - narrated to me, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Muslim ibn Yasar al-Juhani, from Nu'aym ibn Rabi'ah, he said: I was with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man came to him and asked him about this verse: And when your Lord took from the children of Adam, from their backs, their offspring. Then he mentioned something like the hadith of Abu Umayyah, from Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Sinan, from his father, from Zayd, exactly. Abu Ja'far said: So this was of what is fit for us to accept the addition of its narrator upon what Malik narrated thereof; because Abu Abd al-Rahim is accepted in narration, established with the people of hadith. So thereby it was permissible for us to enter this hadith among the hadiths of connected isnads. Then we returned to seeking what is therein of what is meant by the mentioned verse. And we found therein the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing us what Allah Almighty did of extracting Adam's offspring (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his back. And mentioned in this verse are the children of Adam, not Adam himself. So Allah Almighty extracted from his back his offspring, then there was from Him in them what He mentioned in this hadith. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed us from Allah Almighty that He said to those He first extracted from him: I created these for Paradise, and by the deed of the people of Paradise they shall act; and that He said to those He extracted after them from his back: I created these for Hell, and by the deed of the people of Hell they shall act. So thereby we learned that Allah Almighty's knowledge preceded in the children of Adam - of the happy and the wretched - by what shall be from them by which they are happy and by which they are wretched; and that when they come to this world they are upon what preceded in His knowledge that they shall be upon therein; and that He employs His happy ones with the deed of the people of Paradise until He admits them to Paradise as reward for their deeds; and that He employs the wretched among them with the deeds of the people of Hell until He admits them to Hell as punishment for their deeds. Then we looked: was there narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is meant by this verse anything other than mentioned in the hadith of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, which we narrated?"
"4465","3889 - So we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Kulthum ibn Jabr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Allah Almighty took the covenant from the back of Adam at Na'man, meaning Arafah, and brought forth from his loins every atom He seeded, scattering them before Him like dust. Then He spoke to them face to face, saying: Am I not your Lord? They said: Yes, we testify, lest you say on the Day of Resurrection: We were heedless of this; or you say: Our fathers associated before, and we were offspring after them; will You destroy us for what the nullifiers did. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith, of Allah Almighty's extracting Adam's offspring (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from his loins, is like what is in the first hadith, with addition over the first hadith, namely His speaking to them face to face: Am I not your Lord? They said: Yes, we testify. Then He mentioned the rest of the verse we recited. And that is not objectionable in Allah Almighty's subtle power. And others interpreted this verse, of those who did not stand upon what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is meant thereby, that Allah Almighty inspired Adam's offspring (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in creating them, with the knowledge of Him present in all of them: that they have a Creator besides themselves, that they are incapable of creating their like, and that their Creator is unlike them, for He is able to have created them, while they are incapable of the like of that in others, so with that they cannot say otherwise. And that was a testimony from them upon themselves to Allah Almighty that He is their Lord, and a proof against them lest they say, when He seizes them on the Day of Resurrection with the punishment of the wretched among them for the deeds they did in this world: We were heedless of this - meaning, of what He punishes us for of what we did, or of not affirming lordship to You. And since in this world He Almighty sent to them His messengers, revealed to them His Books, clarified therein what He worshipped them with, what He commanded and what He wanted of them, what He forbade them, warning them of punishment therefor if they did it - and this is an interpretation, had we not heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is in the first two hadiths, we would have deemed good from its interpreters, since they interpreted the verse upon what it can bear. But when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified Allah Almighty's intent He meant thereby, that was the proof beside which saying otherwise or interpreting upon other than it is impermissible. And from Allah Almighty we ask guidance."
"4466","607 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about those who love one another for Allah Almighty, give to one another for Him, and visit one another for Him. 3890 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Abu Hazim ibn Dinar, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, that he said: I entered the mosque of Damascus, and there was a youth with bright teeth, the people with him - when they differed in anything they referred to him and issued from his opinion. So I asked about him, and it was said: This is Mu'adh ibn Jabal. Then when it was the morrow, I came early and found he had preceded me in early coming, and found him praying. So I waited until he completed his prayer, then came to him from before his face, greeted him, then said: By Allah, I love you for Allah Almighty. So he said: By Allah? So I said: By Allah. So he said: By Allah? So I said: By Allah. So he took the fold of my cloak and pulled me to him, and said: Rejoice; for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Allah said: My love is due for those who love one another in Me, sit together in Me, visit one another in Me, and give to one another in Me."
"4467","3891 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah ibn Qa'nab narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Idris, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah Almighty said: My love is due for those who love one another in Me, visit one another in Me, give to one another in Me, and sit together in Me."
"4468","3892 - Ali ibn Zayd al-Fara'idi, Fahd ibn Sulayman, and al-Hasan ibn Abdillah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yunus ibn Halbas, from Abu Idris A'idhullah, he said: I entered the mosque of Hims and sat in a circle with over thirty of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among them one would say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying such-and-such, while the others listened; and a man among them would say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying such, while the others listened. And among them was a youth, dark-eyed, bright-toothed; when they differed in anything they ended at his word. Then when I left to my dwelling I spent the longest night, saying: I sat in a circle with so many of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), knowing neither their stations nor their names. Then in the morning I went to the mosque, and there was the dark-eyed youth sitting by a pillar. So I sat to him and said: I love you for Allah Almighty. He said: By Allah, do you love me for Allah Blessed and Exalted? So I said: By Allah, I love you for Allah Almighty. So he took my waist-wrapper until my knee touched his knee, then said: By Allah, do you love me for Allah Almighty? So I said: By Allah, I love you for Allah Almighty. So he said: Shall I not inform you of something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So I said: Yes. So he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Those who love one another in Allah Almighty, Allah Almighty shall shade them with the shade of His Throne on a day when there is no shade but His shade. He said: And while we were thus, a man of those in the circle passed, so I stood to him and said: This one narrated to me a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); did you hear it from him? He said: And what did he narrate to you? He would narrate to you only truth. He said: So I informed him. So he said: I heard this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and better than it I heard him saying, transmitting from Allah Almighty: My love is due for those who love one another in Me, due for those who maintain ties in Me, due for those who visit one another in Me, and due for those who give to one another in Me. I said: Who are you, may Allah have mercy on you? He said: I am Ubadah ibn al-Samit. I said: And who is the youth? He said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal."
"4469","3893 - And al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jabir - namely Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir al-Azdi - narrated to us, he said: Ata al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Idris al-Khawlani saying: I entered the mosque of Hims and sat in a circle, all narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And among them was a young youth; when he spoke the people listened to him, and when a man among them narrated he listened to him. He said: So they dispersed and I did not know who that youth was. So I left to my dwelling and my soul found no rest until I returned to the mosque and sat in it, and there he was. So I stood to him and sat with him until he came to a pillar of the mosque's pillars and prayed fine rak'ahs, then sat. So I faced him, and his silence lengthened without speaking. So I said: Narrate to me, may Allah have mercy on you, for by Allah I love you and love your hadith. So he said to me: By Allah? I said: By Allah. So he pulled me by my waist-wrapper until my knee stuck to his knee, then said, as I think: Praise be to Allah, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Those who love one another out of Allah Almighty's majesty are in Allah Almighty's shade on a day when there is no shade but His shade. I said: Who are you, may Allah have mercy on you? He said: Mu'adh ibn Jabal. So I stood from him, and there was Ubadah ibn al-Samit. So I said: O Abu al-Walid, Mu'adh narrated to me a hadith. He said: And what did he narrate to you? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Those who love one another out of Allah Almighty's majesty are in Allah's shade on a day when there is no shade but His shade. So Ubadah said to me: Come, I shall narrate to you what I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrating from his Lord Almighty. He said: So I came to him, and he said to me: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Your Lord Almighty said: My love is due upon those who love one another in Me, due upon those who sit together in Me, due upon those who visit one another in Me, and due upon those who give to one another in Me."
"4470","3894 - Khayr ibn Arafah Abu al-Tahir narrated to us, he said: Urwah ibn Marwan, known as al-Raqqi, narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Ruzayq narrated to us, from Ata al-Khurasani, from Abu Idris A'idhullah, he said: I came to the mosque of Hims and sat with a circle of thirty men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And among them was a youth, dark, light-bearded, bright-toothed. So I said: Who is this? So they said: This is Mu'adh ibn Jabal. Then when they dispersed I drew near him and said: By Allah, I love you in Allah Almighty. So he struck with his hand to my waist-wrapper and pulled me until my knee stuck, and said: Rejoice, if you are truthful, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Those who love one another by Allah's majesty are under the shade of the Throne on a day when there is no shade but His shade."
"4471","3895 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Ya'la ibn Ata, he said: I heard al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrating from Abu Idris al-A'idhi, he said: I mentioned to Ubadah ibn al-Samit the hadith of Mu'adh ibn Jabal about those who love one another. So he said: I shall narrate to you only what I heard upon the tongue of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): My love is due for those who love one another in Me, due for those who visit one another in Me, due for those who befriend one another purely in Me, or who meet in Me. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined this hadith in its isnad and found therein mention of Abu Idris meeting Mu'adh ibn Jabal and hearing from him what he mentioned of hearing it from him in this hadith. And we have found from him what some people thought contradicted that, denying that Abu Idris met Mu'adh."
"4472","And it is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, he said: I met Ubadah ibn al-Samit and retained from him, met Shaddad ibn Aws and retained from him, counted a number of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Mu'adh escaped me. Then I was informed that he would not sit in a sitting but said: Allah Almighty is a just Judge, blessed be His name; the doubters perish."
"4473","And what Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar, except that he said: And Mu'adh ibn Jabal escaped me, so Yazid ibn Umayrah narrated to me from him, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Abu Ja'far said: So what those we reported of reported imagined, of denying Abu Idris's meeting Mu'adh by what is in this hadith, does not necessitate what he imagined of that; for this hadith is Abu Idris informing of meeting Ubadah and retaining from him, and meeting Shaddad ibn Aws and retaining from him, then saying: And Mu'adh escaped me. So it is possible he meant by his saying 'escaped me,' that he escaped me retaining as I retained from the two he mentioned before him, not that he did not meet him. And how can that be thought of him, with his probity, may Allah have mercy on him, in himself, his precision in narration, and the eminence of those who narrated that from him - namely Abu Hazim ibn Dinar, Ata ibn Abdillah al-Khurasani, Yunus ibn Maysarah ibn Halbas, and al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman - all imams whose narration is accepted, not denied in probity therein, precision, and firmness. And we must carry the narration of one of such standing upon what negates contradiction from it, as far as we find a way. Then we examined the text of this hadith and found it came in two forms: one 'my love is due,' the other 'my love is truly due.' As for 'my love is due,' that obligatoriness may coexist with another obligatoriness of love higher than it, in a rank above its rank of love - as a man says: I love so-and-so, meaning thereby someone, then says after that: And I love so-and-so, for another man, with love above that love. So likewise His saying Almighty, 'my love is due for those He mentioned,' does not prevent His love being due for others with an obligatoriness above that obligatoriness, in a rank higher than its rank. As for 'my love is truly due,' it is above that, the highest ranks of obligatoriness. And Ubadah ibn al-Samit clarified that to Abu Idris when he narrated to him from Mu'adh ibn Jabal what he narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Allah Almighty, 'my love is due,' by his saying to him: I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is better than it; I heard him transmitting from Allah Almighty: My love is truly due. So thereby we understood that what Ubadah narrated to him of what he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is above what Abu Idris narrated to him from Mu'adh from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And among what verifies that is that we find a man saying: So-and-so is learned, affirming knowledge for him, though among the learned there may be one whose rank therein is above his rank therein; and he says: So-and-so is truly learned, raising him thereby to the highest ranks of knowledge. So likewise 'my love is truly due' is upon exaltation, to the highest ranks of His love, for whom it is truly due. And likewise what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we narrated earlier in this book of ours, of his saying to the people of Najran when they asked him to send with them a trustworthy man: I shall send with you a truly trustworthy man, a truly trustworthy man. So he sent with them Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. And that was informing them that he had sent with them one in the highest ranks of trustworthiness. Then he confirmed that by his saying: Every nation has a trustee, and the trustee of this nation is Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. And we mentioned that also with its isnads earlier in this book of ours. And from Allah we ask guidance."
"4474","608 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one praying who does not straighten his back between his bowing and his prostration. 3896 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: I heard Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Ma'mar, from Abu Mas'ud, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no prayer for one who does not straighten his back in bowing and prostration. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined this hadith and found it possible to mean: no complete prayer as the worshipper must bring it, when he does not straighten his back therein between his bowing and his prostration thereby - though it suffices him for the obligation of prayer, with his wasting his own share therein and falling short of the highest ranks its people attain thereby, until he deserves therewith what one who brings it in its perfection, with its obligations and sunnahs, deserves. And a thing may be stressed, so said therein is the like of this, by which the one of whom that is said does not exit the meaning prohibited by that saying."
"4475","3897 - As Muhammad ibn al-Ward al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us (h). And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, they said: Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas, he said: Seldom did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) address us but he said: There is no faith for one with no trustworthiness, and no religion for one with no covenant."
"4476","3898 - And as Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid al-Farisi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, Humayd, and Yunus, from al-Hasan, and a man of Abu Bakrah's offspring informed me, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned something similar. So one with no trustworthiness is not without faith, nor one with no covenant without religion; rather there is no faith of the highest ranks of faith for one with no trustworthiness, and no religion of the highest ranks of religion for one with no covenant. And likewise his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There is no ablution for one who does not mention Allah's name upon his ablution - not that by his ablution thus he has not exited impurity. And we clarified this in the chapter, citing therein things we narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in our book on purification in Sharh Ma'ani al-Athar, whose mention would be long; we disliked repeating it here fearing lengthening the book thereby. Then we looked in this hadith whether Shu'bah was contradicted in the wordings he narrated it with."
"4477","3899 - So we found Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from Umarah, from Abu Ma'mar, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prayer suffices in which a man does not straighten his back when he raises his head from bowing and prostration."
"4478","3900 - And we found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Hilal ibn Yahya ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Abu Ma'mar, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No prayer suffices in which a man does not straighten his back in bowing and prostration. So we examined what al-Thawri and Abu Yusuf narrated this hadith upon, from al-A'mash: does its meaning contradict the meaning of what Shu'bah narrated upon him from him or not? And we found his saying, 'no prayer suffices in which a man does not straighten his back when he raises his head from bowing and prostration,' may mean: it does not suffice him the sufficiency of the highest ranks of excellence - and that is the worthiest construction, so the meanings of the narrations it was narrated upon agree and do not differ. Then we looked: did any besides Abu Mas'ud narrate this hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or not?"
"4479","3901 - So we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Mulazim ibn Amr al-Hanafi narrated to me, he said: My grandfather Abdullah ibn Badr narrated to me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Ali narrated to him, that his father Ali ibn Shayban narrated to him, that he came as a delegate to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us, and glanced with the corner of his eye at a man not straightening his back in bowing and prostration. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned and said: O company of Muslims, there is no prayer for one who does not straighten his back in bowing and prostration. Abu Ja'far said: So these wordings in which this hadith was narrated agreed with the wordings in which Shu'bah narrated his hadith from al-A'mash, which we mentioned in the first section of this chapter. So what this hadith bears is like what we mentioned of what this hadith of Shu'bah bears. And we found the people of knowledge differing about one who falls from his bowing to his prostration in his prayer without raising his back from them: a group of them say his prayer has sufficed him, with the wrongdoing from him therein and his wasting his own share in seeking the highest ranks thereby, and the highest reward of one who brings it otherwise upon bringing it thus. And among those who said that of them are Abu Hanifah and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan. And a group of them say his prayer does not suffice him, and he must repeat it, and among those who said that of them is Abu Yusuf. So we looked in that to establish the worthier of what they said thereof, and what analogy necessitates therein of these two sayings. And the pillars upon which prayer is built include bowing, one of its pillars, and prostration, the highest of its pillars. And we found each of them has dhikr in it and no recitation. Then we found one who raises his head from his prostration in his prayer returns to a sitting not of the core of his prayer - meaning thereby the first sitting thereof, for it is agreed it is so, and whoever forgetfully omits it does not have his prayer nullified thereby. And the last sitting thereof is differed upon: of the scholars, some make it likewise, and some make it otherwise, making it of the core of prayer which does not suffice without it. So we adduced the agreed-upon sitting, leaving adducing the differed-upon sitting. And since the sitting one exits from prostration to, which we mentioned, is of the sunnahs of prayer not of its core, likewise the standing one exits from bowing to is of the sunnahs of prayer not of its core. So thereby is established the saying of whoever said that when he omits it in his prayer, his prayer is not corrupted thereby, and from Allah we ask guidance."
"4480","609 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what is decided among the people of knowledge in what they differed about: marrying an Arab to another's slavegirl with her master's permission, whether Arab or non-Arab, and she bears him a child - is her child a slave to her master or not? Abu Ja'far said: We know no one - of the people of knowledge around whom fatwa revolves in all the lands of Islam, of Hijaz, Iraq, and besides them of the lands of the people of Islam - differing about a slavegirl an Arab marries and begets a child, that he is owned to her master as she is owned to her master - except Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Awza'i, who used to say: Her master does not own him, but he is free, and upon his father is his value to his mother's master. So we looked at what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what falls under this meaning. 3902 - So we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Ayyash, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says in the morning: There is no god but Allah alone, no partner for Him; His is dominion and His is praise, and He is over all things capable - there are written for him ten good deeds, ten evil deeds are expiated from him, it is for him equal to a neck of the children of Ismail, he is in protection from Satan until evening; and when he says it in the evening, the like of that."
"4481","3903 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Suhayl, then he mentioned with his isnad something similar."
"4482","3904 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated it to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Ibn Abi Ayyash - thus he said - from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar."
"4483","3905 - And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated it to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Ya'qub al-Juzajani informed me, he said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Ayyash al-Zuraqi - Abu Abd al-Rahman, namely Zayd ibn al-Nu'man, said - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned something similar, with addition: So a man saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning in a dream - and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Ayyash narrates from you such-and-such. So he said: Abu Ayyash spoke truth."
"4484","3906 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Amir al-Sha'bi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever says: There is no god but Allah alone, no partner for Him; His is dominion and His is praise, and He is over all things capable, ten times - there is written for him equal to ten freedmen of the children of Ismail (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - or he said: equal to a freedman."
"4485","3907 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Khalaf al-Ammi narrated to us, he said: Yazid al-Raqashi narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That I sit with a people remembering Allah Almighty from the Fajr prayer until the sun rises is dearer to me than what the sun rises upon; and that I sit with a people remembering Allah Almighty from the Asr prayer to Maghrib is dearer to me than freeing eight, all of the children of Ismail (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4486","3908 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Musa ibn Khalaf, from Qatadah, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar."
"4487","3909 - Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Abu al-Ja'd narrating from Abu Umamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays the dawn prayer, then sits remembering Allah Almighty until the sun rises, shall have as one who freed four of the children of Ismail."
"4488","3910 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Uthman al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Junadah ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu Shaybah, he said: We said to Amr ibn Abasah: Narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith with no error or forgetfulness. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever shoots an arrow in the path of Allah, missing or hitting, is as freeing a soul of the children of Ismail."
"4489","3911 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayth ibn Abdillah ibn Zubayb ibn Tha'labah narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever owes freeing a neck of the children of Ismail, let him free a soul of Bal'anbar."
"4490","3912 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Ubayd ibn Hasan, from Ibn Ma'qil, he said: Aishah owed a freedman of the children of Ismail; then captives of Khawlan came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not free of these, and free of the captives of Bal'anbar and Banu Lihyan."
"4491","3913 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ubayd Abu al-Hasan, from Ibn Ma'qil - thus in Wahb's hadith - and in Abu Dawud's hadith he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, then he mentioned something similar."
"4492","3914 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ubaydillah al-Fuda'i narrated to us, he said: Maslamah ibn Alqamah narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Three I heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I shall ever love Banu Tamim since I heard them: Livestock of Banu Sa'd came, and he said: These are the livestock of my people. He said: And Aishah owed a freedman of the children of Ismail, then the captives of Bal'anbar came, and he said: If it pleases you to free of the children of Ismail, free of these. And he said the third: They are the sternest of people in the great battles."
"4493","3915 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Abis narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Abdullah, he said: Aishah owed a freedman of the children of Ismail, then the captives of Bal'anbar came, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her to free of them, and said: Whoever owes a neck of the children of Ismail (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), let him not free anyone of Himyar. Asbagh said: Ali ibn Abis said: I said to Ibn Abi Khalid: What is the matter of Himyar? He said: He is greater than Ismail. Abu Ja'far said: So in these reports we narrated is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirmation of ownership occurring over Arabs, as it occurs over others besides them not of the Arabs. And in that is what indicated the correctness of the generality's sayings in what we mentioned, and the corruption of what al-Awza'i said therein. And analogy necessitates that; for the Arab's child from another's slavegirl must either be owned to her master as a non-Arab's child is owned, or not owned to him for his Arabness, so be as the rest of the free besides him. And with that it is impossible that the value of what is not owned be due upon anyone. And in obliging him the value upon his father is what indicated the occurrence of what is due upon him. And in the occurrence of his ownership upon him is what indicated that his ownership does not leave him except by what ownerships leave others of the owned, and from Allah we ask guidance."
"4494","610 - Chapter explaining the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about idolatrous captives: the lawfulness of intercourse with them for Muslims, and evidence of abrogation thereof. 3916 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us. 3917 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ubaydillah ibn Abd al-Majid al-Hanafi narrated to us, then they concurred, each one of them saying: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to me, he said: Iyas ibn Salamah narrated to me, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, over us, and we raided Fazarah. When we drew near the water, Abu Bakr ordered us and we rested, and he led us in dawn prayer. Then he ordered us and we launched the raid, reaching the water, killing whom we killed there. Then a band of people turned away, with captives and offspring, nearly outstripping to the mountain. So I shot an arrow between them and the mountain, hastened, and they stood until I came between them and the mountain. And I brought them driving them, among them a woman of Banu Fazarah wearing a leather garment, with a daughter of hers among the most beautiful of Arabs. So I drove them to Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr granted me her daughter as spoils. Then I did not uncover a garment of hers until I arrived at Madinah, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met me and said to me: O Salamah, grant me the woman. I said: O Prophet of Allah, by Allah, she pleased me, and I have not uncovered a garment of hers. So he was silent until the morrow, when he met me and said to me: O Salamah, grant me the woman, may your father be for Allah. So I said: By Allah, I have not uncovered a garment of hers; she is yours, O Messenger of Allah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent her to Makkah, ransoming thereby Muslim captives in the hands of the polytheists. So in this hadith is Salamah's saying to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he asked her of him: By Allah, she pleased me, and I have not uncovered a garment of hers; and the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not denying that of him. So in that is what indicated that intercourse with her was then lawful to him. And in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ransoming with her and returning her to the polytheists is what indicated her continuance upon what she was upon, and that there was from her no Islam by which intercourse with her became lawful to Salamah."
"4495","3918 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Yaman al-Hakam ibn Nafi' al-Bahrani narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhayriz al-Jumahi narrated to me that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri informed him that while he was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man of the Ansar came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, we take captives and love wealth; so what do you see about withdrawal? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you indeed do that? It is no harm upon you not to do that, for there is no soul Allah Almighty decreed to emerge but it shall emerge. So in this hadith is what also indicated the permissibility of intercourse with captives, and they then took captive none but idolaters."
"4496","3919 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik narrated to him from Rabi'ah ibn Abi Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban: that Ibn Muhayriz narrated to him that Abu Sa'id narrated to him, that some people spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about coitus interruptus (al-'azl), and that was concerning the campaign of Banu al-Mustaliq, when they took female captives and disliked that they bear children from them, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no harm upon you if you do not practice 'azl, for Allah the Mighty and Majestic has decreed what He will create until the Day of Resurrection."
"4497","3920 - Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: And Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, he said: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban narrated to me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"4498","3921 - And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in this hadith those female captives were from Banu al-Mustaliq, and in that is what indicated that intercourse with them was lawful at that time."
"4499","3922 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Ibn Muhayriz, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that they took captives on the day of Awtas and wanted to enjoy them without them becoming pregnant, so they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he said: There is no harm upon you if you do not do it, for Allah the Mighty and Majestic has written who He will create until the Day of Resurrection. So Musa ibn Uqbah differed in this hadith from Rabi'ah and Abu al-Zinad, for he mentioned in it that those captives were from the captives of Awtas, while Rabi'ah and Abu al-Zinad said in it: they were from Banu al-Mustaliq, and that is a severe discrepancy; for the campaign of Banu al-Mustaliq was in year six of the Hijrah, and the campaign of Awtas, which is the campaign of Hunayn, was two years after it, in year eight of the Hijrah. So we examined the reality of that from the narration of others besides them, what it is."
"4500","3923 - So we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq al-Sabi'i, he said: I heard Abu al-Waddak narrating from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When we took the captives of Hunayn we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about 'azl, and he said: Not from all fluid is the child, and when Allah the Mighty and Majestic wills to create a thing, nothing prevents Him."
"4501","3924 - And we found Bakkar had narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id, he said: We took women on the day of Hunayn and we practiced 'azl with them, wanting ransom, so we said: If only we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. So Abu al-Waddak in this hadith agreed with what Musa ibn Uqbah narrated from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban from Ibn Muhayriz, and contradicted what Rabi'ah and Abu al-Zinad narrated. Then an objector said: These are sound reports, so from where did you turn away from them and abandon the permissibility of intercourse with idolatrous captive women? So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that it is possible that what is in these reports was before Allah sent down upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the prohibition of idolatrous women to the believers by His saying: And do not marry the idolatrous women until they believe, and a believing slave woman is better than an idolatrous woman. Then he said: And were the idolatrous women before the revelation of this verse lawful to the believers despite their worship of idols? So our answer to him in that is that they were indeed so at the beginning of Islam, and that was only prohibited in the year of al-Hudaybiyah, after the coming of Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt and whoever came besides her of the believing women to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4502","3925 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi informed us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, in the hadith of al-Hudaybiyah, he said: Then believing women came, so Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down: When the believing women come to you, until He reached: And do not hold to the ties of the disbelieving women. So Umar on that day divorced two wives of his who were his in idolatry, then one of them was married by Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan and the other by Safwan ibn Umayyah."
"4503","3926 - As Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn A'yan narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, (ha) and as Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So in this hadith is the continuation of Umar's (may Allah be pleased with him) marriage, despite the precedence of his Islam and Hijrah, with these two idolatrous polytheist women, until Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down concerning them and their likes what He sent down, with which the continuation of their marriage with him was no longer sound. So that indicated that marriage to their likes had been lawful for the Muslims until Allah the Mighty and Majestic prohibited that to them. Like that is what was concerning the captive idolatrous women: when they became slave women, intercourse with them was lawful before Allah the Mighty and Majestic prohibited marriage to idolatrous women, then marriage to idolatrous women was prohibited by what we mentioned, so they too were prohibited thereby. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down upon His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after that what He informed him by of who is lawful for him and for his community of the disbelieving women, and it is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: This day the good things are made lawful for you, and the food of those given the Book is lawful for you, and your food is lawful for them, and the chaste women of the believers and the chaste women of those given the Book before you. So He, Mighty and Majestic, informed him who He made lawful for him and for his community of the disbelieving women, and those besides them remained upon the prohibition of those He prohibited to him and to them of the idolatrous women in the verse we recited concerning that. And Allah we ask for granting of success."
"4504","611 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what decides between his Companions who differed about what is meant by His saying: And the married women except those your right hands possess. 3927 - Abu Shurayh Muhammad ibn Zakariya and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Uthman al-Batti, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We took women on the day of Awtas and they had husbands, so we disliked falling upon them, so we asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then this verse came down: And the married women except those your right hands possess, so we deemed them lawful."
"4505","3928 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Taymi or al-Batti, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: This verse came down concerning the captives of Awtas: And the married women except those your right hands possess, the decree of Allah upon you. Abu Ja'far said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had differed about the married women meant by what is mentioned in this verse, who they are. So it was narrated from Ali and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both) concerning that."
"4506","What Abu Shurayh and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Ali and Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both) concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And the married women except those your right hands possess. Ali said: The idolatrous women, when taken captive, become lawful thereby. And Ibn Mas'ud said: The idolatrous and the Muslim women. Abu Ja'far said: So the interpretation of this verse according to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was upon the married captive women owned by captivity, and according to Ibn Mas'ud it was upon those upon whom ownership newly occurred of the slave women, by captivity and by other than it. And for that reason he used to say: The sale of the slave woman is her divorce. And more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) followed him upon that. And we shall mention that later in this book of ours in a place more fitting for it than this place, if Allah wills. And Abdullah ibn Abbas opposed them both in what they interpreted this verse upon, so he interpreted it contrary to it."
"4507","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Isra'il, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And the married women except those your right hands possess, the decree of Allah upon you. He said: It is not lawful for a Muslim to marry more than four; if he does, then she is upon him like his mother and his sister. So the married women according to Ibn Abbas meant in this verse were the four who are lawful for a man to the exclusion of others besides them, except that there has been narrated from him concerning its interpretation what contradicts that from an aspect other than this aspect."
"4508","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Al-Hajjaj informed us, from Atiyyah ibn Sa'd, from Ibn Abbas: And the married women. He said: They are those with husbands. Abu Ja'far said: So it is possible that by this saying he agrees with Ali or agrees with Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both). And in the hadith of Abu Sa'id which we narrated in this chapter, in his informing of the cause for which this verse came down, is what confirmed in its interpretation what Ali interpreted it upon. Then an objector said: How did you confirm by this hadith of Abu Sa'id what you confirmed of the interpretation of this verse, when it is a hadith of corrupt isnad? And he mentioned concerning that."
"4509","3929 - What Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Uthman al-Batti narrated to us, he said: Salih Abu al-Khalil narrated to me that a man narrated to him, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Concerning us came down: And the married women except those your right hands possess. He said: We took captive women, among them women with husbands, so one of us would dislike intercourse with a woman because of her husband, then this verse came down, that captivity separated them from their husbands: And the married women except those your right hands possess. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that this hadith is not of corrupt isnad as he mentioned; rather Salih did not name to al-Batti the man between him and Abu Sa'id in this hadith, but he did name him to Qatadah in it."
"4510","3930 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la al-San'ani informed us, he said: Yazid, and he is Ibn Zuray', narrated to us, he said: Sa'id, and he is Ibn Abi Arubah, narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Khalil, from Abu Alqamah al-Hashimi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an army to Awtas, so they met an enemy and fought them and prevailed over them, and took from them captives with husbands among the polytheists. So the Muslims were scrupulous about approaching them, so Allah, Blessed and Exalted, sent down: And the married women except those your right hands possess, i.e.: they are lawful for you when their waiting periods have passed. So we understood thereby that the man left unnamed in the hadith of al-Batti is Abu Alqamah al-Hashimi. Then an objector said: And is this Abu Alqamah among the well-known in knowledge, from whom the like of this is taken? So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that this Abu Alqamah is a man of eminent standing in knowledge; he narrated from more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Uthman ibn Affan."
"4511","3931 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Ali informed us, he said: Yahya, meaning al-Qattan, narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr narrated to me, from Abu Alqamah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: I saw Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) perform ablution, and with him were people of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he washed his palms and his hands three times and his face three times, and wiped his head, and washed his feet until he cleaned them. Then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution with this ablution."
"4512","3932 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. And among them are Abdullah ibn Mas'ud and Abu Hurayrah."
"4513","3933 - As Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Harith informed me, he said: A man called Abu Alqamah, an ally among Banu Hashim, arrived, so I and Ali al-Azdi followed him. So among what he narrated to us was that he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among the signs of the Hour is that obscenity and miserliness appear, and the traitor is trusted and the trustworthy is deemed treacherous, and garments like the throngs of magic appear, worn by women clothed yet naked, and the lowly rise above the noble. Is it so, O Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, did you hear it from my beloved, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Ka'bah. I said: And what are the lowly and the noble? He said: The dregs of men and people of obscure houses are raised above their righteous ones and people of righteous houses. So we came to know thereby the eminence of the standing of this Abu Alqamah, and that he is among the eminent Followers, and that there narrated from him among the people of knowledge Salih Abu al-Khalil and Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr. And Ya'la ibn Ata also narrated from him."
"4514","3934 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn Ata informed us, he said: I heard Abu Alqamah narrating from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to seek refuge from five, saying: I seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave, and from the punishment of Hell, and from the trial of life and death, and the evil of the False Messiah, the Dajjal. And Zuhrah ibn Ma'bad also narrated from him."
"4515","As Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, from Zuhrah ibn Ma'bad, that he said: I heard Abu Alqamah, freedman of Banu Hashim, countless times saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah countless times saying: Whoever says after dawn: Glory be to Allah the Mighty and with His praise, one hundred times, and: There is no god but Allah alone, no partner for Him, one hundred times, and after Asr the like of that, his sins are forgiven even if they are like the foam of the sea."
"4516","Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth informed me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, and neither of them raised it. Then an objector said: Who is Muhammad ibn al-Harith who narrated the hadith of Abu Alqamah before this? So our answer to him in that is that he is Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Sufyan; thus says Yusuf ibn Sa'id ibn Muslim, from Hajjaj. So this hadith was thereby established for us, and it became permissible for us to argue with it against whoever opposed it. And we understood that Abu Alqamah is in the standing we mentioned him in, and he had moved to the region of the Maghrib and held the judgeship of Ifriqiyah in the nights of the Umayyads. And in this hadith was that the women concerning whom this verse came down are the women who were taken captive without their husbands. As for those taken captive along with their husbands, then according to us they do not separate from them by captivity; thus Abu Hanifah and all his companions used to say concerning that. Rather they separate from their husbands by the separation of abode between them and the divergence of their rulings; but if they are equal in that, then no. And the proof for what they said of that is that if they came out to us with aman they would remain upon their marriage, and if they came out to us with dhimmah, renouncing the people of their abode while holding to their religions, they would remain upon their marriage, even if we owned them by our hands falling upon them thereby. And if one of the two came to us thus and left the other behind in the abode of war, the marriage between them is cut by that. So captivity of both of them or of one of them is likewise in ruling. And a questioner asked and said: Is there upon the captive women with husbands, when taken captive without their husbands so separation falls between them and their husbands, any waiting period, as in the hadith of Abu Alqamah from Abu Sa'id which you narrated? So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that there is no waiting period upon them; rather upon their owners is to clear their wombs, upon what we have narrated in what preceded from us in this book of ours, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the captives: A pregnant woman is not to be approached until she gives birth, nor a non-pregnant one until she menstruates. And among them are those with husbands and others. And the scholars received that with acceptance, so they said by it and did not differ in it. And what is in this hadith of mention of the passing of waiting periods may possibly be from the saying of one of its narrators, so what the scholars agreed upon is worthier than that. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4517","612 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what is meant by the saying of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj of the House or Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate them. 3935 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah said: I asked Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) and said: What do you think of the saying of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj of the House or Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate them. So I said: By Allah, there is no blame upon anyone if he does not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah. Aishah said: Evil is what you said, O son of my sister. If this verse were upon what you interpreted it upon, it would have been: there is no blame upon him if he does not circumambulate them. Rather it was sent down concerning the Ansar: before they embraced Islam they used to enter ihram for Manat, the false goddess they worshipped at al-Mushallal, and whoever entered ihram for her was scrupulous about circumambulating between al-Safa and al-Marwah. So when they asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj of the House or Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate them. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instituted as sunnah the tawaf between them, so no one may abandon tawaf between them. Ibn Shihab said: So I informed Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham of what Urwah narrated to me of that from Aishah, so Abu Bakr said: This knowledge I had not heard, and I had heard men of knowledge claim that the people, except those Aishah mentioned of those who entered ihram for Manat the false goddess, all used to circumambulate al-Safa and al-Marwah. So when Allah the Mighty and Majestic mentioned tawaf of the House and did not mention tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah, they said: Is there upon us, O Messenger of Allah, any restriction in circumambulating al-Safa and al-Marwah? So Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj of the House or Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate them. Abu Bakr said: So I hear this verse was sent down concerning both groups: those who were scrupulous in Jahiliyyah about circumambulating al-Safa and al-Marwah, and those who used to circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah in Jahiliyyah, then became scrupulous about circumambulating them in Islam because Allah the Mighty and Majestic commanded tawaf of the House and did not mention al-Safa and al-Marwah along with tawaf of the House when He mentioned it."
"4518","3936 - Fahd and Harun together narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, he said: Ibn Shihab said, then he mentioned the like of it with his isnad."
"4519","3937 - And Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned the like of it with his isnad."
"4520","3938 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah: that Manat was upon the seashore, and around it were dung and blood with which the polytheists slaughtered. So the Ansar said: O Messenger of Allah, when we entered ihram in Jahiliyyah, it was not lawful for us in our religion to circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah. So Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj of the House or Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate them. Urwah said: As for me, I do not care if I do not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah. Aishah said: Why, O son of my sister? He said: Because Allah the Mighty and Majestic says: There is no blame upon him to circumambulate them. Aishah said: If it were as you say, it would have been: there is no blame upon him if he does not circumambulate them. Aishah said: And neither the Hajj nor the Umrah of anyone who did not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah is complete. Abu Ja'far said: So in these reports is that the cause for which this verse came down is the scrupulousness of the Ansar about tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah for the cause mentioned in this hadith, and that Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down this verse and thereby informed them there is no blame upon them in tawaf between them, and informed them therein that they are among the rites of Allah the Mighty and Majestic. And He mentioned His rites elsewhere, His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And whoever honors the rites of Allah, then it is from the piety of hearts. And in the hadith of Hisham from Urwah from Aishah was her saying: By my life, neither the Hajj nor the Umrah of anyone who did not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah is complete. And the like of this is not said by opinion, so we understood thereby that she only said it as tawqif, and tawqif is only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then an objector said: As for what you related from Aishah of her saying to Urwah: If it were as you say it would have been: there is no blame upon him if he does not circumambulate them, yet Abdullah ibn Abbas used to recite it thus."
"4521","And he mentioned what Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to recite: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj of the House or Umrah, there is no blame upon him if he does not circumambulate them. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that what is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas of recitation may possibly have a meaning returning to what is in the hadith of Aishah of it. And His saying, Mighty and Majestic, that he does not circumambulate them in the recitation of Ibn Abbas would be upon superfluity (silah), as He, Mighty and Majestic, said: So that the People of the Book may not know that they have no power over anything, meaning: so that the People of the Book may know that they have no power over anything; and as He, Mighty and Majestic, said: And unlawful upon a town We destroyed that they shall not return, meaning that they shall return; and as His saying, Mighty and Majestic: What prevented you from not prostrating, meaning: what prevented you from prostrating. So likewise, if the recitation is as narrated from Ibn Abbas, therein that he does not circumambulate them would mean that he circumambulates them, upon what is in the recitation of others, and it is the recitation established by proof, contained in our codices. And there has been narrated from Anas ibn Malik concerning the recitation of this letter the like of what was narrated therein from Aishah."
"4522","3939 - As Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, (ha) and as Abu Shurayh and Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, they said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, he said: I asked Anas ibn Malik about al-Safa and al-Marwah, he said: They were among the rites of Jahiliyyah, so when Islam came we held back from them, then Allah the Mighty and Majestic sent down: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj of the House or Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate them. And they are voluntary."
"4523","3940 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Arim narrated to us, he said: Thabit Abu Zayd narrated to us, he said: Asim narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it."
"4524","3941 - And as Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Sulayman narrated to us. He said: I said to Anas ibn Malik: Did you dislike tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah until there came down: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah? He said: Yes, they were among the rites of Jahiliyyah, so we disliked tawaf between them until this verse came down. And what is in the hadith of Anas of mention of tawaf between them as voluntary is of what he did not mention from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so it may be an opinion he held. And Aishah opposed him in that, for she narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he instituted as sunnah tawaf between them in Hajj and Umrah together, and she herself said: Neither the Hajj nor the Umrah of anyone who did not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah is complete. So that was with us worthier than the saying of Anas, especially as the jurists of the lands are upon it without differing in it, and they did not say that, elder from elder, except by what made it obligatory for them to say it thereby. And what opposed what they are upon of that is of what has no meaning and is not sound to say. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4525","613 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions did not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah after entering ihram for Hajj, other than their tawaf which they had performed upon being in Hajj, then they converted it to Umrah and exited it, except whoever among them had the sacrificial animal with him. 3942 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: Neither the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor his Companions circumambulated between al-Safa and al-Marwah except one tawaf, their first tawaf."
"4526","3943 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Jurayj, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both): that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not add to one tawaf for their Hajj and their Umrah between al-Safa and al-Marwah; they did not circumambulate between them after returning from Arafat."
"4527","3944 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Rabah ibn Abi Ma'ruf narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: that the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not add to one tawaf."
"4528","3945 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering ihram for Hajj, with us were the women and children. So when we arrived at Makkah we circumambulated the House and al-Safa and al-Marwah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him exit ihram. We said: Which exiting? He said: The complete exiting. So we approached the women and wore clothes and touched perfume. Then when it was the day of al-Tarwiyah we entered ihram for Hajj, and the first tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah sufficed us. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith of Jabir is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions did not circumambulate between al-Safa and al-Marwah for their Hajj because of their tawaf between them which had been from them before what they had been in of Hajj, into which they had been entered, turned into the Umrah to which their ihram turned, and that they sufficed with their tawaf which had been between al-Safa and al-Marwah in what they had first been upon of Hajj until it turned into what it turned into of Umrah. And this is of what we did not stand upon its meaning; for the first tawaf which had been from them between al-Safa and al-Marwah is of what is indispensable in Hajj in the saying of all the people of knowledge, and tawaf between them before entering Hajj does not suffice for it. And in this hadith of Jabir is what contradicted that. And when his hadith became difficult for us, we sought whether what contradicts it was narrated or not. So we found in the hadith of Urwah from Aishah which we mentioned in what preceded from us in this book of ours, and it is her hadith which Malik narrated from Ibn Shihab from Urwah from her, of her saying: So those who entered ihram for Umrah circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, then they exited, then they circumambulated another tawaf after returning from Mina for their Hajj. So her saying: then they circumambulated another tawaf after returning from Mina for their Hajj is upon a tawaf like the first tawaf which they had performed for Umrah, and in it is tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah. So that contradicted the hadith which Jabir narrated concerning that, and it was worthier than it; for Allah said in His Book: Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are among the rites of Allah, so whoever performs Hajj of the House or Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate them. Then when we narrated from Aishah in the chapter in which we narrated that, in what preceded from us in this book of ours, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) instituted as sunnah tawaf between them, and from her saying after that that neither the Hajj nor the Umrah of anyone who did not circumambulate between them is complete, and that is of what is not permissible to have been said by her as opinion; for the like of it is not said by opinion, but she said it as tawqif, and tawqif in the like of this is only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Allah we ask for granting of success."
"4529","614 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever sells an old possession, Allah sets a destroyer upon him. 3946 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Quddus ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Kabir ibn Shu'ayb ibn al-Habhab narrated to me, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan, and he is al-Allaf, narrated to me, he said: Bishr ibn Shurayh narrated to me - thus it is in our book, but it is Ibn Surayj - he said: Qabisah ibn al-Ja'd al-Sulami narrated to me, he said: Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali narrated to me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Ya'la, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no servant who sells an old possession except that Allah sets a destroyer upon him. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined this hadith and found that talid among the Arabs is the old. So its meaning with us, and Allah knows best, is upon one whom Allah the Mighty and Majestic favored with a thing whose stay with him lengthened, so it thereby became a blessing from Allah the Mighty and Majestic upon him, then by selling what Allah the Mighty and Majestic favored him with of that, he exchanges what is contrary to that. So Allah the Mighty and Majestic sets upon him as punishment for him a destroyer of what he exchanged it for. And the meaning of talif is: destroying, as they say halik meaning destroyer; al-Ajjaj said, meaning destroyer: whoever turns aside. And like that is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Whoever sells a house or real estate, then does not place its price in its like - and in part of the hadith: or from its price in its like - it is not blessed for him therein."
"4530","3947 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Hudhayfah, from Hudhayfah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sells a house or real estate, then does not place its price, or part of its price, in its like, it is not blessed for him therein."
"4531","3948 - And Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Sulayman al-Sarazi narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Malik al-Nakha'i, from Yusuf ibn Maymun, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Hudhayfah, from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sells a house and does not place its price in its like, it is not blessed for him in its price - or he said: its price is not blessed for him."
"4532","3949 - And as Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Amr ibn Hurayth, from Sa'id ibn Hurayth: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever sells a house or real estate, then does not place its price in its like, it is not blessed for him therein. Of what Ibn Uyaynah derived therein is that he found Allah the Mighty and Majestic saying: And He blessed therein and measured therein its sustenance, meaning the earth. So whoever sells a house or real estate has sold what Allah the Mighty and Majestic blessed therein, so He punished him by making what he exchanged it for - meaning of other than it of houses and buildings - unblessed for him therein. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4533","615 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the dung of cattle whose meat is eaten, that it does not defile what it touches of clothes, and that prayer in clothes it touched is permissible. 3950 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Uthman ibn Hakim al-Awdi informed us, he said: Khalid, meaning Ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani, narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Salih ibn Hayy narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, he said: Abdullah narrated to me in the treasury, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying by the House while a group of Quraysh were sitting, and they had slaughtered a camel. So one of them said: Which of you takes this dung with its blood, then waits until he places his face prostrating, then places it upon - meaning his back. Abdullah said: So the most wretched of them hastened and took the dung and went with it, then waited. So when he fell prostrate he placed it upon his back. So Fatimah bint Rasulillah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed, while she was a young girl, so she came hurrying and took it from his back. So when he finished his prayer he said: O Allah, upon You is Quraysh - three times - O Allah, upon You is Abu Jahl ibn Hisham, and Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah, and Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, and Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt, until he counted seven of Quraysh. Abdullah said: So by the One who sent down the Book, I saw them all on the day of Badr in one well. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith the wretch mentioned therein placed upon the back of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while prostrating the dung and blood which he placed upon him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) continued thereafter upon his prayer until he completed it. Then some objectors said: So in this is proof that the dung of what is eaten of meat does not invalidate prayer with the garment it touched, nor by touching bodies, and that it is unlike the impurities of shed bloods of cattle and of other than them, and unlike the dung of what is not eaten of meat of donkeys and mules, and unlike the excrement of the sons of Adam and their urine. And they attached in that to what was narrated from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud of his doing that himself after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4534","And it is what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur and Khalid, from Ibn Sirin, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar: that Ibn Mas'ud prayed while upon his belly was dung and blood, and did not repeat the prayer. Abu Ja'far said: And this school has been held by more than one of the people of knowledge, among them Malik and al-Thawri and al-Hasan ibn Salih and Zufar ibn al-Hudhayl. And they have therein opponents among the people of knowledge, and they are Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan; they used to say: It is impure, and it is in the ruling of the bloods of the things it is from. And of what those holding their saying argued against the people of the first statement, in what they argued for their saying of that, from the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud which we described, is that that hadith of Ibn Mas'ud was only narrated - as they mentioned - by Ali ibn Salih from Abu Ishaq, while Zakariya ibn Abi Za'idah and Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj opposed him, so they narrated it from Abu Ishaq contrary to that."
"4535","3951 - As al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Zakariya ibn Abi Za'idah, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying by the House, and Abu Jahl and companions of his were sitting by the House, and they had slaughtered a camel the day before, Abu Jahl said: Which of you goes to the caul of the camel of Banu so-and-so and takes it and places it upon the shoulders of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prostrates? So the most wretched of the people hastened and took it. So when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated, he placed it between his shoulders. So they laughed and began facing one another, while I stood looking; had I protection I would have thrown it from the back of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained prostrating, not raising his head, until a man went and informed Fatimah. So she came while a young girl and threw it from him. Then she turned to them reviling them. So when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed his prayer, he raised his voice, then supplicated against them - and when he supplicated he supplicated three times, and when he asked he asked three times - then he said: O Allah, upon You is Quraysh, three times. So when they heard his voice, laughter left them and they feared his supplication. Then he said: O Allah, upon You is Abu Jahl ibn Hisham, and Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, and Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah, and al-Walid ibn Utbah, and Umayyah ibn Khalaf, and Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt. And he mentioned the seventh but I did not retain it. By the One who sent Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the truth, I saw those he named fallen on the day of Badr, then dragged into the well, the well of Badr."
"4536","3952 - And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: I heard Amr ibn Maymun narrating from Abdullah, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was praying, and Quraysh were sitting, and the caul of a camel was near him, when he prostrated they said: Who takes this caul and throws it upon his back? As if they feared him. So Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt said: I. So he stood and threw it upon his back while he was prostrating. So he remained prostrating until Fatimah, peace be upon her, came while a young girl and threw it from his back. Abdullah said: I never heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicate against Quraysh except that day. He said: O Allah, upon You is the chiefs of Quraysh. O Allah, upon You is Abu Jahl ibn Hisham. O Allah, upon You is Utbah ibn Rabi'ah. O Allah, upon You is Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah. O Allah, upon You is Uqbah ibn Abi Mu'ayt. O Allah, upon You is Umayyah ibn Khalaf. Abdullah said: I saw them turned over on the day of Badr all together, then dragged until thrown into the well, except Abu Jahl or Umayyah, for he was a corpulent man so he broke apart."
"4537","3953 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. They said: So in the two hadiths of Zakariya and Shu'bah was that what that wretch placed upon the back of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while praying was the caul of a slaughtered she-camel, and it is that in which is what it carried, of what has in it no blood nor dung, and of what is like the rest of its meat. And there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that one in whose sleeve is meat of a slaughtered she-camel with no blood nor dung in it, who prays carrying it thus, that his prayer is permissible. And when this disagreement in this hadith is as we mentioned, that what two narrated is worthier of soundness than what one narrated, even if all its narrators are upright imams, memorizers, firm. And if the two narrations are made equal, neither of them is worthier than the other, and when they contradicted they cancel, and what therein of this disagreement of the dung of things eaten of meat becomes as with no hadith therein. As for what was narrated therein from Ibn Mas'ud of the hadith of Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, it may be that it was not of the amount by which prayer is invalidated, since little blood therein is unlike much in the view of many of the people of knowledge among those holding the second of the two statements we mentioned. Then we returned to seeking the first of these two sayings by sound reasoning, referred to the like of it when the ruling of disputed things is not found in the Book, the Sunnah, and the consensus of the community. So we found the agreed-upon origin that the bloods of cattle eaten of meat are impure, and their falling into waters spoils them, and if they touch clothes they defile them, like the bloods of the sons of Adam in that. And eating the meats of what they return to was not made to the ruling of their meats, but made returning to the ruling of their bloods. So reasoning upon that is that likewise their dung: its purity is not obligatory by the purity of their meats, and their dung is like their bloods and like the excrement of the sons of Adam and their bloods in its impurity. So this is the reasoning in this chapter. Then an objector said: The she-camel mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud which you mentioned was only slaughtered by the idolaters whose sacrifices are not lawful and with it there is no slaughter, so its caul is like the caul of a dead she-camel. So in this hadith is proof against them for whoever holds that one who prayed while in his garment is impurity, or in his body is impurity, or carrying impurity of carrion or other than it, that his prayer is permissible. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that that she-camel was indeed so, but that slaughter of it was at a time when the sacrifices of the idolaters were like the sacrifices of others besides them of the people of Islam, as the marriage of their women at the beginning of Islam was likewise. Then Allah after that prohibited the marriage of their women and eating their sacrifices by His saying, Mighty and Majestic: This day the good things are made lawful for you, and the food of those given the Book is lawful for you, and your food is lawful for them, and the chaste women of the believers and the chaste women of those given the Book before you. So in that was prohibition of what before that time had not been unlawful, then the prohibition we mentioned supervened in women and in sacrifices, so the matter in both returned to what proceeds upon it until the Day of Resurrection. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4538","616 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning those most entitled to imamah. 3954 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Isma'il ibn Raja al-Zubaydi, from Aws, and he is Ibn Dam'aj, he said: I heard Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people are led by the most reciting of them of the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic. If they are equal in recitation, then the most knowledgeable of them of the Sunnah. If they are equal in the Sunnah, then the earliest of them in Hijrah. If they are equal in Hijrah, then the earliest of them in age. And a man is not led in his authority, nor sat upon his honored seat in his house except by his permission."
"4539","3955 - And Musa ibn al-Hasan, known as al-Saqalli, narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr ibn al-Muhallab al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: Za'idah ibn Qudamah al-Quta'i narrated to us, from al-A'mash, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he did not say: nor is a man led. Abu Ja'far said: Thus al-A'mash narrated this hadith from Isma'il ibn Raja, while Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated from Isma'il contrary to that."
"4540","3956 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Raja, from Aws ibn Dam'aj, from Uqbah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people are led by the earliest of them in Hijrah. If they are equal in Hijrah, then the greatest of them in age. If they are equal in age, then the most reciting of them. And al-Mas'udi also narrated it from Isma'il contrary to that."
"4541","3957 - As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Raja narrated to us, from Aws ibn Dam'aj, from Abu Mas'ud al-Badri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people are led by the most reciting of them of the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic. If they are equal in recitation, then the earliest of them in Hijrah. If they are equal in Hijrah, then the greatest of them in age. And no commander is led in his house nor in his authority, and do not sit upon his honored seat until he permits you. And Shu'bah also narrated it from Isma'il contrary to that."
"4542","3958 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Raja, from Aws ibn Dam'aj, from Abu Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people are led by the most reciting of them of the Book of Allah and the earliest of them in recitation. If they are equal in recitation, then the earliest of them in Hijrah. If they are equal in Hijrah, then the greatest of them in age. And no commander is led in his command, nor among his family, and do not sit upon his honored seat except by his permission, or except that he permits you. And Fitr ibn Khalifah also narrated it from Isma'il contrary to that."
"4543","3959 - As Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Raja, from Aws ibn Dam'aj, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let the most reciting of you lead you. If recitation is one, then the earliest of you in Hijrah. If Hijrah is one, then the most knowledgeable of you of the Sunnah. If the Sunnah is one, then the earliest of you in age. And a man is not led in his house, nor sat upon his honored seat except by his permission. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined this hadith and the disagreement of its narrators therein from Isma'il, and found it turns upon four ranks, and they are: the most reciting of the people of the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, and the most knowledgeable of the people of the Sunnah, and the earliest of the people in Hijrah, and the greatest of the people in age. And the Qur'an by which some of them are more reciting than others is of what is indispensable in prayer and of what it is comprised with. And likewise what is taken from the Sunnah of what prayer stands only by it: prayer is comprised with it. So the other two ranks, and they are Hijrah and age, are not thus, and prayer is not comprised with them; for if a group attended, among them a man of the people of Hijrah and the rest not of its people, and they prayed without him, their prayer suffices them, though the better for them and worthier and more excellent is had they made him their imam therein. And likewise had a people attended for prayer and among them a man the eldest of them, and they prayed without him, their prayer is permissible, though the worthier for them and more excellent is had they put him forward and followed him. So the first two ranks are indispensable in prayer and of what it is comprised with, and the other two ranks are only employed therein as etiquette, not obligation, and prayer is not comprised with them. So the higher of the first two ranks is the Qur'an, and the higher of the other two ranks is Hijrah. So we inferred thereby that the worthiest of the people of the four ranks we mentioned for imamah in prayer are the people of the Qur'an, then the people of the Sunnah, then the people of Hijrah, then the people of age. And we did not find in the narration of any who narrated this hadith the placing of imamah among the people of these ranks thus, other than al-A'mash, for his narration of it is thus, so it was thereby the worthiest with us. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4544","617 - Chapter: What was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what decides between those differing concerning imamah in prayers over funerals: does it enter into the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): no commander is led in his command, or not? Abu Ja'far said: We narrated in the chapter before this chapter, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): no commander is led in his command. So Abu Hanifah and his companions entered imamah in prayers over funerals into that, while al-Shafi'i did not enter it into it. So we considered whether anything was narrated from those before them agreeing with one of these two sayings or not. 3960 - So we found Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Jahhaf. Abu Ja'far said: And he is Dawud ibn Abi Awf, from Isma'il ibn Raja, he said: One who witnessed al-Husayn ibn Ali when al-Hasan, peace be upon them both, died, informed me, he said to Sa'id ibn al-As: Advance, for were it not sunnah I would not have put you forward."
"4545","3961 - And we found Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus al-Basri had narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salim ibn Abi Hafsah, from Abu Hazim, he said: I was indeed witness on the day al-Hasan ibn Ali died, so I saw Husayn saying to Sa'id ibn al-As while prodding his neck: Advance; were it not sunnah I would not have put you forward. He said: So there was something between them, then Abu Hurayrah said: Do you envy the son of your Prophet a soil to bury him in? And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever loves them both has loved me, and whoever hates them both has hated me. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith was what indicated the entry of prayers over funerals into that. So analogy with us necessitated this saying. And al-Shafi'i, of what he argued for his saying we mentioned from him concerning that, is that this is of the special obligations. And his opponents therein used to say it is of the general obligations which fall from the generality by the standing of the particular among them with it; for upon the Muslims are prayers over their funerals as upon them is washing them, and as upon them is covering them in their graves. And whoever of them stands with that, the obligation thereby falls from the rest. And congregations for the five prayers in mosques were obligatory upon the Muslims, except that whoever of them stood with that, the obligation thereby fell from the rest. And congregation in the five prayers, had the commander attended it, imamah therein would be to him to the exclusion of others of the people. So likewise in analogy is congregation in prayers over funerals: when the commander attends it, imamah therein is to him to the exclusion of others of the people. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4546","618 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what was attached to concerning the imamah of boys not yet mature in the obligatory prayers. 3962 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar ibn Habib narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Salamah al-Jarmi narrated to us: that his father and a group of his people came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, who prays for us - or they said: who prays with us? He said: The most of you in taking of the Qur'an - or he said: gathering of the Qur'an. He said: So they arrived, and there was no one among the people who had taken of the Qur'an more than I had taken, so they put me forward while a boy, I praying with them with a shawl of mine upon me. Mis'ar said: So I found him praying with them and praying over their funerals, with no one disputing him in that."
"4547","3963 - Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani informed them, from Amr ibn Salamah al-Jarmi, he said: We were at a settlement past which people passed when they came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. And I was a memorizing boy, so I memorized much Qur'an from that. So my father came in a delegation of his people to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he taught them Islam and said: Let the most reciting of you lead you. So there was no one among the people more reciting than I, so I used to lead them while seven or eight years old, with a cloak of mine upon me. So when I prostrated I became uncovered. So a woman passed by us one day while I prayed with them, and said: Cover from us the private part of this reciter of yours. So they bought for me an Omani shirt, and I never rejoiced in anything after Islam as I rejoiced in that shirt. Hammad said: Ayyub said: So the first from whom I heard this hadith was Abu Qilabah."
"4548","3964 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Asim al-Ahwal and Mis'ar informed us, from Amr ibn Salamah, he said: When my people came in delegation to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said to them: Let the most of you in recitation of the Qur'an lead you. So they came and taught me bowing and prostration, so I used to pray with them with a torn cloak upon me. So they used to say to my father: Will you not cover from us the backside of your son? So in this hadith is the imamah of the boy mentioned therein with his people. So a group went - among them al-Shafi'i - to permitting the imamah of a boy not yet mature in prayer, when he understands it, of the five prayers, of mature men. And they argued in that with this hadith. And others opposed them in that, among them Abu Hanifah and his companions, so they did not permit the prayer of one upon whom that prayer is obligatory behind one upon whom it is not. And of the proof for them against the people of the first saying in this hadith is that that act of putting that boy forward and following him was not by the command of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that specifically; rather it was from the act of those who put him forward, of what entered upon the littleness of their knowledge of the rulings of prayer: their following one uncovered of private part therein, and that is of what the Shari'ah prevents. And it is not a proof because it was in the era of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not stand upon it and approve it. And this Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him): there was mentioned to him Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' al-Ansari - and he is a man of the eminent Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and of the captains of the Ansar, and of those who witnessed Badr - that they were in the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4549","3965 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Idris informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ma'mar ibn Abi Habibah, from Ubayd ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi', from his father, he said: I was sitting at the right of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) when a man came and said: O Commander of the Believers, this Zayd ibn Thabit gives people fatwa concerning ghusl from janabah by his opinion. So Umar said: Bring him to me quickly. So Zayd came, and Umar said: Has your affair reached that you give people fatwa concerning ghusl from janabah in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by your opinion? So Zayd said to him: By Allah, O Commander of the Believers, I did not give fatwa by my opinion, but I heard something from my paternal uncles and said by it. He said: From which of your uncles? He said: From Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and Abu Ayyub, and Rifa'ah ibn Rafi'. So Umar turned to me and said: What does this youth say? I said: We used to do it in the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then not bathe. He said: Did you ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that? I said: No. He said: Bring the people to me. So the people concurred: that water is only from water, except what was from Ali and Mu'adh, peace be upon them both, for they said: When the circumcised passes the circumcised, ghusl is obligatory. So the Commander of the Believers said: I find no one more knowledgeable of this of the affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than his wives. So he sent to Hafsah, and she said: I have no knowledge. So he sent to Aishah, and she said: When the circumcised passes the circumcised, ghusl is obligatory. So Umar was crushed, and said: If I am informed of one who does it then does not bathe, I shall punish him severely. So this Umar did not view what Rifa'ah narrated to him - of what they used to do in the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what they did not mention to him so he approves it from them - as proof. So when that of Rifa'ah, with the eminence of his standing and loftiness of his station in that, is thus, likewise the like of it in one who has not of support as his support, nor of companionship to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as his companionship, nor of witnessing Badr and other than it of the campaigns of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as he has, is worthier to be of what their act thereof fell short of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); likewise there is no proof therein. So thereby this hadith returned with no proof therein for any of the people of these two sayings against any of the people of the other saying. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4550","619 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he asked his Lord the Mighty and Majestic, then wished he had not asked Him it. 3966 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hajabi narrated to us, and Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rabi' al-Zahrani narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I asked my Lord the Mighty and Majestic a request, and wished I had not asked Him. I said: O Lord, there were prophets before me; among them one for whom You subjected the wind, then he mentioned Sulayman ibn Dawud, peace be upon him; and among them one who gave life to the dead, then he mentioned Isa son of Maryam, peace be upon him; and among them, and among them - mentioning what they were given. He said: Did I not find you an orphan and shelter? I said: Yes, O Lord. He said: Did I not find you astray and guide? I said: Yes, O Lord. He said: Did I not find you poor and enrich? I said: Yes, O Lord. He said: Did I not expand for you your breast, and remove from you your burden? I said: Yes, O Lord."
"4551","3967 - And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Hisham al-Tammar narrated to us, and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us. Muhammad ibn Ali said in his hadith: Hammad said: And I think it is from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn Abbas. And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim said in his hadith: Hammad said: And most of my thought is that it is from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn Abbas. Then he mentioned this hadith. So in this hadith is what indicates that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had asked his Lord to give him something distinguishing him from the prophets before him, blessings of Allah upon them, of the kind of what He gave those preceding him among them, by which He distinguished him from the rest of the prophets, blessings of Allah upon them, besides him. Among them Sulayman, peace be upon him: when he asked Him to give him a dominion befitting no one after him, so He subjected for him the wind running by his command gently wherever it struck, and the devils, every builder and diver, and others bound in fetters. And among them Isa son of Maryam, peace be upon him: He gave him to heal the blind and the leper by His permission, and to bring forth the dead by His permission. So of Allah the Mighty and Majestic was informing him that He had given him what is above that, of what was recounted in the hadith and of what was not recounted therein, of what is mentioned in the surah: Did We not expand for you, of what He addressed him with of His saying, Mighty and Majestic, to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): And We raised for you your remembrance, until He made him mentioned in the adhan by which is called to the prayers He obligated upon His creation and worshipped them with. And He did not give that to any preceding him of the prophets, blessings of Allah upon them - neither Sulayman nor Isa nor others besides them. And He made him along with that, of what was not mentioned in that surah nor in this hadith, mentioned in the prayers after His mentioning, Mighty and Majestic, therein, and prayed upon therein in the tashahhud of them. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wished, when Allah the Mighty and Majestic apprised him of that, that he had not asked Him what he asked Him to give him, of what He had given him what is above it and what is better than it. Then there was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we have encompassed in knowledge that he only said it after that."
"4552","3968 - What Fahd ibn Sulayman and Muhammad ibn Ja'far, known as Ibn al-Imam, narrated to us, they said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid Yazid al-Asadi narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah al-Suwa'i, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Alqamah al-Thaqafi, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Aqil, he said: I set out in a delegation to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so we came and made our camels kneel at the door, while there was no one among people more hateful to us than a man we would enter upon. Then we did not leave until there was no one among people more beloved to us than a man we entered upon. So one of us said: O Messenger of Allah, will you not ask your Lord for a dominion like the dominion of Sulayman? He said: So he laughed, then said: Perhaps for your companion with Allah the Mighty and Majestic is better than the dominion of Sulayman. Allah did not send a prophet except He gave him a supplication: among them one who took it as worldly and was given it, and among them one who supplicated with it against his people when they disobeyed, so they were destroyed by it. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic gave me a supplication, so I stored it with my Lord the Mighty and Majestic as intercession for my community on the Day of Resurrection. So we understood thereby that his station (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his Lord the Mighty and Majestic is above the station of Sulayman, peace be upon him. Then Allah the Mighty and Majestic increased him by sending him to all people and sending down upon him: Say: O people, I am the Messenger of Allah to you all. And no other of the prophets was sent except to his people, or to particular of people to the exclusion of the rest. And He, Mighty and Majestic, distinguished him by what He brought to us upon his tongue (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying."
"4553","3969 - What al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was given five not given to anyone before me: the earth was made for me a mosque and purification, and I was aided by dread, and spoils were made lawful for me, and I was sent to the red and the white, and I was given intercession. I heard al-Muzani saying: I heard Muhammad ibn Idris - meaning al-Shafi'i - saying: I sat with Sufyan and he mentioned this hadith, so al-Zuhri said: from Abu Salamah or Sa'id from Abu Hurayrah, then he mentioned it. And no other of the prophets (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed except in particular places. And he was also distinguished that purification was made for him with the ground, which is of the earth, as purification standing in place of purification with water when water is lacking, until he performs thereby the obligations, as he performed them with purification and with water had he found it. And that was not given to anyone before him of the prophets, blessings of Allah upon them. And He, Mighty and Majestic, favored him by making spoils lawful for him, while they had not been lawful to any of the prophets before him; rather a fire used to descend from heaven and consume them. So in that is what indicated his excellence over all the prophets, may Allah bless him and them, and increased him in honor and excellence, and rewarded him from us the best of what He rewarded any of His creation from any of them. And Allah we ask for granting of success."
"4554","620 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his prohibition of prayer after sunrise until it rises, and after its standing until it declines, and after its yellowing until it sets. And was that upon all days? And was that upon the obligatory prayers and their supererogatory ones or not? 3970 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, he said: Abdullah said to me: We were prohibited from prayer at sunrise and at its setting, and at midday."
"4555","3971 - Bahr ibn Nasr al-Khawlani narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih al-Himsi narrated to me, he said: Abu Yahya narrated to me - Abu Ja'far said: and he is Salim ibn Amir al-Khabairi - and Damrah ibn Habib and Abu Talhah, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili. He said: Amr ibn Abasah al-Sulami narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: The nearest the Lord the Mighty and Majestic is to the servant is the last hollow of the night, so if you are able to be of those who remember Allah the Mighty and Majestic in that hour, then do. For prayer is attended, witnessed until sunrise. So when it rises, it rises between the two horns of Satan, and it is the hour of the prayer of the disbelievers, so leave prayer until it rises and its rays depart. Mu'awiyah said: As for Damrah, he said: until it rises a spear's length. Then prayer is attended, witnessed until midday. And it is an hour the doors of Hell are opened and it is stoked, so leave prayer until the shadow inclines. Then prayer is attended, witnessed until sunset; for it sets between the two horns of Satan and it is the hour of the prayer of the disbelievers."
"4556","3972 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ulayy, from his father, he said: I heard Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani saying: Three hours the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to prohibit us to pray in them or to bury in them our dead: when the sun rises shining until it rises, and when the stander of noon stands until it declines, and when the sun inclines to setting until it sets."
"4557","3973 - Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Iyad ibn Abdullah al-Qurashi narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, is there among the hours of night and day an hour you command me not to pray in? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. When you have prayed, refrain from prayer until the sun rises, for it rises between the two horns of Satan. Then prayer is witnessed, attended, accepted until midday. So when midday comes, refrain from prayer until the sun declines; for then Hell is stoked, and the severity of heat is from the exhalation of Hell. So when the sun declines, prayer is attended, witnessed, accepted until you pray Asr. So when you have prayed Asr, refrain from prayer until the sun sets. Then prayer is witnessed, attended, accepted until you pray Fajr."
"4558","3974 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abdullah al-Sunabihi: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun rises and with it is the horn of Satan. So when it rises high it leaves it, until when it is even it accompanies it. So when it declines it leaves it. So when it nears setting it accompanies it. So when it sets it leaves it. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited prayer in those hours."
"4559","3975 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Malik and Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, they said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from Ata ibn Yasar, he said: I heard Abdullah al-Sunabihi saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, then he mentioned the like of it. As for the rest: these times are covered by this prohibition mentioned in these reports, and there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that all supererogatory prayer entered into that, except that Malik held that prayer at the sun's standing is not prohibited, since with him it is of what prayer is not feasible in, for it only stands then declines with no interval of time before its like, so prayer is not feasible therein. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the proof over all people, and he only prohibited what is possible, by one who if he does it is disobedient. Yet we found it stands and remains as if disturbed for a duration, then passes after that; so that duration is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited prayer in. And his saying that he did not prohibit prayer therein initially: that entering into that prohibition is entering prayer upon which that time prohibited for prayer supervenes; for those praying need from when they enter their prayer until they exit it to be upon the states upon which they may only enter: purity, covering of private parts, facing the qiblah. Likewise they are in the time they were prohibited to pray in. Except that Abu Yusuf and al-Shafi'i excluded Friday from that for prayer therein at the standing of the noon sun, and distinguished between Friday in that and the rest of days. And they argued in that with reports they narrated therein excepting Friday from the prohibition narrated therein, and that is of what we did not find sound nor narrated from a firm one of the firm from whom knowledge is taken; rather we found it in interrupted reports and in reports with no chains establishing proof with the people of chains. And what is like this, nothing of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) generalized should be excluded by it, nor should anything excluded from it be acted upon except by the like of what came by which the rest of reports therein enter. Except that a group argued for them in that saying: We saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command cooling for Zuhr prayer in heat, and inform along with that that severity of heat is from the exhalation of Hell, yet did not command for that cooling for Friday. They said: So that indicated Friday is specified in that by a meaning distinguishing it from the rest of days. So we examined what they said of that and found for it no meaning; for the time cooled for Zuhr prayer therein is after the sun's decline, while the time prohibited for prayer at the sun's standing is before its decline. So they are two different times; in each was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) other than what was in the other. So obligatory upon us is holding to his command and stopping at his prohibition, and not making anything of his command and prohibition contradicting the other, until we employ all he commanded us with, and until we do not exit from anything of his command nor his prohibition. Then the people of knowledge spoke concerning making up prayer, meaning the obligations, in these hours prohibited for prayer therein. So some said: It is not permissible to pray therein any of the obligatory prayers in any state. And some said: It is not permissible to pray therein any of the obligatory prayers other than the Asr of the day prayed in; for it is prayed in the state of the sun's yellowing and before its setting. And of those saying that among them are Abu Hanifah and his companions. And they held in that that the last time of Asr is sunset, and that the prohibition of prayer after its yellowing to its setting came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as we mentioned, so they excluded what is a time for it thereof therein, and entered therein what is besides it of prayers. And analogy with us thereof necessitated that its last time is the sun's yellowing; for we found every time besides that time in which obligations may be prayed, supererogatory may be prayed therein, and every time in which obligations may not be prayed, supererogatory may not be prayed therein. And this is a saying narrated from Abu Bakrah, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4560","Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn al-Hasan al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Yazid ibn Abi Bakrah, he said: Abu Bakrah promised us to his land, so he preceded us to it. So we came to him while we had not prayed Asr, so he laid his head and slept, then awoke while the sun had yellowed. So he said: Did you pray Asr? We said: No. He said: I was awaiting none but you. So he delayed prayer until the sun set, then prayed it. So this is the analogy in this chapter. And Malik ibn Anas and Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i used to hold that the prohibition we mentioned in these reports is only upon supererogatory prayers, not upon obligatory prayers thereof. So we examined what they differed in thereof and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by his prohibition of prayer in these reports times of the days he mentioned thereof therein. So we wanted to see whether obligations enter into that with supererogatory or do not enter with them therein. So we found him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited fasting days of the year, and they are the day of Fitr and day of Sacrifice and days of Tashriq. So we found them all made that upon obligatory fasting of Ramadan's makeup and of expiations, and upon supererogatory fasting. So they made for no one to fast them for anything thereof, nor made his fasting them if he fasted them discharging from what he fasted them for. And he did not mean thereby the fasting of the mutamatti' the days of Tashriq when he finds no sacrificial animal; for that is of what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed in, so some permitted it and some prohibited it. But we meant what is besides it of Ramadan's makeup and of fasting for expiations and for zihar. And when prohibition had entered all that therein, likewise the prohibition of prayers in the times mentioned of prohibition of prayers therein in these reports we narrated: its obligations and its sunnahs enter therein. Then an objector said: You should have returned the prohibition of prayers in these times to the prohibition of prayers after Fajr prayer until sunrise and after Asr until sunset, while all the people of knowledge permit making up missed prayers in both. And rulings of prayers are more like rulings of prayers than rulings of prayers are like rulings of fasting. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that the prohibiter of prayer after Fajr prayer until sunrise and after Asr prayer until sunset is the prayer, not the time itself. Do you not see that two men, had they attended while people prayed Fajr and the sun had not risen, one not having prayed Fajr and the other having prayed it, that we command the one not praying it to pray it, and prohibit the other from prayer for other than it of what entered the prohibition of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him from it? And that had they attended after Asr prayer while the sun had not yellowed, one having prayed Asr and the other not praying it, and they wanted to pray supererogatory with time ample, that we permit that to the one praying Asr of them, and prevent from that the one having prayed it of them. So we understood thereby that the prohibiter of prayer in those two times is the prayer, not the two times. While the prohibition of prayer in the times mentioned in the reports we narrated in this chapter: all people are equal therein and do not differ therein. So we understood thereby that the prohibiter of prayers therein is its time, not other than it. And the days prohibited for fasting are of what all people are equal in, in what they agreed entered the prohibition of fasting them for. So that prohibition of prayers therein of time is for time, not for other than it; its counterpart is prohibition of fasting in time for time, not for other than it. So for that we returned the ruling of prayer in these times to it, not to prayer after Fajr until sunrise and after Asr until sunset. Then this objector said: So for those holding permissibility of obligatory prayers in these times is of proof against whoever opposed them therein what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein."
"4561","3976 - So he mentioned what Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Khilas, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever catches a rak'ah of the dawn prayer before the sun rises, let him pray another to it."
"4562","3977 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever catches a rak'ah of Asr prayer before the sun sets, his prayer is complete; and when he catches a rak'ah of Fajr prayer, his prayer is complete. And concerning that are many reports, these two the firmest; we left bringing them fearing the book's length with them. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is that it may be that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before he prohibited prayer in the times we mentioned at the head of this chapter, then prayer in those times was prohibited, so what is in these two hadiths was abrogated thereby. And it may be that what is in these two hadiths is the abrogater of that. And when the two possibilities are equal therein, they cancel, and the matter in what therein is this disagreement returns to what must be returned to therein when lacking it from the Book, the Sunnah, and consensus, and it is the analogy we mentioned. And Allah we ask for granting of success. And there has been narrated from Abdullah ibn Umar what indicates the separation of the ruling of prayer after Fajr and after Asr in its time for obligatory prayers, from after sunrise before its rising for that."
"4563","As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik and al-Layth informed me that Nafi' narrated to them, from Abdullah ibn Umar: that he used to pray over funerals after Fajr prayer and after Asr prayer when both were prayed in their time. Abu Ja'far said: Meaning: when both were prayed in their time and there remained of their time before exiting what is prayed therein over funerals, which are obligations."
"4564","And as Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah, freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sufyan ibn Huwaytib: that Zaynab bint Abi Salamah died while Tariq was governor of Madinah, so her funeral was brought after Fajr prayer and placed at al-Baqi'. He said: And Tariq prayed Fajr in the dark. Ibn Abi Harmalah said: So I heard Abdullah ibn Umar saying to her family: Either pray over your funeral now, or leave it until the sun rises."
"4565","And as al-Qasim ibn Abdullah ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Unays ibn Abi Yahya, from his father: that a funeral was placed before sunrise, so Ibn Umar stood and said: Where is the guardian of this funeral to pray over it before the horn of Satan rises. Abu Ja'far said: So that indicated that the school of Abdullah ibn Umar was: no harm in prayer over funerals after Fajr prayer before sunrise, and that it is not sound to pray over them after sunrise until it rises. And prayer over funerals is of the obligations, even if some people discharge it from others until the obligation thereby falls from the rest. So likewise missed obligatory prayers: thus is their ruling - prayed after Fajr prayer before sunrise, and not sound to pray after sunrise until it rises. And with Allah is granting of success."
"4566","621 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his sleep and his Companions' sleep past Fajr prayer until the sun's heat awakened them. 3978 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from his father: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey and said: Who will guard for us tonight, not sleeping until dawn? So Bilal said: I. So he faced the sunrise and their ears were struck until the sun's heat awakened them. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and performed ablution, and they performed ablution. Then they sat a while, then prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then prayed Fajr."
"4567","3979 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah al-Ansari, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveled by night in one of his campaigns with those with him, so some of the people said: If only you rested. So he said: I fear you will sleep past prayer. So Bilal said: I shall awaken you. So the people dismounted and lay down, and Bilal leaned his back to his mount, and sleep was cast upon them. So the people awoke while the sun's edge had risen. So he said: Where is what you said, O Bilal? He said: O Messenger of Allah, Allah took your souls when He willed and returned them to you when He willed. He said: Then call the people to prayer. So he called them, and they performed ablution. So when the sun rose, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then prayed Fajr."
"4568","3980 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, he said: Husayn informed us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"4569","3981 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abu Qatadah, he said: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a campaign - or he said: in a detachment - so when it was the end of the pre-dawn, we rested, and we did not awake until the sun's heat awakened us. So the man of us began leaping, confused, frightened. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) awoke and commanded us, so we moved from our march until the sun rose. Then we dismounted, and the people relieved themselves. Then he commanded Bilal, so he called to prayer, and we prayed two rak'ahs, then he established, then he prayed the dawn. Abdullah said: So Imran ibn Husayn heard me while I narrated this hadith in the congregational mosque. So he said: Who is the man? I said: I am Abdullah ibn Rabah al-Ansari. So he said: The people are more knowledgeable of their hadith; see how you narrate, for I am one of the seven that night. So when I finished he said: I did not think anyone retains this hadith besides me. Hammad said: And Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Bakr, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abu Qatadah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So in these reports was the delaying by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Fajr prayer to the sun's rising. So in that is support for the saying of whoever said: obligatory prayers are not prayed at sunrise; for had sunrise not prevented that, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have delayed making up prayer therein to the time he delayed it to. Then an objector said: You narrated to us in what preceded of this book of yours from Aishah that she said: O Messenger of Allah, do you sleep before you pray witr? So he said: O Aishah, my eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep. Then he said: So in this hadith is that he slept a sleep by which understanding left him with his heart, and in that is the sleep of his heart. He said: And he confirmed what we said."
"4570","3982 - So he mentioned what Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Maysarah al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Raja al-Utaridi, he said: Imran ibn al-Husayn narrated to us, he said: We rested with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and we did not awake except by the sun's heat. So six of us awoke, then Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) awoke and began preventing them from awakening him, saying: Perhaps Allah the Mighty and Majestic has detained him in his need. So Abu Bakr began saying takbir until he awoke. He said: So in this hadith is what entered: that his eyes had slept and his heart had slept; for had there remained to him a heart untouched by sleep, the awakening of those awakening before him would not have been hidden from him, nor would he need following takbir until that awakens him from his sleep. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that the matter therein is not as he imagined, and that what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon of what is in the hadith of Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) is what he was upon, and it is a sign of the signs of his prophethood by which Allah the Mighty and Majestic distinguished him from others of His creation. As for his sleep in the night he slept in, like the sleep of others of people, it was for a meaning by which Allah the Mighty and Majestic willed it be a cause for what is done after him in the like of that state. And the proof upon that."
"4571","3983 - What Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Jami' ibn Shaddad Abu Sakhrah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Alqamah, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from the campaign of al-Hudaybiyah, he alighted at a stop and said: Who will guard us tonight? Abdullah said: I. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will sleep. He repeated three times. Abdullah said: I. He said: You then. So he guarded them. So when it was toward dawn, what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said overtook me, and I did not awake except with the sun upon our backs. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and did as he used to do for prayer, and prayed with us. Then he said: Had Allah the Mighty and Majestic willed that you not sleep, you would not have slept; but He willed it be a sunnah for those after you. And thus for one who slept or forgot."
"4572","3984 - And what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Al-Mas'udi narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he said: from Abd al-Rahman ibn Alqamah, and did not say: Ibn Abi Alqamah."
"4573","3985 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Zafir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Jami' ibn Shaddad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Alqamah - and he did not say: Ibn Abi Alqamah - from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the campaign of Tabuk. So when we were at dahas of land, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will guard us tonight? Bilal said: I. He said: Then you will sleep. So he slept until the sun rose, and so-and-so and so-and-so awoke. So we said: Speak until he awakes. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) awoke and said: Do what you used to do. And likewise does one who slept or forgot. So that sleep was for this meaning. Then this objector said: And what need did they have to know that by what was from him after his awakening from his sleep, which they did not know before that? So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is that it may be they did not - meaning know - how is the ruling of Allah the Mighty and Majestic concerning one who slept past a prayer of the written prayers until its time it was prayed in exited: does he pray it in other than it? Or does he not pray it, as he does not pray Friday in other than its time when he did not pray it in its time? And it may also be that the obligation of Allah the Mighty and Majestic did not obligate upon him that prayer, since its time He commanded to pray it in was while the pen was lifted from him."
"4574","3986 - As Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), from Ali, peace be upon him. Ha."
"4575","3987 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The pen is lifted from three: from the boy until he matures, and from the sleeper until he awakes, and from the insane until he recovers. So they knew thereby from the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from his saying what they had not known before that. So it became clear, by the praise of Allah and His favor, that there is no contradiction in any of these reports, and that each kind of them is for a meaning intended by it other than the meaning opposing it intended by another of them. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4576","622 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty of the cause for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed the prayer he and his Companions slept past until the sun rose, to the time he delayed it to - what it is. We mentioned in the reports we narrated in the chapter in which we mentioned the sleep of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions past this prayer until the sun rose upon them, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delayed it until the sun rose high upon him. So a group said: His delaying it was to exit from him the time in which prayer is not lawful, and the time in which prayer is lawful enters upon him. And they are Abu Hanifah and his companions. And opponents among them al-Shafi'i opposed them in that and said: The cause of his delaying it was only the presence of Satan attending them in that valley, and to exit from it to other than it of the place in which is that Satan. And they mentioned concerning that. 3988 - What Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abu Salamah Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rested with us on his return from Khaybar and said: Who will guard for us our prayer? So Bilal said: I. So they slept and did not awake except by the sun. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Move from this place in which heedlessness befell you. Then he said: O Bilal, did you sleep? He said: What took me took you. Then he commanded Bilal, so he called to prayer and established, and prayed. Then he said: Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it. Then he said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: Establish prayer for My remembrance."
"4577","3989 - And what Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Kaysan narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: We rested a night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and nothing awakened us except the sun's heat. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let each man of you take his mount's head, for this is a stop where Satan attended us. So each of us took his mount's head. Then when we dismounted, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us."
"4578","3990 - And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Kaysan, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, except that he said: Then he called for water and performed ablution, then prostrated two prostrations, then prayer was established and he prayed the dawn."
"4579","3991 - And what Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rested one night on the road of Makkah, and neither he nor any of his Companions awoke until the sun struck them. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) awoke and said: This is a stop with a devil in it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led his mount on, and his Companions led on, until the forenoon rose. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions made halt and he led them and prayed Fajr. They said: His delaying prayer was only for the place of Satan in that place, not because it was in a time not permissible to make it up therein. And when they differed in that, we considered what they differed in thereof and found the presence of Satan is of what does not prevent prayer, since he had presented himself to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while in prayer, and he did not exit it for that, and there was from him to him therein and of his completing it until he finished from both."
"4580","3992 - What Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, (ha) and what Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, they said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu al-Darda (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood praying, and we heard him saying: I seek refuge with Allah from you. Then he said: I curse you with the curse of Allah, three times. Then he extended his hand as if taking something. So when he finished prayer they said: O Messenger of Allah, we heard you saying in prayer something we never heard you say before that, and saw you extend your hand. He said: The enemy of Allah, Iblis, came with a flame of fire to place it in my face, so I said: I seek refuge with Allah from you, but he did not retreat. Then I said it but he did not retreat. Then I said that but he did not retreat. Then I said: I curse you with the complete curse of Allah, but he did not retreat. Then I said it but he did not retreat. Then I said that but he did not retreat. Then I wanted to seize him, and were it not for the supplication of our brother Sulayman, he would have remained bound, the boys of Madinah playing with him. So thereby it became impossible that his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving prayer was for that, especially as Abu Qatadah and Imran ibn al-Husayn mentioned in their two hadiths we mentioned in that chapter that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had delayed prayer until the sun rose, then prayed it. So in that was what indicated that his delaying it was with them to the sun's rising, not for other than that. Then the others said: In this hadith is what indicated that the time prohibited for prayer therein had exited, and it is the saying of its narrators: nothing awakened them except the sun's heat. So in that is what indicated its rising before they awoke from their sleep. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is: that the sun may have risen with its heat as is found in the Hijaz in its heat to now; and were that not so, there would be no meaning to the mention by Abu Qatadah and Imran of its rising. And we mentioned in that chapter of what reasoning necessitates concerning prayer at sunrise, of what we are sufficed by from repeating here. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4581","623 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his answer to Abu al-Darda when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited while upon the pulpit: And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens. So Abu al-Darda said to him: Even if he fornicated and stole? - by his saying to him: Even if he fornicated and stole. 3993 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu al-Darda: that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while upon the pulpit saying: And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens. So I said: Even if he fornicated and stole, O Messenger of Allah? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the second time: And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens. So I said: Even if he fornicated and stole? So he said the third time: And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens. So I said: Even if he fornicated and stole, O Messenger of Allah? So he said: Even if the nose of Abu al-Darda is rubbed in dust. So we examined this hadith to stand upon what is meant by it, if Allah wills. So we found fear of the station of the Lord the Mighty and Majestic a lofty rank, and found its reward with Him the Mighty and Majestic a mighty reward, and found it prevents from minor disobediences of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and from major ones. And as narrated from Mujahid concerning the saying of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens. He said: When he intends disobedience and remembers the station of Allah the Mighty and Majestic over him in this world, he leaves it."
"4582","As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid informed us, from Mansur, from Mujahid: And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens. He said: A man intends disobedience, then remembers Allah the Mighty and Majestic and leaves it. And it was impossible that that fear of the station of Allah the Mighty and Majestic mixes with one committing fornication and theft. So we understood thereby that the fornication and theft meant in this hadith are only fornication and theft that had been in a state of one they were from, then he moved from that state to fear of the station of his Lord the Mighty and Majestic, the fear preventing him from falling into anything thereof. And when these two states, each the opposite of the other, we understood thereby that each was in the state of the absence of the other. So the blameworthy state was at first, then followed by the praiseworthy state. So its person moved therein to fear of the station of his Lord, and returned the theft to the one stolen from, and sought his Lord's promise and feared His threat. And he was thereby of the people of what is mentioned in this hadith, even if he had fornicated and stolen in a state he moved away from to a praiseworthy state he moved to. And we found concerning that in the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic what indicated that, and it is His saying therein: And those who do not call with Allah another god, nor kill the soul Allah prohibited except by right, nor fornicate. And whoever does that meets sin. Multiplied for him is punishment on the Day of Resurrection, and he abides therein humiliated. So He, Mighty and Majestic, informed us that whoever is of the people of these acts is of the people of this threat. Then He followed that with His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Except whoever repents and believes and does righteous deed, then those, Allah changes their evils to good. So whoever moved to this state moved to the people of this promise and exited the people of threat. So that indicated that the states of fornication and theft are other than the states of fear of the station of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, even if each of the two states was, with the other state absent. And in what we mentioned is clarification of what we described. And Allah we ask for granting of success."
"4583","624 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his answer to one who said to him, after his saying: Whoever dies associating nothing with Allah enters Paradise: Even if he fornicated and stole? And by his saying to him: Even if he fornicated and stole. 3994 - Abu Umayyah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: By Allah, Abu Dharr narrated to us at al-Rabadhah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Jibril, peace be upon him, came to me and informed me that whoever of my community dies associating nothing with Allah the Mighty and Majestic enters Paradise. I said: O Messenger of Allah, even if he fornicated and stole? He said: Even if he fornicated and stole."
"4584","3995 - And Abu Umayyah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: Abu Salih narrated to me, from Abu al-Darda, the like of it. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, even if he fornicated and stole? He said: Even if he fornicated and stole, even if the nose of Abu al-Darda is rubbed in dust."
"4585","And Abu Umayyah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: Al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: I said to Zayd ibn Wahb - meaning when he narrated to him the hadith we mentioned at the head of this chapter - that it reached me that it is Abu al-Darda. So he said: I bear witness Abu Dharr narrated it to me at al-Rabadhah."
"4586","3996 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us, they said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Zayd ibn Wahb, from Abu Dharr - Hammad said: Between me and Abu Dharr is none but him - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out toward al-Gharqad, and I set out with him. Then he mentioned the like of the first hadith exactly."
"4587","3997 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, from Hatim ibn Abi Sagheirah, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated to us that Abu Sulayman al-Juhani narrated to him, he said: Abu Dharr narrated to me, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"4588","3998 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi and Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, they said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Wasil al-Ahdab, from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd, from Abu Dharr, then he mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except that he said: A comer from my Lord the Mighty and Majestic came to me, and did not mention Jibril, peace be upon him."
"4589","3999 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, he said: Shayban, meaning al-Nahwi, narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Salamah ibn Nu'aym - and he was of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever meets Allah the Mighty and Majestic associating nothing with Him enters Paradise, even if he fornicated and stole."
"4590","4000 - And Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Murajja ibn Raja narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Umm al-Darda, from Abu al-Darda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Jibril, peace be upon him, said: Whoever says: There is no god but Allah, enters Paradise. He said: I said: Even if he fornicated and stole? He said: Even if he fornicated and stole."
"4591","4001 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ja'far al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Malih narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Yazid, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens. He said: O Messenger of Allah, even if he fornicated and stole? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Even if he fornicated and stole, even if the nose of Abu Hurayrah is rubbed in dust."
"4592","4002 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hakim narrated to us, he said: Abu Maryam narrated to me, he said: I heard Abu al-Darda narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever testifies that there is no god but Allah, or dies associating nothing with Allah the Mighty and Majestic, enters Paradise, or: does not enter Fire. He said: I said: Even if he fornicated and stole? He said: Even if he fornicated and stole, even if the nose of Abu al-Darda is rubbed in dust."
"4593","4003 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Imran informed us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Musa, from his father Abu Musa: that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among a group of his people and said: Give glad tidings, and give tidings to those behind you, that whoever says: There is no god but Allah, truthful in it, enters Paradise. So they went out giving people tidings, then Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) met them, so they gave him tidings and he turned them back. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who turned you back? They said: Umar turned us back. So he said: Why did you turn them back, O Umar? He said: Then people will rely, O Messenger of Allah. And in what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter is what suffices from speech in this chapter, except that we bring in this chapter a meaning therein confirming what we brought in that chapter, if Allah wills. And it is that when one who says: There is no god but Allah has said it knowing what is obligatory upon its people, then he said it while knowing the station of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and what its people hope at their fear of opposing Him and exiting His command. And in that is what indicates that the state of fornication and state of theft which had been from him moved away from them to their opposite, upon what we mentioned concerning that in the first chapter. And that is also indicated by what is in the hadith of Abu Musa we mentioned in this chapter: that whoever says: There is no god but Allah, truthful in it - and the meaning of his saying: truthful in it, and Allah knows best, i.e.: fulfilling its right - and we mentioned in this chapter also the hadith of Yazid ibn al-Asamm from Abu Hurayrah: And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens. And the first chapter was worthier of it, but its mention there escaped us, so we mentioned it here; for both chapters are of one kind. And a questioner asked about the meaning of the saying of Allah: Then those, Allah changes their evils to good - what was said concerning that? So our answer to him in that, and Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success, is: that what we found from the predecessors therein."
"4594","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid: Then those, Allah changes their evils to good. He said: Faith in place of disbelief. And what we found of what the people of Arabic say therein is that that is upon elision, and that it means: those whose evils Allah changes to good in their place. So He elided, like His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And ask the town we were in, meaning: and ask the people of the town we were in. So He elided mention of the people of the town, while they are meant. And Allah knows best, and with Him is granting of success."
"4595","625 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Aishah: Beware of belittled sins, for they have from Allah the Mighty and Majestic a seeker. 4004 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from so-and-so ibn al-Harith, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: O Aishah, beware of belittled sins, for they have from Allah the Mighty and Majestic a seeker."
"4596","4005 - And Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Muslim ibn Banak narrated to us, he said: I heard Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Awf ibn al-Harith ibn al-Tufayl narrated to me, that Aishah informed him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her, then they mentioned the like of it."
"4597","4006 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Muslim ibn Banak narrated to us, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Awf ibn al-Harith informed me, that Aishah informed him, then he mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"4598","4007 - And al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Muslim ibn Banak narrated to us, he said: I heard Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, he said: Awf ibn al-Harith ibn al-Tufayl narrated to me that Aishah informed him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: And of what Muhammad ibn Sa'd mentioned in his book on Tabaqat, he said: And Awf ibn al-Harith ibn al-Tufayl ibn al-Harith al-Azdi. He said: And al-Tufayl, meaning his grandfather, is the maternal brother of Aishah, and he is the son of Umm Ruman. Al-Harith came from al-Sarah and allied with Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) and followed him, with him his wife Umm Ruman and his child. Then he died, so Abu Bakr married Umm Ruman, and their call today is among Banu Tamim. So we examined this hadith and found therein the warning by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the people of faith against belittled sins. So that indicated that they are taken by them despite their faith, punished for them unless Allah the Mighty and Majestic pardons them. And in that is what indicated that faith does not lift punishments of minor sins; and when it does not lift punishments of their minors, it is worthier not to lift punishments of their majors. And in that is what indicated what we mentioned in the two chapters we mentioned before this chapter. And we found in the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic what indicates this meaning, and it is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: And the record is placed, and you see the criminals fearful of what is therein, saying: Woe to us, what is with this record, it leaves neither small nor great except it enumerated it. And they found what they did present. And your Lord wrongs no one. And in that is what indicated that the people of the promise mentioned in the two hadiths of Abu al-Darda and Abu Hurayrah, at the recitation by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of: And for whoever feared the station of his Lord are two gardens, and at his answering each of Abu al-Darda and Abu Hurayrah for what he said to him: Even if he fornicated and stole, with what he answered him of both - that they moved after the fornication and after the theft which had been from them, away from the fornication and theft which had been from them, to their opposite. So they exited the people of threat for the people of the first meaning, and entered the people of the promise following it. So it became clear, by the praise of Allah and His favor, by what we mentioned of the meanings of the hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what we mentioned of what became clear thereby of both. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4599","626 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he commanded the one responded to at sneezing to say, of: May Allah guide you and set right your state, and of: May Allah forgive you. 4008 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Aban, meaning al-Abyad ibn Aban, narrated to me, from Ata, meaning Ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to teach us, saying: When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. So when he says that, let whoever is with him say: May Allah have mercy on you. And when he says that to him, let him say: May Allah forgive me and you."
"4600","4009 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Al-Fadl ibn Sahl al-A'raj informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Raqashi narrated to me, he said: Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. And said to him: May Allah have mercy on you. And when said to him: May Allah have mercy on you, let him say: May Allah forgive you. Thus Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us with this wording. So this hadith was with us better than the hadith of al-Abyad ibn Aban; for both return to Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, while the hearing of al-Abyad from Ata was at Kufah, and therein was the confusion of Ata, while the hearing of Ja'far ibn Sulayman from him was at Basrah, and the hearing of its people from him is sound; it was not in the state of his confusion; of them the two Hammads: Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd. And Abu Awanah narrated this hadith from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib and stopped it upon Abdullah, not passing with it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4601","As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, he said: Ibn Mas'ud used to teach us, saying: When one of you sneezes, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Abyad ibn Aban, and did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And the people of hadith say: The hearing of Sufyan al-Thawri from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib was in his sound state, and likewise Shu'bah, and likewise the two Hammads. And they say: The hearing of Abu Awanah from him was in both states together, and they do not distinguish it."
"4602","4010 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Khalid ibn Arfajah, he said: We were with Salim ibn Ubayd when a man of the people sneezed and said: Peace be upon you. So Salim said: And upon you and upon your mother. What is the affair of peace and the affair of what is here? Then he traveled an hour, then said to the man: Did what I said to you weigh upon you? He said: I wished you had not mentioned my mother with good nor other. He said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man of the people sneezed and said: Peace be upon you. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you and upon your mother. When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds, or: in every state. And let them respond to him: May Allah have mercy on you. And let him respond to them: May Allah forgive you."
"4603","4011 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from a shaykh of Ashja', he said: We were with Salim, then he mentioned the like of it. So in these reports is what the responded one says to one responding to him at his sneezing. And this is the school of the Kufans, among them Abu Hanifah and his companions. And the Hijazis opposed them in that, among them Malik and his companions, and they narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that."
"4604","4012 - What al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and al-Husayn ibn Nasr together narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah. And let his brother or companion say to him: May Allah have mercy on you. And let him say: May Allah guide you and set right your state. And we know no hadith narrated in this chapter better in isnad nor firmer of narrators than this hadith. And there has also been narrated therein."
"4605","4013 - What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, and what Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from his brother, from his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it. And when they differed in that this disagreement, and the like of this is not attained by reasoning and derivation, so we employ therein what we employed in other than it of what preceded from us in this book of ours, except that we stood upon their consensus that what is said to the sneezer in that is supplication for him. So we viewed supplication with forgiveness as supplication for the sneezer for forgiveness of his sins, and viewed supplication for him with guidance as supplication that may be upon one of two aspects: one, indication to praiseworthy things - and of that is the saying of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: Guide us the straight path; then the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the supplication he taught people in witr: And guide me among those You guided. And the other, firmness upon praiseworthy matters - and of that is the saying of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: And those guided, He increased them guidance. So in supplication with guidance is what is not in supplication with forgiveness. So supplication with that was worthier than supplication with forgiveness, especially as he joined to that: and set right your state, i.e.: and set right your condition. So thereby this became the worthier of the two, and that this is what the responded one says to one responding to him. Then if an objector says: The people of the first saying mentioned that that saying intending supplication with guidance was only from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the Jews, not to the Muslims, to be supplication for them that Allah guides them to Islam."
"4606","4014 - And he mentioned concerning that what Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hakim ibn al-Daylami, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, he said: The Jews used to feign sneezing before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hoping he would say: May Allah have mercy on you. So he used to say: May Allah guide you and set right your state."
"4607","4015 - And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hakim ibn al-Daylami, from al-Dahhak, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it, adding in his isnad over Abu Nu'aym: from al-Dahhak. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is that what is in this hadith is not at all of what is in the first hadiths; for what is in this hadith is that the Jews used to feign sneezing before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hoping he would say to them: May Allah have mercy on you, so he used to say to them: May Allah guide you. So this was only from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the Jews when sneezing. And the people of knowledge do not differ concerning what is said to the sneezer at his sneezing; rather they differ therein in what the sneezer says to one responding to him at his sneezing: so some say: May Allah forgive you, and some say: May Allah guide you and set right your state. And the hadith of Abu Musa is not at all in this. And if he also says: It has been narrated from Ibrahim."
"4608","So he mentioned what Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Isa narrated to us, (ha) and what Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Wasil, from Ibrahim, he said: May Allah guide you and set right your state at sneezing is something the Khawarij said, for they did not seek forgiveness for people. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is that the worthiest for us is to carry what Ibrahim said of this upon that it was only from him because there did not reach him what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by what we mentioned. And we know that the like of him, may Allah be pleased with him, upon his knowledge, jurisprudence, and loftiness of rank, had the like of this reached him, he would not have opposed it nor said other than it; but he is human, there escapes him what escapes humans."
"4609","And Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to me, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, he said: I said to Ibrahim: A man prayed with a man, where does he place him from him? So he said: At his left. So I said to him: Ibn Abbas narrated that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while praying and stood at his left. He said: So he moved me back and placed me at his right. So Ibrahim said: I never heard of this - i.e.: So when I heard of it, it was worthier than what I said. And thus must be employed therein and in its likes of the people of knowledge, may Allah be pleased with them. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic we ask for granting of success."
"4610","627 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: People are about to strike the livers of camels in seeking knowledge, yet find no scholar more knowledgeable than the scholar of Madinah. 4016 - Abu Ayyub Ubaydullah ibn Ubayd ibn Imran al-Tabarani, known as Ibn Khalaf, narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People are about to strike the livers of camels seeking knowledge; they find no scholar more knowledgeable than the scholar of Madinah."
"4611","4017 - And Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: Al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People are about to strike the armpits of mounts in seeking knowledge, yet find no scholar more knowledgeable than the scholar of Madinah. Sufyan said: So they view that he is Abdullah ibn Abd al-Aziz, of the offspring of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). And the knower of the affair of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: the faqih is only one who fears Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"4612","4018 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), raising it, he said: People are about to strike upon the livers of camels in seeking knowledge, yet find no scholar more knowledgeable than the scholar of Madinah. Sufyan said: If in our time there is one, it is that Umari, the worshipper, the knower who fears Allah the Mighty and Majestic, and his name is Abdullah ibn Abd al-Aziz. Abu Ja'far said: So we examined this hadith and found this name mentioned therein, meaning the scholar, may be deserved by one of two meanings: one, knowledge of the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and the laws of His religion, then of the sunnahs of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so whoever this is his description is a scholar, and he is the scholar who may be called faqih. And the other, fear of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and knowledge of what its person deserves of Allah's reward upon it and of His punishment in falling into its opposite; and of it is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Only those fear Allah of His servants, the learned. And whoever this is his description does not deserve to be called faqih. Then we needed to know which of the two knowers is the scholar mentioned in this hadith. So we found in this hadith what indicates to us which of these two knowers he is; for therein is: until they strike the armpits of camels in seeking knowledge. And the armpits of camels are only struck in seeking the knowledge that is fiqh, not in seeking the knowledge that is fear of Allah the Mighty and Majestic. So we understood thereby that the scholar mentioned in this hadith is the knower of the knowledge by which he may be called faqih. Then when he deserves this name, and with him is of fear of Allah the Mighty and Majestic what must be with him, of what is not found with others of the scholars we know called fuqaha, whoever this is his description is in the highest ranks of scholars, and he is the deserver of the rank the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned whoever it is in, in what he mentioned of it in this hadith. And we do not know that there was in Madinah after the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and after their Followers, any in whom are these two meanings, other than this man Sufyan mentioned with what he mentioned him with; for he was a faqih, ascetic, pious, sound, of those perhaps no blamer's blame overtakes him concerning Allah the Mighty and Majestic, and of those we know none who expended himself in the essence of Allah the Mighty and Majestic as he expended himself, nor who alerts to teaching knowledge one falling short of seeking it and one fallen short of by it besides him; for he used to go out to the desert whose people do not attend the cities for seeking knowledge, nor do the people of knowledge go out to them, so he teaches them knowledge, gives them understanding, teaches them their religion's affair, inclines them to what draws them near their Lord the Mighty and Majestic, and warns them of what distances them from Him, until they are thereby as they must be upon. So may Allah's pleasure be upon him and His mercy, and may Allah's pleasure also be upon Sufyan and His mercy, for his alerting to this place and his knowing its people. And Allah we ask for granting of success."
"4613","628 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying concerning one said of to him: So-and-so slept the night until morning - that is one in whose ear Satan urinated. 4019 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, he said: Shayban, and he is al-Nahwi, narrated to us, from Mansur, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, he said: It was said to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): So-and-so slept the night until morning. So he said: That is a man in whose ear Satan urinated."
"4614","4020 - Al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur al-Balisi narrated to us, he said: Al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, from Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid, from Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about one sleeping from the first of the night to its end. He said: That is one in whose ear Satan urinated."
"4615","4021 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Abdullah al-Hammal narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, he said: I mentioned before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a man and said: So-and-so slept the night until morning, not praying. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is a man in whose ear Satan urinated, or: in his ears. He said: So we examined this hadith to stand upon what is meant by it, if Allah wills. So we found therein, in the hadith of Ishaq, that that man had not prayed until morning. And we found of the praiseworthy morals the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) approved for his community his mentioning to them its opposite."
"4616","4022 - What Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil al-Lakhmi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sayyar ibn Salamah, he said: I entered with my father upon Abu Barzah, and heard him saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked sleep before the last Isha and talk after it."
"4617","4023 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal al-Anmati narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Sayyar ibn Salamah, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith upon what is in the hadith of Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil. And the sleep mentioned in the hadith we began with mentioning in this chapter was a sleep whose sleeper thereby wasted the obligation of Allah the Mighty and Majestic in Isha, then opposed what his Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked for him of sleep before it, which was a cause for wasting it and for leaving performing its obligation in the time Allah the Mighty and Majestic obligated its performance therein. So he was thereby opposing his Lord the Mighty and Majestic, obeying Satan in what he wants from him. So he struck upon his ears with that sleep, and it is what was cast into them of heaviness of sleep. And the Arabs call the like of that striking upon the ear. And of it is the saying of Allah the Mighty and Majestic concerning the people of the Cave: So We struck upon their ears in the Cave for numbered years. And that act was attributed to Satan because it is of what Satan approves from him. And he mentioned therein Satan's urinating in his ear, i.e.: he did with him the ugliest done with sleepers. And that is not upon the reality of urinating from him in his ear, but upon similitude and metaphor in meaning - like what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said of what we mentioned in what preceded from us in this book of ours, of Satan's tying at the head of one sleeping three knots; he does not mean thereby three knots of the knots sons of Adam tie with, but a similitude to them and metaphor for their meaning; for the knots sons of Adam tie prevent one tied with them from disposing of what he attempts disposing in. So like it is what is from Satan to the sleeper who does not rise from his sleep to what sleepers should rise to of remembrance of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and of prayer to Him. So this is the best what attended us of what this hadith bears. And Allah the Mighty and Majestic knows best what His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant in that. And Him we ask for granting of success."
"4618","629 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the ruling of slaughtered meat when it stinks. 4024 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Hadrami, from his father, from Abu Tha'labah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said concerning one finding his game after three: Let him eat it, unless it stinks."
"4619","4025 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Khalid al-Khayyat narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When you shoot game and find it after three, with your arrow, then eat it so long as it has not stunk. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is his preventing eating game meat when it stinks. Then an objector said: You narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts this. And he mentioned."
"4620","What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb narrated to us, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Muhajirun and Ansar set to digging the Trench, then were brought a handful of barley, so made for them with rancid dripping. So placed before the people, while the people were hungry, and it is harsh in the throat, with a foul odor. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is that what is in this hadith is other than what is in the first hadith; for what is in the first hadith is concerning slaughtered meat which returned by the stink occurring therein until it returned it to the carrions of dead animals, and returned it thereby to the foul things He prohibited by His saying, Mighty and Majestic, in describing His Prophet, and it is His saying: And He makes lawful for them the good things and prohibits for them the foul things. And this is of the foul. As for dripping, it is not of the things made lawful in its body by slaughter; rather it is of other than that, like ghee and milk and their likes. And the occurring of rancidity therein is only change of its taste, not its corruption in itself like the corruption of the meat we mentioned before it. Rather the occurring of that therein is like the occurring of rancidity in the oils people anoint with and in the oil they eat with; so that is not of what prohibits either of them upon them, as the occurring of the like of that in the water they drink and purify with does not prohibit that water upon them; for that is incidental therein, with no transformation to another kind like the transformation of meat to the corruption it transforms to, so it becomes thereby like the blameworthy things of carrions and other than them. And Allah we ask for granting of success."
"4621","630 - Chapter: Clarification of the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning floating fish, of preventing eating it, and what was narrated from him by which a group argued permissibility thereof. 4026 - Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Wahb ibn Kaysan and Nu'aym ibn Abdillah, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: What the sea recedes from, eat; and what it casts, eat; and what you find dead, floating upon water, do not eat."
"4622","4027 - And Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"4623","4028 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Umayyah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What the sea casts or recedes from, eat it; and what floats, do not eat it. So a group went to disliking eating what floats of fish and prevented that, and made its ruling like the ruling of meat that stank. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereby prevented eating it, upon what we mentioned in the hadith of Abu Tha'labah we narrated in the chapter before this chapter. And they also narrated concerning that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what agrees with this meaning."
"4624","What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Maysarah: that Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: What the sea casts is lawful, and he disliked floating fish."
"4625","And what Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Zadham and Maysarah, or one of them, from Ali: that he disliked floating fish."
"4626","And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, from Maysarah, from Ali, peace be upon him, he said: Eat what the sea casts, and what floats do not eat. They said: What floats of fish only floats due to its corruption, and in that is the stink of its meat. And of those holding this saying are Abu Hanifah and his companions. And a group permitted that, and they are Malik and al-Shafi'i. And they argued in that with what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4627","4029 - Of what Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Sa'id ibn Salamah of the family of Ibn al-Azraq, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Burdah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said concerning sea water: It is pure, its water; lawful, its dead."
"4628","4030 - And what Nassar ibn Harb al-Misma'i al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Sa'id ibn Salamah al-Zuraqi, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Burdah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So we examined this hadith in its isnad and found Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari had narrated it from al-Mughirah ibn Abdillah."
"4629","4031 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from al-Mughirah ibn Abdillah, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is pure, its water; lawful, its dead."
"4630","4032 - And as al-Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn al-Mughirah: that a man of Banu Mudlij said: We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: We fish upon rafts and go out with little water, so we perform ablution with sea water. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is pure, its water; lawful, its dead. And al-Mughirah ibn Abdillah mentioned in the hadith of Hammad from Yahya - he is al-Mughirah ibn Abi Burdah. And Yahya had referred him to his father, while Sa'id ibn Salamah had referred him to Abu Hurayrah. So Yahya referred him to disconnection and to an unknown man not known, while Sa'id referred him to Abu Hurayrah. And when Sa'id and Yahya differed, Yahya was worthier of correctness for his retention and firmness, and for the falling short of Sa'id ibn Salamah thereof and lagging behind him. And we also found this hadith from the hadith of Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, contrary to what Sa'id ibn Salamah narrated upon it."
"4631","4033 - As Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Rushdin narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn Umar narrated to us, from Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Burdah, from Abdullah al-Mudliji, he said: We were upon rafts in the sea, carrying little water with us; when we performed ablution with it we thirsted, and when we performed ablution with sea water it sufficed us. So we mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: It is pure, its water; lawful, its dead. And we found Julah Abu Kathir had narrated this hadith from Sa'id ibn Salamah, so attributed this Sa'id to Makhzum, opposing Safwan therein; for Safwan attributed him to the family of al-Azraq, who are not of Makhzum."
"4632","4034 - As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Kathir Julah: that Sa'id ibn Salamah al-Makhzumi informed him that al-Mughirah ibn Abi Burdah informed him, that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day when a fisherman came to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, we go out in the sea wanting to fish, so one of us carries with him one waterskin or two, hoping to find fish near. So perhaps he finds it thus, and perhaps he does not find fish until he reaches of the sea a place he did not think to reach. And perhaps he has a wet dream or performs ablution; so if he bathes or performs ablution with it at every prayer, the water runs out, so perhaps thirst destroys one of us. So what do you view, O Messenger of Allah, concerning sea water? Do we bathe with it or perform ablution with it when we fear that? So he claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, so bathe from it and perform ablution; for it is pure, its water; lawful, its dead. And this hadith was of what confused us its isnad, the confusion with which arguing with its like is not sound. And we bore Abd al-Jabbar ibn Umar in what we narrated from him of what we narrated from him therein, even though there befell him in his narration what befell him; for the people of hadith only reject of his narration what he narrated thereof from al-Zuhri and Ibn al-Munkadir, and do not reject what he narrated from others besides them, praising him therein. And what we narrated of his hadith is only from others besides them, and he is Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id al-Ansari. Then if an objector says: This hadith has been narrated from other than this aspect with this meaning."
"4633","4035 - So he mentioned what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Humayd ibn Sakhr, from Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Misri, from Abdullah ibn Razin, from al-Araki who asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, we ride upon rafts and go far in the sea, with water for our lips with us. So if we perform ablution with it we thirst, while they claim sea water is not pure. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Its water is pure and its dead is lawful. So our answer to him in that, by the granting of success by Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His help, is that the isnad of this hadith is fair as he mentioned, except that Abdullah ibn Razin is old; the meeting of Ayyash ibn Abbas with him does not fall in hearts. And he said: In this chapter also are reports in this meaning, of them."
"4634","4036 - What Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abi Shu'ayb al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from al-Julah, from Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Makhzumi, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Burdah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: A man of Banu Mudlij came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Rabi' from Shu'ayb from al-Layth from Yazid from Abu Kathir Julah, except that he opposed him in the name of the man who narrated this hadith from him. So al-Rabi' said in his hadith: Sa'id ibn Salamah, while Abu Umayyah said in his hadith: Abdullah ibn Sa'id. And this is severe confusion. And it has also been narrated from another aspect."
"4635","4037 - As Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us. 4038 - And as Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-Layth, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Muslim ibn Makhshi, that he narrated to him, that al-Firasi said: I used to fish in the green sea upon rafts. Then he mentioned this hadith."
"4636","4039 - And as Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: My grandfather narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah and Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Abu Mu'awiyah al-Alawi, from Muslim ibn Makhshi al-Mudliji, from al-Firasi: that he said: O Messenger of Allah. Then he mentioned this hadith. And this hadith was of what is not sound for us to argue with; for among its narrators is some unknown, and he is Abu Mu'awiyah al-Alawi and Muslim ibn Makhshi. And had we authenticated this hadith, there would be in it nothing contradicting the hadith of Jabir we narrated at the head of this chapter; for what is in this hadith is only: and its dead is lawful. So it may be that its dead is the dead the hadith of Jabir ibn Abdillah permitted. So both hadiths are sound, straight. And what is in the hadith of Jabir would be upon prohibiting the floating, and what is in the other hadith upon the dead besides the floating. And this is the worthiest carried upon these two hadiths, so one does not contradict the other, and the aspect of each is other than the aspect of the other. Then if an objector says: There has been narrated concerning permitting floating fish."
"4637","So he mentioned what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I bear witness upon Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: Floating fish is lawful for whoever wants to eat it."
"4638","And what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from Abu Bakr, the like of it."
"4639","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Abu Abd al-Rahman saying: I heard Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: There is nothing in the sea except Allah the Mighty and Majestic slaughtered it for you."
"4640","And what Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to me, from Abu Mijlaz and from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Fish is all slaughtered."
"4641","And what Muhammad narrated to us — he said: Hajjaj narrated to us — he said: Hammad narrated to us — he said: Habib ibn al-Shahid narrated to me, from Jabalah ibn Atiyyah: that the companions of Abu Talhah found a floating (dead) fish, so they asked Abu Talhah about it, and he said: Gift it to me. He said: In this is what indicates the permissibility of floating fish. Our answer to him regarding that, by the providence of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, is that in this hadith the statement of Abu Bakr and Abu Talhah indicates what was mentioned, but Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him, opposed them in it, and Jabir ibn Abd Allah agreed with him in opposing them."
"4642","As Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us — he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: Whatever floats (dead), do not eat it; whatever is on its banks (cast ashore), eat it; and whatever the ebb (of the tide) leaves, eat it. This is among what the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed over, and the worthiest of what they said regarding it is what agrees with what we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding it, and that is prohibition, not permissibility. And there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas what adds to this meaning."
"4643","As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us — he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us — he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, who said: A shepherd came to Ibn Abbas and said: I come to the sea and find it full of dead fish. He said: Do not eat carrion. In our view, this statement of Ibn Abbas contradicts what others among the people of knowledge said, and it refers to the mass (of dead fish) that is accompanied by floating upon the water — not other kinds, neither what (the sea) casts up nor what the ebb leaves behind. Thus the statement of Ibn Abbas comes back to the dislike of eating floating fish. And Allah we ask for providence."
"4644","631 – Chapter explaining the problematic (apparently conflicting) ruling on settlements between those among the people of knowledge who differ, concerning settling known-measured things for shares of their own kinds whose (amounts) are unknown, in light of what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that. 4040 – Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us — he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us — he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab — he said: Ibn Kab ibn Malik informed me: that Jabir ibn Abd Allah informed him that his father was killed on the day of Uhud as a martyr while a debt was upon him, and the creditors pressed hard for their dues. Jabir said: So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and spoke to him, and I asked them to accept the fruit of my walled garden (of dates) and to release my father (from the remainder), but they refused. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not give them my garden, nor did he break it (up) for them; rather he said: I shall come to you in the morning. So he came to us in the morning when dawn broke, walked among the date-palms, and supplicated for blessing in its fruit. Then we harvested it, and I paid them their dues, and there remained for us a remainder of its fruit. So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Umar, who was sitting: Listen, O Umar. Umar said: Should we not have known — we indeed knew that you are the Messenger of Allah; by Allah, you are indeed His Messenger."
"4645","4041 – Yunus narrated to us — he said: Ibn Wahb informed us — he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd Allah ibn Kab ibn Malik: that the father of Jabir ibn Abd Allah was killed on the day of Uhud as a martyr while a debt was upon him, and the creditors pressed hard for their dues. Jabir said: So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and spoke to him — then he mentioned the like of it exactly."
"4646","4042 – Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam informed us — he said: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, that he informed him that his father died leaving thirty wasqs owed to a Jewish man. Jabir asked him for respite, but he refused to grant him respite, so Jabir spoke to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to intercede for him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him and spoke to the Jew to take the fruit of his date-palms in settlement of what was due to him, but he refused. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered and walked among them, then said: O Jabir, harvest for him and pay him in full what is due to him. So he harvested it after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had left, and paid thirty wasqs in full, and seventeen wasqs remained over for him. Then Jabir came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to inform him of what he had done, and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying Asr. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished, Jabir came to him and informed him that he had paid in full, and informed him of the surplus that remained for him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Inform Ibn al-Khattab of that. So Jabir went to Umar and informed him, and Umar said: I knew, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) walked among them, that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, would surely bless them."
"4647","4043 – Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us — he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us — he said: Said ibn Salamah, who is Ibn Abi al-Husam, narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, who said: A man was owed such-and-such wasqs by my father, so I offered him the fruit of my date-palms in settlement of what was due to him, but he refused. And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered it to him, that he take it in settlement of his right, but he refused. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me and blessed my fruit, so I harvested and paid the man his due, and there remained from it the like of the fruit of the date-palms of every year."
"4648","4044 – Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us — he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us — he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us — he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: that there were wasqs of dates (owed) upon his father, so we said to the man: Take the fruit of our date-palms in settlement of what is upon him, but he refused. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him, accompanied by Umar, and supplicated for blessing for us in them. So we harvested them and gave the man everything that was due to him, and the estimated (yield) of our date-palms remained as it was. So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, and he said: Go to Umar and inform him. So I came to Umar and informed him, and he said: I knew, O Messenger of Allah, when you supplicated for blessing for them in them, that they would surely be blessed."
"4649","4045 – Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us — he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us — he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, who said: My father was stricken (martyred) leaving two gardens, while dates owed to a Jew upon him consumed all that was in the two gardens. So we came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him to speak to him to defer part of it from us. He spoke to him, but he refused. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Come, harvest your dates. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and entered one of the two gardens, the smaller of the two, and said to us: Harvest. So we began harvesting and bringing him the basket, and he would supplicate over it. When we finished, he said to the Jew: Measure out. So he gave him his due from the smaller of the two gardens, and the other garden remained for us. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is the request of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the creditors of Abd Allah ibn Haram that they accept the fruit of his walled garden — whose measured amount they had not ascertained, nor the equivalent of it matching their debt upon him — and that they release him from the remainder of their debt upon him, without their ascertaining its amount relative to their debt upon him. This is a matter over which the people of knowledge differed: some of them permitted release from known debts and from debts unknown to the one granting the release — among those who held that were Abu Hanifah and his companions, and that is the import of the saying of Malik. Others said: That is not permitted except regarding what both the one granting release and the one released know and ascertain the amount of at the time of the release — among those who said that was al-Shafii. Likewise what they differed over concerning settlement of claims that some people have upon others, for amounts thereof — short of their full measure — of their own kind, whose amounts the two settling parties do not know of what they settled upon: some of them permitted that, and they are those we mentioned regarding permission of the release we described, while others did not permit it, among them al-Shafii. In this hadith is what indicates the permissibility of that in releases and in settlements together, since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked the creditor of Abd Allah ibn Haram to take the fruit of that garden in settlement of what was due to him upon him, whose amount he did not know what it was, and to release him from the remainder of his debt, whose amount he did not know what it was. In this hadith is also another meaning that decides between those among the people of knowledge who differ concerning the heir settling with the creditors of his deceased father for part of their debt upon him: is that lawful for them, and is the remainder of his estate lawful for them, or not? We found all the people of knowledge permitting that except al-Awzai, for he did not permit it and barred the heir from it, because the creditors of his father have more right to his father's wealth than he does until they take their debts from it in full. In what we narrated of the request of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the creditor of Abd Allah ibn Haram — what he requested of him by way of waiting for part of his debt, in part of what we narrated — and of the debt remaining established upon Abd Allah ibn Haram, with no release from it until it is paid off on his behalf, is what indicates contrary to that: for if it is permitted for the creditor to be deferred for his debt until a time when the fruit of the deceased's garden contains what pays his debt and the remainder of its fruit is delivered to his heir, that indicates contrary to what al-Awzai said of what we mentioned from him. In the hadith of Yunus and Bahr, the garden is attributed to Jabir ibn Abd Allah, while in the hadith of Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam it is attributed to Abd Allah ibn Haram, the father of Jabir. What is in the hadith of Muhammad is in our view worthier of the two meanings, because of what is in the hadith of Ali ibn Shaybah, from Yazid, from Hammad, from Ammar — of Abd Allah ibn Haram leaving behind the two gardens, whose debt was paid from the fruit of the smaller of the two. And the saying of Jabir in others: the fruit of my garden, is as people attribute the belongings of those connected to them to themselves, not upon literal fact — to the point that that ascended to the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in his saying to Zayd ibn Harithah, when he judged between him and Ali and Jafar, may Allah be pleased with them, concerning the daughter of Hamzah, peace be upon him, in what he judged between them concerning her: As for you, O Zayd, you are my freedman and hers — though the allegiance (wala) of Zayd was to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not to her. We have mentioned that with its chain in what preceded from us in this book of ours. And Allah we ask for providence."
"4650","632 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) over which the people of knowledge differed concerning the shrouds of the dead: some of them said they come from the top of their estates, and some of them said they come from a third of their estates, in light of what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicating that. Abu Jafar said: We know of no one among the people of knowledge who held that the shrouds of the dead come from a third of their estates except Said ibn al-Musayyib; for there has been narrated from him regarding that what we took: from Harun ibn Kamil — whether by recitation to him or by license from him to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih informed us — he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me — he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Jafar narrated to me, from Bukayr, who is Ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that he said: The shroud of the dead person comes from his third — even if contrary to that has been narrated from him."
"4651","As Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah ibn Rashid al-Basriyyan narrated to us — they said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim al-Azdi narrated to us — he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah al-Dastuwa'i narrated to us — he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from al-Hasan and Sa'id, who said: The shroud comes from the whole of the wealth. As for those among the people of knowledge besides Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, they hold that it comes from the top of the estates — among them al-Hasan, whom we have mentioned in this hadith, and among them Ibn Sirin."
"4652","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Hisham, who is Ibn Hassan, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, who said: The shroud comes from the whole of the wealth. And among them is Mujahid."
"4653","As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Mujahid, who said: The shroud and the embalming perfume come from the whole of the wealth. And we have found this saying from Abd Allah ibn Umar as well."
"4654","As Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh and Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — they said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — he said: al-Hasan ibn Abi Jafar narrated to us — he said: Matar al-Warraq narrated to me, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The shroud comes from the whole of the wealth. Since they differed over that with this disagreement, we sought the right view in what they differed over, and the worthiest of what they said, of what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4655","4046 – So we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us — he said: Jafar ibn Awn al-Makhzumi, then al-Umari, narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Shaqiq, from Khabbab, who said: We emigrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking the Face of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and our reward became due upon Allah, Mighty and Majestic. Among us are those who died without having consumed any of their reward — among them was Musab ibn Umayr; he was killed on the day of Uhud and left nothing but a striped cloak. When we covered his head, his feet showed, and when we covered his feet, his head showed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Cover his head and place idhkhir (a fragrant grass) over his feet. And among us are those whose fruit ripened for them, and he is plucking it. Abu Jafar said: i.e., harvesting it, eating from it."
"4656","4047 – And we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us — he said: Abu Mamar Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj al-Minqari narrated to us — he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Juhadah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Abu Wa'il, from Khabbab al-Aratt — then he mentioned the like of it. Ibn Abi Dawud said to us: Abu Mamar said to us: Thus it was in the book of Abd al-Warith: Khabbab al-Aratt, whereas all the people besides him say Khabbab ibn al-Aratt."
"4657","4048 – And we found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — he said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us — he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought food, and he said: Mus'ab ibn Umayr was killed, and he was better than me, yet nothing was found to shroud him in except his cloak. And Hamzah, peace be upon him — or another man, may Allah be pleased with him — was killed, and he was better than me, yet nothing was found to shroud him in except his cloak. I fear that our good things have been hastened for us in our worldly life. Then he began to weep."
"4658","4049 – And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Sabiq al-Kufi narrated to us — he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: The martyrs of Uhud were buried in their clothes."
"4659","4050 – And we found Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us — he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me that Ibn Shihab narrated to them: that Anas ibn Malik narrated to him that the martyrs of Uhud were not washed, and were buried in their blood, and funeral prayer was not performed over them."
"4660","4051 – And we found Abu Umayyah narrated to us — he said: Khalid ibn al-Qatawani narrated to us — he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ansari narrated to us — he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, from his father: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Uhud: Who saw where Hamzah was killed? A man said: May Allah honor you — I saw where he was killed. He said: Then go and show it to us. So he went out until he stood over Hamzah, and saw that his belly had been split open and he had been mutilated. He said: O Messenger of Allah, he has been mutilated. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked looking at him, and stood among the slain and said: I am a witness over these. Wrap them in their blood, for there is no wound inflicted for the sake of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, except that it will come on the Day of Resurrection bleeding — its color the color of blood and its scent the scent of musk. Put forward the one with the most Quran and place him in the lahd (niche grave)."
"4661","4052 – And Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi narrated to us — he said: Ali ibn Asim narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered concerning the slain of Uhud that iron and skins be removed from them, and said: Bury them in their blood and their clothes. He said: What is in these reports — of the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to bury the dead mentioned in them, may Allah be pleased with them, in their clothes, which were all the wealth they left behind them, without any regard for what would be spent in settling a debt if one was upon them, and without any regard for what returns to their heirs of their estates equal to twice what they were shrouded in of their estates — indicates that the shrouds of the dead come from their estates with precedence over their debts, over their bequests, and over what is due to their heirs of their estates through inheritance from them. This is the saying of all the jurists of the garrison cities around whom legal verdicts revolve and to whose sayings recourse is had in them. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for providence."
"4662","633 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying to the Qurashis who had come from Makkah and said: O Muhammad, our sons and our slaves have joined you, so return them to us — whereupon he said: O company of Quraysh, Allah will surely send against you a man from among you whose heart Allah has tested for faith; he will strike you over religion. 4053 – Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us — he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah al-Nakha'i narrated to us, from Mansur ibn al-Mutamir, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, when he conquered Makkah and people from Quraysh came to him and said: O Muhammad, we are your allies and your people, and our sons and our slaves have joined you, with no desire in them for Islam — they only fled from work — so return them to us. He consulted Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, about their affair, and he said: They spoke the truth, O Messenger of Allah. His face changed, and he said: O Umar, what do you think? He said the like of the saying of Abu Bakr. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O company of Quraysh, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will surely send against you a man from among you whose heart Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has tested for faith; he will strike your necks over religion. Abu Bakr said: Is it I, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No. Umar said: Is it I, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No; rather it is the mender of the sandal in the mosque. He said: He had handed to Ali, peace be upon him, his sandal to mend it. He said: And Ali said: Yet I heard him say: Do not lie about me, for whoever lies about me will enter the Fire."
"4663","4054 – And Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid al-Farisi narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us — he said: Sharik narrated to us — he said: Mansur narrated to us — and had other than Mansur narrated to me I would not have accepted it from him; indeed I asked Mansur about it and he refused to narrate it to me, but when acquaintance developed between me and him, he himself narrated it to me — he said: Ribi ibn Hirash narrated to us — he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, peace be upon him, narrated to us at al-Rahbah, saying: Quraysh gathered before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Suhayl ibn Amr, and they said — then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Fahd exactly. We examined this hadith and found in it that the Qurashis mentioned in it, after the conquest of Makkah, said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the saying reported from them in it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered them with what he mentioned of his answer to them in it. That conquest was the conquest of al-Hudaybiyah, which preceded the conquest of Makkah."
"4664","As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us — he said: Musaddad narrated to us — he said: Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us — he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Amir, regarding: Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory — he said: The conquest of al-Hudaybiyah. And regarding His saying: Not equal among you are those who spent before the victory and fought — he said: The conquest of al-Hudaybiyah. This saying has also been narrated from one above Amir, namely Anas ibn Malik: as Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us — he said: Musaddad narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Shubah, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik, regarding: Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory — he said: Al-Hudaybiyah. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what confirms that."
"4665","4055 – As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us — he said: Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad al-Narsi narrated to us — he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us — he said: Sa'id, who is Ibn Abi Arubah, narrated to us, from Qatadah, that he narrated to them — he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to us that it was sent down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on his return from al-Hudaybiyah — meaning: Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory, that Allah may forgive you your former and latter sins — while his Companions were overcome with grief and gloom, having been barred from their rites, and they had sacrificed the sacrificial animals at al-Hudaybiyah. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There has been sent down upon me a verse dearer to me than the whole world. So the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited it to them. A man from the people said: Congratulations, O Messenger of Allah; Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has made clear to us what He will do with you — so what will He do with us? Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: That He may admit the believing men and the believing women into gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein, and expiate their evil deeds from them; and that is with Allah a great triumph. Thus Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made clear what He will do with His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and what He will do with them."
"4666","4056 – And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us — he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas — then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention his return from al-Hudaybiyah."
"4667","4057 – As Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us — he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us. (H) And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us — he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami and Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us — they said: Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us — he said: Abu Iyas, who is Muawiyah ibn Qurrah, informed us — he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn al-Mughaffal say: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest of Makkah upon a she-camel, reciting Surat al-Fath, and he repeated the melody in it. Shu'bah said: And Abu Iyas recited al-Fath, and Abu Iyas said: Were it not that people would gather, I would recite with this melody — or he said: with that melody; the wording is Yazid's. What we mentioned from Anas indicates that the conquest intended in this verse and in these reports is the conquest of al-Hudaybiyah, not the conquest of Makkah. That conquest was attributed to Makkah because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, cut off through it from His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then from his Companions, may Allah's pleasure be upon them, what the idolaters of the people of Makkah had against them, and thereby restrained them from them; it was a cause for lifting the war between him and them, for the strengthening of the people of Islam over them, and for breaking their might. And the threat of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to those who came to him from Quraysh of Makkah and asked him what they asked him in the hadith of Ali mentioned at the head of this chapter — of threatening them if they did not desist, with what he threatened them — could only be while they were upon disbelief and because Makkah was a land of war. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sufficed him against them, conquered Makkah for him, and they entered thereby into Islam upon whatever they entered it — willingly or unwillingly. And Allah we ask for providence."
"4668","634 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: Among you is one who will fight people over the interpretation of the Quran as I fought them over its revelation. 4058 – Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn Hafs al-Baghdadi, known as Ibn al-Imam, narrated to us — he said: Yusuf ibn Musa al-Qattan narrated to us — he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ismail ibn Raja al-Zubaydi, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were sitting waiting for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he came out to us from the chamber of A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her. His sandal broke, so he threw it to Ali, peace be upon him, then sat and said: Among you is one who will surely fight over the interpretation of the Quran as I fought over its revelation. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Is it I? He said: No. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Is it I? He said: No; rather it is the mender of the sandal in the chamber. Raja al-Zubaydi said: A man came to Ali at al-Rahbah and said: O Commander of the Believers, was there anything in the hadith of the sandal? He said: O Allah, You bear witness that it is among what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to confide to me."
"4669","4059 – Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim and Muhammad ibn Qudamah informed us — the wording is his — from Jarir, from al-Amash, from Ismail ibn Raja, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri — then he mentioned the like of it up to his saying: but it is the mender of the sandal, and did not mention what comes after it to the end of the hadith."
"4670","4060 – And Ismail ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi and Fahd ibn Sulayman together narrated to us — they said: Abu Nuaym al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us — he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Raja, who said: I heard my father say: I heard Abu Said al-Khudri say: We were waiting for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he came out to us from the houses of some of his wives. We stood and walked with him, then the strap of his sandal broke. Ali took it and stayed behind with it to repair it, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood waiting for him, and we stood with him. Among the people that day were Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. He said: Among you is one who will surely fight over the interpretation of the Quran as I fought over its revelation. Abu Bakr and Umar craned toward it, and he said: No; rather it is the mender of the sandal. So I came to him to give him glad tidings of what was said about him, and it was as if he paid no heed to it, as if it were something he had already heard."
"4671","4061 – And Fahd narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Ghaniyyah narrated to us, from his father, from Ismail ibn Raja, from his father, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were sitting in the mosque when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us, as if birds were upon our heads, none of us speaking. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Among you is one who will fight people over the interpretation of the Quran as I fought them over its revelation. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Is it I, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No. Umar said: Is it I, O Messenger of Allah? He said: No; rather it is the mender of the sandal in the chamber. Then Ali came out to us with the sandal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), repairing it. Abu Jafar said: We sought the name of the father of Ismail ibn Raja and whether any besides his son narrated from him, and we found Muhammad ibn Ismail al-Bukhari mentioned that he is Raja ibn Abi Rabiah. He said: He narrated from al-Bara ibn Azib and from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Jafar said: Among those who narrated from him besides his son was Yahya ibn Hani."
"4672","As Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us — he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us — he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Hani, from Raja al-Zubaydi, from al-Bara: that he used to wipe over socks and sandals. We examined this hadith and found what is in it different from what is in the hadith we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter: what is in the hadith we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter was a threat, on account of the matter about which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked by those of Quraysh who came to him from Makkah; while in the hadith we mentioned in this chapter is the promise of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), which he promised, of the one mentioned in it who would fight after him over the interpretation of the Quran as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fought over its revelation. What is in this hadith was a promise that must have occurred, of what Allah brought about at the hand of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him — his fighting the people of interpretation whom He mentioned in His Book."
"4673","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Dawud al-Khuraybi narrated to us, from Bassam al-Sayrafi, from Abu al-Tufayl: that Ibn al-Kawwa asked Ali, peace be upon him, about the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: Those whose effort went astray in worldly life while they deem that they do good work. He said: They are the people of Harura. Abu Jafar said: They are those whom Ali fought over the interpretation of the Quran."
"4674","And as Ibrahim narrated to us — he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shubah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Musab ibn Sad, who said: I used to recite the mushaf to my father, may Allah be pleased with him, and came upon this verse: Say: Shall We inform you of the greatest losers in works — those whose effort went astray in worldly life while they deem that they do good work. He said: I said: Are they the Haruriyyah? He said: No; rather they are the disbelievers among the People of the Book: as for the Jews, they do not believe in Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); as for the Christians, they do not believe in Paradise, saying there is in it neither food nor drink. But His saying, Mighty and Majestic: Those who break the covenant of Allah after its ratification, and sever what Allah commanded to be joined, and spread corruption in the land — those are the Haruriyyah. Abu Jafar said: They are those mentioned in the interpretation of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. What was in the hadith we mentioned in the first chapter was a threat, and a threat — its author may carry it out or may not carry it out. What is in this hadith is a promise, and a promise must be fulfilled. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, fulfilled it for the one He promised it to upon the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Among what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning promise and threat is that they are as we mentioned."
"4675","4062 – As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us — he said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us — he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Hazm al-Qutai narrated to us — he said: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever Allah, Mighty and Majestic, promises a reward for a deed, He will fulfill it for him; and whomever He threatens with punishment for a deed, He has the choice in it. Abu Jafar said: And thus it is in the speech of the Arabs and with the people of language."
"4676","I heard Bakkar ibn Qutaybah mention, from al-Asma'i, who said: We were with Abu Amr ibn al-Ala when Amr ibn Ubayd came to him and said to him: O Abu Amr, is it permitted that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, promise a reward for a deed then not fulfill it? Abu Amr said: No. He said: Likewise, when He threatens punishment for a deed, it is not permitted that He not carry it out. Abu Amr said to him: You have come from the side of non-Arabs (ajm): the Arabs, when they promised, their honor was to fulfill; and when they threatened, their honor was not to fulfill. Abu Jafar said: I mentioned this hadith to Muhammad ibn Jafar, known as Ibn al-Imam, and he recognized it, saying: I heard it from Sawwar ibn Abd Allah al-Anbari, the judge, as you mentioned it to me from Bakkar — except that Sawwar added what is in it, from al-Asma'i, who said: Then Abu Amr turned to us and recited to us (verse). Someone said: Both hadiths were in fact about one meaning, and the description of the man he mentioned as the mender of the sandal indicates that — but the narrators did not fix it precisely, so they brought it as they brought it, which you, on that account, made into two different hadiths. Our answer to him regarding that, by the providence of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, is that the matter was not as he imagined: the narrators of both hadiths are upright in themselves, jurists in the religion of their Lord, established in the hadiths of their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), eloquent in their languages, knowing what they were addressed with, for they were addressed in their language, and they are the understanding ones in the matters of their religion, transmitting to us what they heard from their Prophet and from those who heard it from him, may Allah's pleasure be upon them. As for the mending of the sandal, it may well have been on two different days — and that is the worthiest construction of the reports, so that they do not contradict. Among what confirms the promise that was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith we mentioned in this chapter is what occurred in the affair of Dhu al-Khuwaysirah."
"4677","4063 – As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Ali ibn al-Mundhir al-Kufi al-Tariqi informed us — he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us — he said: Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi narrated to us, from his father, who said: I was sitting with Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man in travel clothes entered upon him, while Ali was speaking to the people and they speaking to him. He said: O Commander of the Believers, do you permit me to speak? He did not turn to him, so the man sat with me, and I asked him what his story was. He said: I was performing umrah when I met A'ishah, and she said to me: Are these people who came out in your land called Haruriyyah? I said: They came out in a place called Harura, so they were named by that. She said: Blessed is he who witnessed — meaning their destruction — had Ibn Abi Talib wished, he would have informed you of their story. So I came to ask him about their story. When Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, finished, he said: Where is the caller? He narrated to him as he had narrated to us. He said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with no one with him but A'ishah, Mother of the Believers. He said to me: O Ali, how will you fare with such-and-such people? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. Then he gestured with his hand toward a people emerging from the east, reciting the Quran without it passing their throats, passing through religion as the arrow passes through the game-animal. Among them is a man with a defective arm, as if his hand were a breast. I adjure you by Allah: did I inform you of them? They said: O Allah, yes. Then you came to me and informed me that he was not among them, so I swore to you by Allah, Mighty and Majestic, that he was among them. Then you came to me dragging him as I described to you. They said: Yes. He said: Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth."
"4678","4064 – And as Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Jafar al-Kufi narrated to us — he said: Ahmad ibn Imran al-Akhnasi narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"4679","4065 – And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us — he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us — he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abidah: that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Among them is the one with the defective hand — or the one with the fleshy hand, or the one with the short hand. They sought him among the slain but did not find him. He said: Were it not that you would exult, I would inform you of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, decreed upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for whoever kills these, knowing our guidance and discerning their misguidance."
"4680","4066 – And as Yazid narrated to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us — he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abidah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him — then he mentioned the like of it, with the addition: I said to him: Did you yourself hear this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, by the Lord of the Ka'bah — yes, by the Lord of the Ka'bah — yes, by the Lord of the Ka'bah."
"4681","4067 – As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Ismail ibn Mas'ud informed us — he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman informed us, from Awf, who said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us — he said: Abidah said — then he mentioned this hadith, with the addition: So we followed him and found him, and guided them to him. When he saw him, he said: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest."
"4682","4068 – And as Ahmad narrated to us — he said: al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Azim al-Anbari informed us — he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us — he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, who said: Zayd ibn Wahb narrated to us that they were in the army that was with Ali, those who marched against the Khawarij. Ali said: O people, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will emerge a people from my ummah reciting the Quran — your recitation is nothing beside their recitation, nor your prayer beside their prayer anything, nor your fasting beside their fasting anything. They recite the Quran deeming it for them, while it is against them; their prayer does not pass their throats. They pass through Islam as the arrow passes through the game-animal. If the army that strikes them knew what Allah decreed for them upon the tongue of their Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they would rely (and abandon work). The sign of that is that among them is a man with an upper arm but no forearm, upon the top of his upper arm like the nipple of a woman's breast, upon it white hairs. Salamah said: Zayd made me alight stage by stage, until he said: We passed over a bridge. When we met — the Khawarij that day being under Abd Allah ibn Wahb al-Rasibi — he said to them: Cast down the spears and draw your swords from their sheaths, for I fear they will adjure you. So they drew the swords and cast away their sheaths, and the people pierced them — meaning with their spears — and some of them were killed upon others, and of the people that day none was struck but two men. Ali said: Seek among them the defective one, but they did not find him. Then Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, stood himself until he came to people slain, some upon others. He said: Strip them. They found him on the side toward the ground. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Allah is Greatest, and said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, spoke the truth, and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conveyed. Then Abidah stood to him — then he mentioned the remainder of the hadith before this hadith."
"4683","4069 – And as Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us — he said: Ahmad ibn Jamil al-Marwazi narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Humayd ibn Abi Ghaniyyah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman — then he mentioned the hadith before this hadith."
"4684","4070 – And as Yunus narrated to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us — he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi: that when the Haruriyyah rebelled with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, they said: There is no judgment but Allah's. Ali said: A word of truth intended for falsehood. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) described people — I recognize their description in these: those who speak the truth with their tongues, without this of them passing — and he gestured to his throat — among the most hateful of Allah's creation, Mighty and Majestic, to Him; among them a black man, one of whose hands is like a sheep's udder or like a breast's nipple. When Ali fought them, he said: Look. They found nothing. He said: Return, for by Allah, I did not lie nor was I lied to — twice or thrice. Then they found him in a ruin, and brought him until they placed him before him. Ubayd Allah said: I am present at that affair of theirs and at the saying of Ali concerning them."
"4685","4071 – And as Yunus narrated to us — he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us — he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, who informed him, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as he was distributing a distribution of his, there came Dhu al-Khuwaysirah, a man from Banu Tamim, who said: O Messenger of Allah, be just. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woe to you — who will be just if I am not just? I have indeed failed and lost if I am not just. Umar ibn al-Khattab said: O Messenger of Allah, permit me regarding him to strike his neck. He said: Leave him, for he has companions before whose prayer one of you belittles his prayer, and his fasting beside their fasting. They recite the Quran without it passing their throats; they pass through Islam as the arrow passes through the game-animal. One looks at its head and finds nothing in it, then looks at its binding and finds nothing in it, then looks at its shaft — the reed — and finds nothing in it, then looks at its feathers and finds nothing in it; it outstripped the dung and the blood. Their sign is a black man, one of whose upper arms is like a woman's breast or like a piece of flesh, quivering. They emerge against the best faction of people. Abu Said said: I bear witness that I heard this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I bear witness that Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, fought them while I was with him. He ordered that man (sought), and he was searched for and brought, until I looked at him upon the description of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which he described."
"4686","4072 – And as al-Rabi' al-Muradi and Sulayman al-Kaysani narrated to us — they said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us — he said: al-Awzai narrated to us — he said: al-Zuhri narrated to me — he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Said — then he mentioned the like of it."
"4687","4073 – And as al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us — he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from al-Awzai, that he narrated to him, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik and from Abu Said al-Khudri: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be in my ummah disagreement and division, and a people who speak well and act badly, reciting the Quran without it passing their throats. One of you belittles his prayer beside their prayer and his fasting beside their fasting. They pass through Islam as the arrow passes through the game-animal, then do not return to it until it (the arrow) exceeds its notch. They are the worst of creation and creatures. Blessed is he who kills them and whom they kill. They call to the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, while they have nothing of it. Whoever fights them is worthier of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, than they are. They said: O Messenger of Allah, what is their mark? He said: Their mark is shaving. Then he narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also concerning the description of those who kill these people."
"4688","4074 – What Fahd narrated to us — he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us. (H) And what al-Husayn al-Hibri narrated to us — he said: Affan narrated to us — they said: al-Qasim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A renegade faction will secede at a division among the Muslims; the nearer of the two factions to the truth will kill them. Abu Jafar said: These are the people of interpretation whom Ali and his companions fought, over what they fought them — of what the promise of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning them had preceded with what preceded. This is among the special distinctions with which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, distinguished the Rightly-Guided, Guided caliphs of His Messenger, may Allah's pleasure be upon them: this was among the distinctions of Ali, who is of them, and belonged to none other of them. Just as among the distinctions of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him — who is of them — was what Allah distinguished him with: fighting the people of apostasy who sought to restore the affair of ignorance and efface what had come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Islam, until Allah annihilated them at his hand, and restored through him the Islam with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had been sent — and that belonged to none of the caliphs besides Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. And of that is what Allah distinguished Umar, may Allah be pleased with him — who is of them — with: fighting the non-Arabs, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, conquered at his hand what He made a stronghold for the Muslims, and what He made of it open land, and what He made for them of them by which they establish what they need to establish until the Day of Resurrection — and that did not occur at the hands of any of his companions besides him. And of that is what He distinguished Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him — who is of them — with: writing the mushafs and spreading them in the lands, until Allah gathered the people through him upon a single harf (reading), by which He established the proof, and made clear that whoever opposes a letter of it is a disbeliever. May Allah, Mighty and Majestic, protect us through him from being like the people of the two Books before us, who differed over their Book until it became possible for those of them who could, to alter it, and until they became equivalent in what they claim of disagreement over it. May Allah's pleasure be upon the caliphs of His Messenger, and His blessings and mercy. We ask Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to reward them on our behalf with the best with which He rewarded any of His prophets for obedience to Him. We praise Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for making us know their stations, their virtues, and their distinctions, and for not placing in our hearts rancor toward any of them, nor toward any besides them of the Companions of His Prophet, may Allah's pleasure be upon them. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for providence."
"4689","635 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated concerning the length of the period Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, spent with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the cave in which they hid, in time. 4075 – al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us — he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us — he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, from Talhah ibn Amr al-Nasri, who said: When one of us emigrated to Madinah, if he had a registrar (arif) he stayed with his registrar, and if he had no registrar he stayed with the people of the Bench (Suffah). I arrived in Madinah with no registrar there, so I stayed with the people of the Suffah, and I accompanied a man. There would come out for us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a mudd of dates between two men. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed one of his prayers, and when he gave the salutation, a man from the people of the Suffah called to him: O Messenger of Allah, the dates have burned our bellies and the garments are torn. He inclined toward the pulpit, praised Allah, Mighty and Majestic, extolled Him, mentioned what he met from his people of trial and hardship, then said: I and my companion spent a dozen-odd nights with no food for us but barir, until we came to our brothers of the Ansar, who shared their food with us — and their food is these dates. By Allah, besides whom there is no god, if I found bread and meat for you, I would feed you with them. It may be that you will live to a time — or whoever of you lives to it — in which you wear garments like the coverings of the Ka'bah, and bowls are brought to you morning and evening. Abu Jafar said: Abu Ubaydah Mamar ibn al-Muthanna said: The fruit of the arak tree is mard, then barir, then kabath. Abu Jafar said: As if — and Allah knows best — he means that it comes in stages, passing from some to others: at one time it is mard, at one time barir, at one time kabath — like the fruit of the date-palm: at one time balah, at one time busr, at one time rutab. In this hadith is the information of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the people that his stay and the stay of his companion with him in the cave in which they hid was a dozen-odd nights, and their food in them was the food mentioned in this hadith. In that is evidence of the severity of the hardship they met in that period. Someone said: But you have narrated concerning the stay of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the stay of his companion with him in the cave that it was less than this period mentioned in this hadith — that it was only three nights — and that they would obtain therein, from the messengers, of a milch-camel granted to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. And he mentioned regarding that."
"4690","4076 – What Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us — he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab — he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me: that A'ishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said in a long hadith whose beginning is: I never knew my parents but that they professed this religion — in it she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr reached a cave in a mountain called Thawr, and stayed in it three nights. Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr would spend the night with them — a sharp, perceptive young lad — then leave them before dawn and wake among Quraysh in Makkah as one who spent the night there, hearing no plot they plotted except that he retained it, until he brought them news of it when darkness mingled. And Amir ibn Fuhayrah, freedman of Abu Bakr, would pasture for them a milch-camel and return it to them, and they would spend the night in the fresh milk of their milch-camel and its warm milk, until Amir ibn Fuhayrah drove them away in the dark, doing that every night of those three nights. He said: The hadith of al-Bara ibn Azib which you narrate regarding that confirms it."
"4691","4077 – Then he mentioned what al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us — he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us — he said: My father and others narrated to me, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came and bought a saddle from Azib for thirteen dirhams. Abu Bakr said to Azib: Tell al-Bara to carry it to my saddle. He said: No — until you narrate to me how you and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fared when you two set out with the idolaters seeking you. Abu Bakr said: We set out from Makkah by night with the people lying in ambush for us. We hid our day and our night and our day until the midday stander stood. I cast my glance: do I see any shade to shelter in? A rock came into our view; we set out to it, and it had some shade. We alighted; I looked at the remainder of its shade and leveled it, took a fur I had with me, and spread it for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then I said: O Messenger of Allah, lie down until I inspect what is around you. A shepherd boy came with sheep of his, seeking from the rock the like of what we sought. I said: Whose are you, boy? He said: A man of Quraysh — and he named him, and I knew him. I said: Is there any milk in your sheep? He said: Yes. I said: Will you milk for us? He said: Yes. I gave him a vessel I had; he took to milking, and I said: Shake the dust off the sheep's udder. Then I ordered him to shake his hands, and he said thus, striking one of his hands upon the other. Then he milked for me a good amount of milk. I had brought with me for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a water-skin with a cloth upon its mouth; I poured upon the milk until I felt the cold of the water beneath the vessel. I brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found him awake. I said: Drink, O Messenger of Allah. He drank. He said: I said: The time of departure has come. We departed with the people seeking us, and none overtook us but Suraqah ibn Malik ibn Ju'shum upon a horse of his. I said: This pursuer has caught up with us, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Grieve not; Allah is with us. When he drew near, within two or three spear-lengths, I said: This pursuer has caught up with us — and I wept. He said: What makes you weep? I said: By Allah, I weep not for myself; I weep only for you, O Messenger of Allah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against him, saying: O Allah, suffice us against him as You will. His horse sank into the ground up to its belly; he leapt off it, then said: O Muhammad, I know this is your doing; supplicate Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to deliver me from what I am in. By Allah, I shall blind those behind me of the pursuers. This is my quiver — take an arrow from it; you will pass by my sheep and camels in such-and-such place; take from them your need. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We have no need of your camels. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for him, and he set off returning to his companions, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on, and I with him."
"4692","4078 – And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us — he said: Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us — he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib — then he mentioned the like of it. This speaker said: This is severe confusion and far-reaching disagreement. Our answer to him regarding that — by the providence of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help — is that there is neither confusion nor disagreement in that, and all these reports are sound, given the uprightness of their narrators and the excellence of their transmission. It may be that each group — of Talhah ibn Amr, of A'ishah, and of al-Bara — informed of a cave other than the cave the other group of them informed of: the stay of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his companion, may Allah be pleased with him, in each of the two being other than his stay in the other of the two. The stay of him with his companion in one of them is strengthened by the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in His Book: If you do not aid him, Allah has already aided him, when those who disbelieved expelled him, the second of two, when they were in the cave, when he said to his companion: Grieve not; Allah is with us. Then what has been narrated from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, of what he feared for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then for himself, in one of the two caves in which he was with him — of his looking at the feet of the idolaters above that cave, and of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) thereupon, what he said to him there."
"4693","4079 – And what Abd al-Aziz ibn Muawiyah al-Attabi and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq together narrated to us — they said: Hibban ibn Hilal narrated to us — he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us. 4080 – And as Yazid ibn Sinan, al-Husayn ibn Nasr, Nassar ibn Harb, Muhammad ibn al-Ward ibn Zanjawayh al-Baghdadi, and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah al-Kufi narrated to us — they said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us. (H) And as Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn al-Harith al-Baghandi also narrated to us — he said: Affan narrated to us — he said: Hammam narrated to us. 4081 – And as Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Awqi and Musa ibn Ismail al-Minqari narrated to us — they said: Hammam narrated to us — then they all agreed, saying: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us — he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to us: that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him, saying: I looked at the feet of the idolaters while they were above our heads and we in the cave. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, if one of them looked beneath his foot, he would see us beneath his foot. He said: O Abu Bakr, what do you think of two, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, being the third of them?"
"4694","4082 – And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us — he said: Hibban ibn Hilal and Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us — they said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. Abu Jafar said: In that is evidence of the severity of the hardship they were in, and of the fear of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the idolaters, and of his shielding him with himself from what he shielded him with thereupon, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, delivered His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and delivered him with him, to the land of his emigration, which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made a stronghold for His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and for his Companions, may Allah's pleasure be upon them, a place of emigration. He distinguished its people with emigration to them, and with support from them for him; with the Garden (Rawdah) which He placed between his grave and his pulpit, of the gardens of Paradise; with the revelation descending upon him among them; with the mixing of the best of His angels — Allah's blessings upon them — with them, by their descending upon him from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, with what He sent them with to him, and what He sent down upon him of His Quran and His revelation. May the blessings of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, His mercy, and His benedictions be upon His Messenger, the best of the first and the last, the imam of the God-fearing; then His mercy and benedictions upon Abu Bakr, his companion, may Allah be pleased with him — for what came from him concerning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), seeking His Face and seeking what is with Him — until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, honored him by mentioning him in His Book with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in what He mentioned him with with him in it, and in what He distinguished him by from his Companions besides him, may Allah's pleasure be upon him and upon them. In what we mentioned is what negates what this ignorant one supposed — for deficiency of his knowledge and understanding — of confusion in the reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their disagreement; it is evidence of their harmony and of the absence of disagreement and contradiction from them. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for providence."
"4695","636 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated concerning the sleeping of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in the place of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and his wearing his cloak, on the night in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out from Makkah intending the land of emigration. 4083 – Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna informed us — he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to me — he said: al-Waddah — who is Abu Awanah — narrated to us — he said: Abu Balj — who is Yahya ibn Abi Sulaym — narrated to us — he said: Amr ibn Maymun narrated to us, saying: I was sitting with Ibn Abbas when nine men came to him and asked him about Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: He was the first of the people to embrace Islam after Khadijah, may Allah be pleased with her. He wore the garment of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and slept, while the idolaters pelted as they pelted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), deeming him to be the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came and said: O Prophet of Allah. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has gone toward the well of Maymun; follow him. So he followed him and entered the cave with him. The idolaters kept pelting Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, until morning. We examined this hadith and found in it the wearing by Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, of the garment of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his sleeping in it, what came to him from the idolaters while they deemed him the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his bearing that, and his persistence in it. It is possible that was by the command of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him, and it is possible that his doing that was of his own act, without a command from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him, to be a cause for the distancing of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Makkah, and for the idolaters falling short of overtaking him. We looked into that: do we find anything indicating to us the reality of the affair as it was?"
"4696","4084 – So we found Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us — he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Balj, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me when he set out — meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stationed him in his place and clothed him in his cloak. Quraysh came intending to kill the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and began pelting Ali while deeming him the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), having clothed him in his cloak. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, began to writhe, so they looked — and behold, it was Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. They said: He is indeed in pain; had he been your companion, he would not have writhed. We indeed deemed that strange. We understood from what is in this hadith that the wearing by Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, of the shirt of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his sleeping in his place were by the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing that with him; that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, supposed, upon seeing Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, where he saw him, that he was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him what he said to him, informing him of the place the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had headed to; that that could not have been from Ali except by the command of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to him with it; his informing Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, of it, that he join him to the place he had headed to; what came from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, after that ceased; Abu Bakr alone had the companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), entering into the fear in which he was, and bearing the hardship to which they came; what came from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, of what we mentioned from him, was only part of a night; while what came from Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was — upon what is in the hadith of A'ishah which we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter — three nights, and in the hadith of Talhah a dozen-odd nights, and bid' is from three to ten — so the total of that was sixteen nights or more, in which Abu Bakr was upon what he was upon of companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of shielding him with himself, and of the fear and hardship they were upon, until they arrived at the land of emigration. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, distinguished Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, for that, with mention in His Book with His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), singling him out with that apart from the rest of his companions. He informed them, Mighty and Majestic, that he had been in that period with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then with Abu Bakr, may Allah's pleasure be upon him. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for providence."
"4697","637 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: When two Muslims meet with their swords, the killer and the killed are in the Fire — and what came from Abu Bakrah of his addressing al-Ahnaf with it when he addressed him with it on its account. 4085 – Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — he said: Mu'ammal ibn Ismail narrated to us — he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub and Yunus, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, from Abu Bakrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When two Muslims meet with their swords, the killer and the killed are in the Fire."
"4698","4086 – Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Shubah, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from Abu Bakrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When two Muslims bear arms, one against his companion, they are upon the brink of the Fire; if one of them kills his companion, they both enter it. We sought the meaning on account of which Abu Bakrah brought this hadith."
"4699","4087 – So we found Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us — he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us — he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub and Yunus, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, who said: I took my weapon intending to aid the cousin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when Abu Bakrah met me and said: Where do you intend? I said: To aid the cousin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. I said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When two Muslims face one another with their swords and one of them kills his companion, they are both in the Fire. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, this is the killer — but what of the killed? He said: He intended to kill his companion. We examined this hadith: it is possible that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him — since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that he would fight over the interpretation of the Quran as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fought over its revelation — knew thereby that that would not be from him except while he was a caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it. He sought the station by which he would meet fighting those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised him he would fight. Talhah and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, may not have learned of that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning Ali, may Allah be pleased with him; Ali may not, in their view, have been worthier of authority over the affair of this ummah than each of them; they knew that people must have one to manage their affairs — to fight their enemy behind them, to perform what only their imams perform of their prayers, of placing their zakats where they must be placed, of leading them in hajj, of dividing their fay among them, of establishing other matters of their religion besides that, which only their imams perform. So they fought him for that. He had with him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a designation in that worthier than one with nothing like that — with him only what his exertion and striving lead him to. They were only bound, in what they were upon, by what their path imposed. Each faction — of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and of them, may Allah's pleasure be upon them — fought upon what fighting was his. Whoever fought with each faction of those two factions, upon what that faction fought for, is not blamed for that; rather he is praised for it. What came from Abu Bakrah to al-Ahnaf ibn Qays was not by way of forbidding him what he intended; rather he alerted him that what he intended — of what the striving of the one he intended to fight with had led him to, without his learning of what had come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, of what he called the people to and fought them over, which is above striving and exertion — and whoever fights upon striving and exertion may be overtaken by insight cutting him off from fighting and obliging him to abandon it. He feared for him lest that overtake him while the zeal that had entered him through fighting cuts him off, so he persist in his fighting and enter the category he narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Arabs use this; of that is what came in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, of the saying of one of the two sons of Adam to his companion: If you stretch your hand toward me to kill me, I shall not stretch my hand toward you to kill you; I fear Allah, Lord of the worlds. He could have stretched his hand to him to repel him from himself when he intended killing him, but he feared that his companion would turn back from what he intended while he himself persist in defending himself until his companion perishes by what he does to him — so he feared Allah, Mighty and Majestic, on that account. Likewise is the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O Allah, this is my division in what I own; blame me not for what You own and I own not — with his knowledge (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not take him to account for what he owns not — but upon guarding against excess in what he owns not until he enter thereby into what he owns. Of that is his teaching Husayn al-Khuzai to have among his supplication: Forgive me what I erred in and what I intended — while he knows that Allah does not take him to account for what he erred in, for He said, Mighty and Majestic, in His Book: There is no blame upon you for what you erred in, but what your hearts intended. What came from Abu Bakrah to al-Ahnaf was an alerting of him to what is feared for him. The turning away of al-Ahnaf was upon apprehension from it, given his knowledge of himself and of the character he is upon. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for providence."
"4700","638 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning what he commanded the people to adhere to of remembrance after the obligatory prayers. 4088 – al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us — he said: Asad narrated to us — he said: Sulayman ibn Hayyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Abundant good, yet few who do it: after every prescribed prayer, ten takbirs, ten tasbihs, and ten tahmids — that is one hundred fifty by the tongue and one thousand five hundred in the Balance. When he lays his side down, he glorifies Allah, Mighty and Majestic, thirty-three times, praises Allah, Mighty and Majestic, thirty-three times, and magnifies forty-four (thirty-four) times — that is one hundred by the tongue and one thousand in the Balance. So which of you commits in a day and night two thousand five hundred evil deeds?"
"4701","4089 – And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Yamani Abu Hummah narrated to us — he said: Abu Qurrah narrated to us, from Zam'ah ibn Salih, from Ziyad ibn Sa'd, from Aban — who is Ibn Salih — who said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to me, from his father: that Abd Allah ibn Umar — or Amr — informed him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Two traits no Muslim servant guards in his day and night but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, admits him to Paradise — they are easy, yet few guard them. They said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The servant glorifies after every prayer ten times, praises ten times, and declares unity ten times — that is thirty, and they are one hundred fifty in his day and night, and with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, one thousand five hundred good deeds. And he glorifies thirty-three tasbihs, praises thirty-three tahmids, and magnifies thirty-four takbirs. Abu Jafar said: As if he means — at his sleep: that is one hundred, and with Allah, Mighty and Majestic, one thousand good deeds — so that is two thousand five hundred. Let none of you suppose he incurs in his day and night two thousand five hundred evil deeds. They said: O Messenger of Allah, why do we not guard that? He said: When one of you comes to his prayer, Satan comes and reminds him of his needs, so he rises before saying them; and when he retires to his bed, he comes to him and distracts him until he sleeps."
"4702","4090 – Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us — he said: Abu Bakr al-Nahshali narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two traits no Muslim combines but he enters Paradise — they are easy, yet few who practice them: he glorifies ten times after every prayer, praises ten times, and magnifies ten times. When he retires to his bed at night, he glorifies thirty-three times, praises thirty-three times, and magnifies thirty-four times — those are two hundred fifty good deeds; when multiplied, they are two thousand five hundred. So which of you commits in his day and night two thousand five hundred evil deeds?"
"4703","4091 – And Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us — he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us — he said: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us — he said: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Two traits no man enumerates but he enters Paradise — they are easy, yet few who practice them. We said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The five prayers: he glorifies, praises, and magnifies after every prayer ten times. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counting them upon his hand — those are one hundred fifty by the tongue and one thousand five hundred in the Balance. When he takes his resting-place, he glorifies, praises, and magnifies — those are one hundred upon the tongue and one thousand in the Balance. So which of you commits in a single day two thousand five hundred evil deeds? They said: Every one of us, O Messenger of Allah, enumerates them. He said: Satan comes to one of you in his prayer and says: Remember the need of such-and-such — so he prays, and perhaps he does not glorify; and he comes to him while he is in his resting-place and puts him to sleep, and perhaps he does not glorify. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith of Abu Qurrah is the seeing of Abd Allah ibn Amr of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counting the tasbih — and he (others) agreed with him upon that."
"4704","4092 – What Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Qudamah narrated to us — he said: Aththam ibn Ali narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counting the tasbih."
"4705","4093 – Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Rifa'i, who is Abu Hisham, said — he said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us — he said: Ata narrated to us, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said — then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu Qurrah, except that he did not mention in it his seeing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) counting the tasbih, nor the preoccupying of Satan of the people from that."
"4706","Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us — he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us — he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, saying: Ata ibn al-Sa'ib came to us in Basrah, and Ayyub said to us: Go to him and ask him about the hadith of the tasbih. Al-Qawariri said: Meaning the hadith of his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr."
"4707","And Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us — he said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, saying: We prayed Asr with Hammad ibn Zayd, and the companions of hadith crowded upon him. He said to them: I have narrated to you the hadith of Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, concerning the tasbih — so which of you acted upon it? I bear witness: I shall not narrate to you for a month."
"4708","4094 – And Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us — he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us — he said: Hamzah al-Zayyat narrated to us, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Three things whose sayer after every prayer is not disappointed: thirty-three tahmids, thirty-three tasbihs, and thirty-four takbirs."
"4709","4095 – And Abu Umayyah narrated to us — he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us — he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Following (remembrances) whose sayer — or doer — is not disappointed — then he mentioned the like of it."
"4710","Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us — he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b, the like of it — and he did not raise it (to the Prophet)."
"4711","4096 – Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Samurah informed us, from Asbat — he said: Amr ibn Qays narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4712","Yunus narrated to us — he said: Asad narrated to us — he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla say: I heard Ka'b ibn Ujrah — then he mentioned the like of it and did not raise it. Al-Hakam said: I have not abandoned them since. He said: In this hadith is contrary to what is in the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Amr, in the number of things commanded after the prayers. Then we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — of what came from him after what Ka'b narrated from him — what returned the measures of the numbers in that, after the prayers and at sleep, to it."
"4713","4097 – As Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Musa ibn Hizam al-Tirmidhi narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Adam informed me, from Ibn Idris, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Kathir ibn Aflah, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: They were commanded to glorify after every prayer thirty-three times, praise thirty-three times, and magnify thirty-four times. A man of the Ansar was visited in his dream and told: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command you to glorify after every prayer thirty-three times, praise thirty-three times, and magnify thirty-four times? He said: Yes. He said: Then make them twenty-five, and include the tahlil in them. In the morning he mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Make them so. The worthiest thing for the practitioner to adopt, of the number after the prayers, is what is in this hadith of Ahmad — for it is what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded after what is in the hadith of Ka'b of what he had commanded."
"4714","Some people disliked counting the tasbih — among them Abu Hanifah and his companions: as Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated to us, from Ali ibn Ma'bad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Ya'qub, from Abu Hanifah, to that effect. Abd Allah ibn Umar preceded them in what they said of that."
"4715","Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us — he said: Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh al-Anbari narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Uqbah ibn Suhban, who said: I said to Ibn Umar: A man glorifies and counts what he glorifies. He said: Glory be to Allah — do you reckon with Allah? Abu Jafar said: I say: every matter the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded, having a number, which cannot be fixed precisely except by counting the tasbih — counting in that is included in his command and urged to be practiced, so its doer knows he has earned the promise of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, which He promised the doers of that upon it. Every matter he commanded without mentioning a number in it — employing counting in it has no meaning; rather its employment is grave, as Abd Allah ibn Umar deemed it grave. And Allah we ask for providence."
"4716","639 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what he commanded whoever intends sleep to say at his sleep. Abu Jafar said: We have mentioned in the chapter before this chapter what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this meaning. 4098 – And Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us — he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us — he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrate, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him: that Fatimah came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining to him of the trace of the millstone upon her hand. It had reached her that captives had come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so she came to him asking him for a servant, but did not meet him; A'ishah met her, and she informed her of the hadith. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, she informed him of that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us after we had taken our resting-places, and we went to rise, but he said: Stay in your place. He sat between us until I felt the cold of his foot upon my chest, and said: Shall I not guide you to better than what you asked? You magnify Allah thirty-four times, glorify thirty-three times, and praise thirty-three times, when you take your resting-places; it is better for you than a servant."
"4717","4099 – al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us — meaning from Asad — he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: that he said to Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, one day: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has brought your father abundance and slaves; go to him and seek a servant from him. She came to him and mentioned that to him, and he said: By Allah, I shall not give them to you while I leave the people of the Suffah with their bellies folded with hunger, finding nothing to spend upon them; rather I shall sell them and spend upon them. Shall I not guide you to better than what you asked, which Jibril, peace be upon him, taught me? You magnify after every prayer ten times, glorify ten times, and praise ten times; and when you retire to your bed — then he mentioned what is in the hadith of Sulayman which we mentioned before it. Abu Jafar said: In what we mentioned in the chapter before this chapter is what suffices us from speech in this chapter. And Allah we ask for providence."
"4718","640 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the reward of whoever memorizes the first ten verses of the surah Qad aflaha al-mu'minun (The Believers). 4100 – Jafar ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us — he said: Ishaq Ibn Rahawayh narrated to us — he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us — he said: Yunus ibn Sulaym narrated to us, saying: Yunus ibn Yazid, who is al-Ayli, dictated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari, who said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, say: When revelation descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a humming like the humming of bees would be heard at him. We waited a while; he faced the qiblah, raised his hands, and said: O Allah, increase us and diminish us not; honor us and disgrace us not; deprive us not; prefer us and prefer not over us; be pleased with us and please us. Then he said: There have been sent down upon me ten verses — whoever establishes them enters Paradise. Then he recited: Qad aflaha al-mu'minun — those who are humble in their prayers."
"4719","4101 – And Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain, except that he said, in place of deprive us not: disgrace us not. 4102 – And Ahmad narrated it to us another time, saying in it as Jafar said in it: deprive us not. Abu Jafar said: This Yunus ibn Sulaym is a man of the people of Sana'a; we know of no one who narrated from him besides Abd al-Razzaq, nor do we know him to have narrated from him except with this hadith. The eminent among those who took knowledge from him narrated this hadith from Abd al-Razzaq — among them Ahmad ibn Hanbal, and among them Ishaq ibn Rahawayh. Someone said: This hadith brings an impossible meaning, for the fasting of Ramadan and the hajj of the House are not mentioned in the verses recited in it — while we know that whoever meets Allah, Mighty and Majestic, abandoning the fast of the month of Ramadan while able, and abandoning the hajj of the House while finding a way to it, does not enter Paradise. Our answer to him regarding that, by the providence of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and His help, is that what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what is mentioned from him in this hadith, may have been before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down the obligation of fasting the month of Ramadan upon those He obligated it upon, and the obligation of hajj upon those He obligated it upon. Whoever brought the obligations of Allah upon him besides those two would be entitled to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed, from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, that He does with whoever practices that. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, obligated upon His servants the fasting of the month of Ramadan and the hajj of the House, upon what He obligated upon them — so they joined the obligations imposed upon people before them. Those promised with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in this hadith came back to being those who discharged all the obligations of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, upon them — in which are the fasting of the month of Ramadan, the hajj of the House, and the rest of what Allah obligated upon them besides that. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for providence."
"4720","641 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning what is volunteered after the Friday prayer of bowing, in the place in which it is prayed. 4103 – Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us — Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever of you prays after Friday, let him pray four."
"4721","4104 – Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us — al-Firyabi narrated to us — Sufyan, meaning al-Thawri, narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"4722","4105 – Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us — he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"4723","4106 – Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us. (H) And Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us — he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us — they said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"4724","4107 – Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us — Ma'mar narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"4725","4108 – Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us — Yusuf al-Saffar narrated to us — he said: Ubayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us — Abyad ibn Aban al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays, let him pray four before Friday and four after it. Ubayd said: I said to Abyad: Sufyan narrated it to me, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays after Friday, let him pray four. He said: That is what Sufyan heard, and this is what I heard. Indeed I took the book of Suhayl. Abu Jafar said: This Ubayd ibn Sa'id is al-Umawi. I heard Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud say: Yahya ibn Ma'in said: The sons of Sa'id al-Umawi are five: Anbasah ibn Sa'id, Yahya ibn Sa'id, Muhammad ibn Sa'id, Ubayd ibn Sa'id, and Abd Allah ibn Sa'id. They were all in Baghdad except Ubayd ibn Sa'id. He was among the eldest of them; he narrated from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, and none wrote his books from him. He was a man of authority — he and his brother Abd Allah. Abu Jafar said: In these reports is the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to whoever prays after Friday to pray four. Someone said: But you have narrated, of the hadith of al-Thawri from Suhayl with this chain, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he prayed Friday, prayed after it two rak'ahs, then four. And he mentioned regarding that."
"4726","4109 – What Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us — Nuh ibn Habib al-Qumasi narrated to us — Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Friday, he prayed after it two rak'ahs, then prayed four. Our answer to him regarding that is that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the people with, of what we narrated in this hadith, may be what he commanded them to pray in the mosque, without leaving it for other than it of their houses and besides them; while what he did, of what is in this hadith of al-Harawi, was because it was from him in his house after leaving the mosque. Of what indicates that."
"4727","4110 – What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — Arim Abu al-Nu'man narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar saw a man praying two rak'ahs after Friday, pushed him, and said: Do you pray Friday as four? He said: Ibn Umar would pray the two rak'ahs in his house, saying: Thus did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4728","4111 – And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us — Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us — Abd al-Aziz al-Qasmali narrated to us — Abd Allah ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: that when he prayed Friday, he left for his house and prayed two prostrations (rak'ahs). Ibn Umar mentioned that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that."
"4729","4112 – And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us with — Mansur ibn Salamah al-Khuzai narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar — then he mentioned the like of it exactly. Abu Jafar said: Thereby we learned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray these two rak'ahs after Friday in his house, not in the mosque; that Ibn Umar followed that after him, emulating him in it — praying them in his house, not in the mosque, after leaving the mosque. It was of his sunnah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning one who prayed a prayer of the five prayers then intended to volunteer after it in the mosque in which he prayed it, not to do that until he moves forward or speaks."
"4730","4113 – As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Umar ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar: that Nafi' ibn Jubayr sent him to al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, asking him what he heard from Mu'awiyah concerning prayer after Friday. He said: I prayed Friday with Mu'awiyah in the Maqsurah; when I finished, I stood to volunteer, and he seized my garment and said: Do not do it until you move forward or speak, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commands that."
"4731","4114 – And as Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us — Abu al-Ashhab Hawdhah ibn Khalifah al-Bakrawi narrated to us — Ibn Jurayj narrated to us — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. Each of moving forward and speaking permitted him to pray whatever volunteering he wished in the mosque, after the obligatory prayer he prayed in it. What is in the hadith of Ibn Umar does not permit him that in the mosque, but permits it in his house after leaving the mosque for it. The validation of these two meanings from these reports is that what the hadith of Ibn Umar forbade is volunteering after Friday with two rak'ahs resembling Friday in its number; the Friday-prayer is intended to pray them elsewhere than the mosque in which he prayed that Friday — as one intending the mosque for the dawn prayer is commanded to pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr in his house, then pray the Fajr prayer in the mosque after that. It was said to one who prayed them in the mosque before the Fajr prayer: Do you pray them as four? We shall mention that after this chapter, and what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it, if Allah wills. What is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah — of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever prays after Friday, let him pray after it four — is upon four, unlike Friday in form, for they are four rak'ahs while Friday is two rak'ahs. He permitted him to volunteer after Friday in the mosque in which he prayed it with what is unlike it — four rak'ahs and above — after there is from him the speaking or moving forward mentioned in the hadith of Mu'awiyah which we mentioned. Likewise volunteering before it was set for him, upon what is in the hadith of Muhammad ibn Ali which we narrated in this chapter. The validation of the meanings of these reports comes back to permitting volunteering after Friday in the mosque in which he prays, with what does not resemble Friday in its number, and forbidding praying in the mosque after it its like — commanding that be from him after leaving it, elsewhere of houses or besides them. These are sunnahs of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); whoever learns them and understands them must praise Allah for what He granted him of that. Ali ibn Abi Talib and Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, after the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), taught the people to pray after Friday."
"4732","As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us — he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us — he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, who said: Abd Allah came to us and would pray four after Friday. Then Ali came after him, and when he prayed Friday, prayed after it two rak'ahs and four. The saying of Ali pleased us, and we chose it."
"4733","And what Yunus narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami — then he mentioned the like of it. He said: What we narrated from Ibn Mas'ud, of what he would pray after Friday, was four rak'ahs in the mosque and outside the mosque, since they were unlike Friday in form. What we narrated from Ali — that he would pray after it six unrestrictedly — in the mosque and outside the mosque: it is possible he would pray the four first, then pray the two rak'ahs after them; and possible he would pray the two rak'ahs first, then pray after them the four. Worthier for us is to set what he would pray first of these two kinds as the four, then the two rak'ahs — for the four are unlike Friday, while the two rak'ahs are like it. The mention of the two rak'ahs before the mention of the four in the hadith does not prevent the narrator intending that he prayed the four before them — for they are Arabs, and the Arabs use this in their speech: mentioning two things, putting the mention of one before the other, while the latter in mention was earlier in act than the former in mention. That exists in the Book of Allah, Exalted: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: O Mary, be devout to your Lord, prostrate, and bow with those who bow — mentioning bowing last, while in the prayers the Muslims pray, and in the prayers the People of the Book prayed before them, it precedes prostration. Likewise is the saying of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in the verses of inheritance: After a bequest they bequeath or a debt — and: After a bequest you bequeath or a debt — and: After a bequest bequeathed or a debt: the mention of debt in them is last after the mention of the bequest, while intended in them is that it precede the bequest. Like that is what we narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning his praying the two rak'ahs and the four after the Friday prayer: the narrator's mentioning the two rak'ahs before his mentioning the four does not prevent the four being intended as preceding the two mentioned before them — so these reports confirm one another and do not contradict one another. Of what strengthens putting the four before the two rak'ahs in this meaning is what has been narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4734","As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us — Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Sulayman ibn Mushir, from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr: that Umar would dislike praying after a prayer its like. Abu Jafar said: The two rak'ahs are like Friday, while the four are not like it. For this meaning — and Allah knows best — it was permitted in the hadith of al-Abyad ibn Aban from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, concerning volunteering before Friday, that it be four, since unlike Friday in its number. That was contrasted with volunteering before the dawn prayer: that was not permitted in the mosque, since two rak'ahs resembling the dawn prayer — commanded to be in the houses, unlike the place in which the dawn prayer is prayed, so there be between them what separates them of the two different places. And Allah we ask for providence."
"4735","642 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the place in which he prays the two rak'ahs of Fajr — of the mosque or of the houses. 4115 – Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us — Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Nasa'i narrated to us — Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us — Salih ibn Rustum narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man praying after the prayer had been commenced, seized his garment, pulled him, and said: Do you pray the morning prayer as four?"
"4736","4116 – Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us — Ya'qub ibn Ka'b al-Halabi narrated to us — Makhlad ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Buhaynah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out for the dawn prayer while Ibn al-Qishb was praying. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck his shoulder and said: O Ibn al-Qishb, do you intend to pray the dawn prayer as four — or twice? Makhlad doubted."
"4737","4117 – al-Hasan ibn Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Marwazi narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Hamzah al-Zubayri narrated to us — Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad informed us, from Sharik ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out when the dawn prayer was commenced and saw people praying the two rak'ahs of Fajr. He said: Two prayers together?"
"4738","4118 – Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us — Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Yazid, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman, that he said: I met all the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except those killed on the day of Uhud, and heard them mention that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the mosque while Talhah was praying, with the prayer commenced, and said: Two prayers, O Talhah?"
"4739","4119 – And Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us — Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us — Hammad narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Hafs ibn Asim, from Malik ibn Buhaynah, that he said: The Fajr prayer was commenced, and the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, came upon a man praying the two rak'ahs of Fajr. He stood over him while the people crowded around him, and said: Do you pray them as four — three times."
"4740","4120 – Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain, except that he did not say: the people crowded around him."
"4741","4121 – Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain, except that he did not say: three times. We have mentioned in the chapter before this chapter of this book of ours what suffices us from repeating it in this chapter. And with Allah is providence."
"4742","643 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: When the prayer is commenced, there is no prayer but the prescribed one. 4122 – Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — Abu Asim narrated to us, from Zakariyya ibn Ishaq, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar — Abu Jafar said: Thus he said — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When the prayer is commenced, there is no prayer but the prescribed one."
"4743","4123 – And Abu Umayyah narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us — Warqa ibn Umar al-Yashkuri narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace be upon him, the like of it."
"4744","4124 – And Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us — he said: Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us — he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Warqa, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4745","4125 – Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Firyabi narrated to us — Huraym ibn Mis'ar al-Azdi al-Tirmidhi narrated to us — al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Sa'd, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4746","4126 – Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us — Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4747","4127 – Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati narrated to us — Abu Mus'ab al-Zuhri narrated to us — Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn Mujammi' al-Ansari, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"4748","4128 – And Fahd narrated to us — Abu Salih Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us — al-Layth narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Ayyash al-Qitbani, from his father, from Abu Tamim, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When the prayer is commenced, there is no prayer but the one for which it was commenced."
"4749","4129 – And Abu Qurrah Muhammad ibn Humayd al-Ruayni narrated to us — Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. He said: We examined this hadith in its chains, since some of its narrators — I mean of the hadith of Amr ibn Dinar — stopped it at Abu Hurayrah and did not raise it to the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him — among them Sufyan ibn Uyaynah."
"4750","As Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar: he heard Abu Hurayrah say — then he mentioned the like of it and did not raise it. Among them are Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd."
"4751","As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — he said: Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us — he said: Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, the like of it — and they did not raise it."
"4752","And as Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — Humayd ibn Mas'adah narrated to us — Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, the like of it — and he did not raise it."
"4753","And as Ishaq narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Ishkab narrated to us — Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us — Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Amr, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, the like of it — and he did not raise it. Hammad said: Ayyub used to raise it, from Amr ibn Dinar. We sought the reality of the affair in that, and found this hadith of Ata ibn Yasar revolves upon Amr ibn Dinar; we found Amr ibn Dinar narrated from in it."
"4754","4130 – What Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the prayer is commenced, there is no prayer but the prescribed one. Sa'id said: I said to Sufyan: Is it raised? He said: Amr deems it raised. The hadith of Amr ibn Dinar comes back to being doubted — is it raised or not raised? Thereby it is negated that there be in it a proof in this chapter. We found in this chapter nothing probative in it, undoubted in its raising, besides the hadith of Abu Qurrah and Fahd which we narrated from them, from Abd Allah ibn Salih, in this chapter. There has also been narrated in this chapter, from Amr ibn Dinar, besides the hadith of those we narrated from him, also."
"4755","4131 – What Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us — Dawud ibn Amr al-Dabbi narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: When the prayer is commenced, there is no prayer but the prescribed one. What we narrated from Amr ibn Dinar — of doubt in it, whether it is raised or not raised — repels this hadith also from being a proof in it. There has also been narrated in this chapter another hadith going back to Ibn Umar."
"4756","4132 – As Abu Umayyah narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Dimashqi narrated to us — Abd Allah ibn Marwan al-Dimashqi narrated to us — he was trustworthy — from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the prayer is commenced, there is no prayer but the prescribed one — except that we are not satisfied, in the like of this, with the accreditation of one who accredited this man whom we do not know, among those who narrated this hadith. In what preceded from us, in the chapter before this chapter, was the forbidding by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the people praying the two rak'ahs of Fajr in the mosque in which the Fajr prayer is prayed, before the prayer. Since that is so, they are — after the prayer is commenced — more emphatically forbidden in the mosque. Thereby we were sufficed from what we narrated in this chapter of these doubted reports, and we must hold to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us in the two rak'ahs of Fajr: to pray them in our houses before coming to the mosque for the Fajr prayer, until we pray it in it. That is in our view — and Allah knows best — so long as no necessity intervenes between us and praying the two rak'ahs of Fajr in our houses, until we come to the mosque. For we saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he slept past the dawn prayer until the sun rose, pray — when prayer became lawful for him, after the adhan of Bilal for it — the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then pray the Fajr prayer. That was from him in a single place, for he had then no house there. That indicates the permissibility of praying them in the place in which the Fajr prayer is prayed, upon the like of this necessity calling to that. We mentioned what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, in what preceded from us in this book of ours. We found from more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed them in the mosque after the Fajr prayer was commenced, for a necessity calling him to that — among them Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, in the presence of Hudhayfah and Abu Musa for that, who did not disapprove of him — indicating their agreement with him upon it."
"4757","As Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us — Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us — Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq — Abd Allah ibn Abi Musa narrated to us, from his father, when Sa'id ibn al-As invited them: he invited Abu Musa, Hudhayfah, and Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud before praying the morning prayer, and asked them: How is the Eid prayer prayed? Abd Allah answered him with what he answered him with in it. Then they left him with the prayer commenced; Abd Allah sat by a pillar of the mosque, prayed the two rak'ahs, then entered the prayer. That was — and Allah knows best — upon the necessity calling him to that, since it is possible Sa'id invited them at night and the affair extended with them at his place to a time they did not suppose the affair would extend with them at his place to — so necessity called him to pray those two rak'ahs at that time in that place, out of dislike that they escape him, given what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged them upon them, and what he informed them of merit for them in them."
"4758","4133 – As Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us — Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us — Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Sa'd ibn Hisham, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two rak'ahs of Fajr are better than the world and what is in it."
"4759","4134 – And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us — Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Qunfudh, from Ibn Silan, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not abandon the two rak'ahs of Fajr, even if the horses chase you."
"4760","4135 – And as Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj — Ata narrated to me, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not more assiduous over any of the supererogatory prayers than over the two rak'ahs before dawn."
"4761","4136 – And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us — Hafs narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. Likewise is what is narrated from Abu al-Darda."
"4762","As Fahd narrated to us — Abu Nuaym narrated to us — Mis'ar ibn Kidam narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abi Malik, from Abu Abd Allah, who said: Abu al-Darda narrated to us, saying: I come to people while they are in the prayer — the dawn prayer — and pray two rak'ahs, then join the rows. That is in our view — and Allah knows best — upon a necessity calling him to that, not upon his choice of it nor upon an intent intending it while able to do its opposite. Thus should be observed concerning the two rak'ahs of Fajr, in the place in which they are prayed; do not exceed in them what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), upon which we validated these reports. And Allah, Mighty and Majestic, we ask for providence."
"4763","644 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning one who misses praying the two rak'ahs of Fajr until he prays Fajr: does he pray them right after it or after that? 4137 – al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us — Asad ibn Musa narrated to us — al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us — Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather Qays ibn Qahd: that he prayed the dawn prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) without having prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave the salutation, he gave the salutation with him, then said: He bowed the two rak'ahs of Fajr while the Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, looked at him, without disapproving of him. Abu Jafar said: This hadith is among what the people of knowledge of hadith criticize Asad ibn Musa for — among them Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud; I heard him say: I saw this hadith in the original books, stopped at Yahya ibn Sa'id. Of what the people of lineages also criticize, claiming that Yahya ibn Sa'id's grandfather is not Qays ibn Qahd either — he is Qays ibn Amr ibn Sahl: among them Muhammad ibn Isa ibn Fulayh — I heard him say, and his place in these matters was the loftiest place: Yahya ibn Sa'id — his grandfather is Qays ibn Amr ibn Sahl, not Qays ibn Qahd. Muhammad ibn Ishaq mentioned that in the lineages of the Ansar."
"4764","4138 – And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us — Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us — Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us — Sa'd ibn Sa'id ibn Qays al-Ansari narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Qays, grandfather of Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw me praying the two rak'ahs after dawn and said: What are these two rak'ahs, O Qays? I said: O Messenger of Allah, I had not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr; these are those two rak'ahs. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, remained silent toward me. Sufyan said: Ata ibn Abi Rabah used to narrate this hadith from Sa'd ibn Sa'id."
"4765","4139 – And Ismail ibn Hamdawayh al-Bikandi narrated to us — al-Humaydi narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us — Sa'd ibn Sa'id ibn Qays al-Ansari narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Qays, grandfather of Sa'd — then he mentioned the like of it."
"4766","4140 – Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — Abu Umar al-Darir narrated to us — he said: Hammad ibn Salamah said: Abd Rabbih ibn Sa'id, brother of Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, informed me: that his grandfather missed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, prayed the morning prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and when he finished his prayer, stood and prayed the two rak'ahs. The Messenger of Allah, peace be upon him, said to him: What are these two rak'ahs? He said: I had not prayed them before the morning prayer, so I prayed them now. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent toward him. Abu Jafar said: As for the hadith of Sa'd ibn Sa'id — though Sa'd ibn Sa'id is not with people like one of his two brothers, Yahya and Abd Rabbih, and they speak about his hadith — he mentioned it from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Qays, his grandfather. Muhammad ibn Ibrahim's hadith is from Abu Salamah and the like of him among the Followers; no meeting is known for him with any of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar said: This hadith entered the disconnected hadiths with the like of which the people of chains do not argue."
"4767","4141 – And Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi narrated to us with a hadith — some of the people of knowledge among our companions confirmed me in it — he said: Ali ibn Yunus narrated to us — Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Qays ibn Qahd: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him praying two rak'ahs after the morning prayer and said: What are these two rak'ahs, O Qays? He said: I had not bowed them before the prayer. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remained silent toward him. Abu Jafar said: The people of hadith criticize this hadith and do not know it, nor do they know Ali ibn Yunus, from whom Ibn Abd al-Mu'min narrated it to us. We found in this chapter, of the hadith of Qays, nothing that must be practiced in this chapter. So we sought that from the hadith of others."
"4768","4142 – So we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us — Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Kaysan, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Prophet, peace be upon him, missed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, he prayed them when the sun rose. This hadith is better in chain and worthier of practice than what we narrated before it in this chapter. There has been narrated from Abd Allah ibn Umar, from himself, the like of that."
"4769","As Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati narrated to us — Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us — Sulaym ibn Akhdar narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar came and entered the mosque while they were in the dawn prayer, without having prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. He entered with them into their prayer, then waited until the sun rose and prayer became lawful, and prayed them. Likewise is narrated from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad."
"4770","As Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us — Abu Umar narrated to us — Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari informed them, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: Had the two rak'ahs before the morning prayer escaped me, I would delay them until the sun rises, then pray them. Hammad said: Ayyub informed us, from Nafi', that Abd Allah ibn Umar missed them and prayed them after the sun rose. He said: Hammad informed us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"4771","And as Bakkar narrated to us — he said: Abd Allah ibn Humran narrated to us — al-Ash'ath narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, who said: When Ibn Umar did not pray them before the Fajr prayer, he prayed them of the forenoon. This Ibn Umar would make them up when the sun rose and prayer became lawful. That is in our view worthier than anything else said in this chapter contradicting that — given what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it, of the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, then what is narrated from Ibn Umar agreeing with it, and what is narrated from al-Qasim agreeing with that. And with Allah is providence."
"4772","645 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the rulings of guarantees of debts on behalf of the dead, what of that indicates their rulings upon the living without their instruction, and concerning payment of what one guaranteed on their behalf likewise: does its payer on their behalf have recourse for what he paid on their behalf against them in their lives, or in their estates after their deaths? 4143 – Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us — Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed us; and Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated to us — Yunus informed me — Yunus said in his hadith — and Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be brought a dead man with a debt upon him, and ask: What did he leave for settling his debt? If informed he left sufficiency, he prayed over him; otherwise he said: Pray over your companion. When Allah, Exalted, granted him conquests, he said: I am nearer to the believers than themselves; whoever dies with a debt upon him, its settlement is upon me; whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs. Abu Jafar said: In what we narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — that he would not pray over deceased debtors who left no settlement for their debts, and that he would pray over them if they left settlement for their debts, even if the settlement they left did not clear them of the debts upon them, for it may meet loss and perishing before being spent in settling the debts upon them, so the debts that were upon them remain."
"4773","4144 – Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us — Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Muhajir narrated to us, from his father, who said: Asma bint Yazid narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was called to the funeral of a man of the Ansar. When the bier was placed, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stepped forward to pray over him, then turned and said: Is there a debt upon your companion? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Pray over your companion. Abu Qatadah al-Ansari said: They are upon me, O Messenger of Allah. So he prayed over him. What is in this hadith indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the deceased debtor who left no settlement for his debt — through guarantee of his debt on his behalf after his death — like one leaving sufficiency for the debt upon him. In these reports is jurisprudence: the binding by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the guarantor for what he guaranteed on behalf of the one it is upon, without instruction from the one it is upon to him with that. In it is his binding guarantee without acceptance from the one guaranteed to, of it, from him — as Abu Yusuf and Muhammad say in that — contrary to what Abu Hanifah used to say in it, for he would not bind the guarantor for what he guaranteed except with acceptance by the one guaranteed to, of that, from him. In it also is binding guarantee for the debt upon the dead who left no settlement for it — as Abu Yusuf and Muhammad say in that — contrary to what Abu Hanifah says in it, for he would not permit guarantee with that, holding that the debt, when as we mentioned, perished with the passing of the liability in which it was. He said: Guarantee for a perished (debt) is guarantee for what has lapsed — it has no meaning. No one may depart from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a saying or a ruling."
"4774","4145 – Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Sa'id Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us — Sharik ibn Abd Allah informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah: that a man died with a debt upon him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray over him until Abu al-Yasar — or another — said: It is upon me. So he prayed over him. He came to him the next day demanding him, and he said: That was only yesterday. Then he came to him after the morrow and paid him, and he said: Now you have cooled his skin. In this hadith is what indicates binding the guarantor for the debt he guaranteed on behalf of the one it is upon; the obligation of the one guaranteed to, taking the guarantor for it; evidence that guaranteeing it did not clear the one it is upon of it, by its obligation upon the guarantor — for the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed in this hadith that the skin of the dead was cooled only by his guarantor's payment of the debt he guaranteed on his behalf, not by his lord's guaranteeing it on his behalf. In that is what indicates that the one guaranteed a debt to may demand it of the guarantor; and since he may demand it of the guarantor, the one guaranteed on behalf of — if demand of him is possible — is worthier that he have demand of him for it. In that is evidence of the soundness of what Abu Hanifah, his companions, and al-Shafi'i held, concerning guaranteed wealth: that the one guaranteed to may demand it of each of the one guaranteed on behalf of and the guarantor for it — contrary to what Malik said, that he demands the guarantor only when unable to demand the one guaranteed for, with what that guarantor guaranteed for him on his behalf. For in this hadith is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bound the guarantor for what he guaranteed, by his guaranteeing it. If one says: The one guaranteed to may demand the guarantor only because the one guaranteed on behalf of left nothing equal to what the debt-holder may take his debt from. It is said to him: Was anything of that stipulated in the guarantee? In it was only guarantee of the debt unrestrictedly. Since guarantee binds the guarantor for what he guaranteed, he must be taken for what bound him in all states. Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim mentioned that Malik used to hold the saying we mentioned from Abu Hanifah and his companions, then retracted after that from his saying to the saying we mentioned from him."
"4775","4146 – Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us — Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us — Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abd Allah ibn Mawhab, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father, that he said: A man of us died, and they took him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pray over him. He said: Did he leave anything? They said: No, by Allah, he left nothing. He said: Did he leave a debt upon him? They said: Yes, eighteen dirhams. He said: Did he leave settlement for it? They said: No, by Allah, he left no settlement for it of anything. He said: Then pray you over him. He said: Abu Qatadah said: O Messenger of Allah, what if I settle on his behalf — will you pray over him? He said: Yes, if you settle on his behalf with sufficiency, I shall pray over him. Abu Qatadah went and settled on his behalf, then came and said: I have paid what was upon him. He said: Yes. He called for him and prayed over him. Abu Jafar said: We examined this hadith and found its chain defective."
"4776","4147 – As Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us — Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us — he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bukayr ibn Abd Allah narrated to him: that Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah narrated to him: that a man of Najran asked him, while he was with Nafi' ibn Jubayr, saying: What of the hadith mentioned to us concerning the man upon whom was a debt of two dinars — the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was called to him but refused to pray over him, until Abu Qatadah bore them: did you hear your father mention that? I said: No; but one of my family whom I do not suspect narrated it to me."
"4777","4148 – And as al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us — Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us; and as Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us — he said: My father and Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth informed us — they said: al-Layth informed us, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abi Qatadah, that he said: I heard one of my family whom I do not suspect narrate that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with two dinars upon him, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused to pray over him until Abu Qatadah bore them. Since the chain of this hadith is defective, none may argue with its text against one who differs with him in it. In what we mentioned before it in this chapter — of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to one who guaranteed a debt, after his paying it on behalf of the one he guaranteed on his behalf: Now you have cooled his skin — is evidence of the soundness of what Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad used to say, concerning one who settles a debt upon a man without his instruction: that he has no recourse for it against him — contrary to what Malik used to say in it, that he has recourse for it against him, making the debt transferred, by the payment of the one who paid it on behalf of the one it was upon, from the one it was to, to the one who paid it. For had the debt transferred to the one who paid it, his paying it would not have cooled the skin of the one it was upon — for in his saying he is not cleared of the debt; in his saying it is only transferred to its payer, from the one he paid it to. No one may depart from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a saying or an act, so long as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not alert through him (an exception) from his ummah. All we mentioned in this chapter of the sayings of Abu Hanifah and his companions."
"4778","Muhammad ibn al-Abbas narrated it to us, from Ali ibn Ma'bad, from Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Yusuf, from Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, with what we mentioned from him; and from Ali, from Muhammad, from Abu Yusuf, with what we mentioned from him; and from Ali, from Muhammad, with what we mentioned from him. And Allah is the Granter of success."
"4779","646 – Chapter explaining the problematic (report) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning what people claimed indicates the permissibility of i'tikaf without fasting. 4149 – Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us — he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us — Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that Umar asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed in the pre-Islamic period to perform i'tikaf in the Sacred Mosque. He said: Fulfill your vow. Abu Jafar said: There is not in this hadith mention of what Umar had vowed to perform i'tikaf of. So we looked into that."
"4780","4150 – So we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us — Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain, except that he said: I vowed to perform i'tikaf for a night."
"4781","4151 – Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us — Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us — Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us; and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us — he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us — he said: Hafs narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Umar, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed in the pre-Islamic period a vow, and Allah has brought Islam. He said: Fulfill your vow. He did not mention in this hadith what it was he had vowed. So we looked into that."
"4782","4152 – So we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — Hafs informed us — then he mentioned the like of it with its chain, except that he said: I vowed to perform i'tikaf for a night in the Sacred Mosque. This hadith comes back to the vow being i'tikaf of a night. People went to permitting i'tikaf without fasting, arguing with this hadith in that. We looked into that: were Yahya and Hafs contradicted upon Ubayd Allah in this hadith, and in the vow that was from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him — what it was?"
"4783","4153 – So we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam al-Kurdi informed us — Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — he said: I heard Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that Umar had placed upon himself a day to perform i'tikaf of in the pre-Islamic period. He asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and he commanded him to perform i'tikaf."
"4784","4154 – And we found Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us — he said: Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar narrated to us — Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, from Umar: that he vowed in the pre-Islamic period to perform i'tikaf for a day in the Sacred Mosque. When he embraced Islam, he mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Fulfill your vow. So he did. Thereby we learned of their disagreement from Ubayd Allah in this hadith: that some of them narrate it from him that the vow was a night, and some of them narrate it from him upon the vow being a day. Neither narration was worthier than the other. Then we looked: did any besides Ubayd Allah narrate this hadith from Nafi', that we learn how what he narrated upon him from him is?"
"4785","4155 – So we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb narrated to us — he said: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yazid narrated to us; and we found Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Hawari al-Baghdadi narrated to us — he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us — they said: Sufyan narrated to us — Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani narrated to us — thus in the hadith of Abd al-Malik; and in the hadith of Ahmad: from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: Upon Umar was i'tikaf of a night in the Sacred Mosque in the pre-Islamic period. He asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who commanded him to perform i'tikaf and to fulfill his vow. In this hadith is that the vow of Umar of that was a night. We looked: was Sufyan contradicted, from Ayyub, in that?"
"4786","4156 - We found Yunus had narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me that Ayyub narrated to him that Nafi narrated to him that Abdullah ibn Umar narrated to him that Umar ibn al-Khattab asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was at al-Jiranah, saying: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed in the pre-Islamic period to observe itikaf for a day in the Sacred Mosque, what do you think? He said: Go and observe itikaf for a day."
"4787","4157 - And we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, and he mentioned the like of it. So in the two narrations of Jarir and Mamar from Ayyub of this hadith is that Umar vow was a day not a night, and that the Prophet, peace be upon him, commanded him for that vow of his to observe itikaf for a day, nothing else. And when this hadith came through the two narrations of Ubaydullah and Ayyub from Nafi as we mentioned, it is negated that there be in it a proof for whoever holds the permissibility of itikaf without fasting against whoever does not permit it except with fasting. Then we considered whether anything else has been narrated in this chapter indicating whether the vow was for what cannot be except with fasting, namely the day, or for what may be without fasting, namely the night."
"4788","4158 - So we found Ahmad ibn Shuayb had narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ali ibn Said informed us, al-Hasan ibn Hammad al-Warraq narrated to us, Amr ibn Muhammad al-Anqazi narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Budayl ibn Warqa, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar that Umar asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about an itikaf incumbent upon him, so he commanded him to observe itikaf and fast."
"4789","Abu Jafar said: So I mentioned that to Ali ibn Said ibn Bashir al-Razi, and he said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated it to me, from Amr ibn Muhammad al-Anqazi, from Abdullah ibn Budayl, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, as I mentioned."
"4790","4159 - And we found in our book, from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus, from Harun ibn Abdullah, meaning al-Hammal, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Budayl ibn Warqa narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So we established thereby that Umar vow, may Allah be pleased with him, which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to fulfill, was of what fasting occurs in, namely the daytime, not of what fasting does not occur in, namely the night. And we found in that also what confirms that Umar vow was for what fasting may occur in, not for what fasting does not occur in."
"4791","What al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Abdallah ibn Wahb narrated to me, Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Ibn Umar, they both said: There is no jiwar (itikaf) except with fasting. So it is impossible that Ibn Umar had learned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his permitting Umar itikaf of a night in which there is no fasting, then say this statement. Then an objector said: But Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated this hadith from Ibn Jurayj with what necessitates the corruption of its chain."
"4792","And he mentioned what Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us that he heard Ata say: Some of our companions informed us, from Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, that they both used to say: There is no jiwar except with fasting. I said: Is it established from them both? He said: Yes. So our answer to him in that was that there is nothing in what he mentioned that necessitates corruption of the chain of this hadith, because in it is Ata informing that the one of his companions who narrated it to him from Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas is trustworthy, and that dispenses with his naming him. Then we considered what has been narrated from any of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this matter."
"4793","So we found Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani had narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, al-Ashjai narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ata, from Aishah, she said: Whoever observes itikaf, fasting is incumbent upon him. So here is Aishah saying this statement, and there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas what we have mentioned from him, and there has also been narrated from him in this matter."
"4794","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Fakhitah, the freedman of Jadah ibn Hubayrah, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: There is no itikaf except with fasting."
"4795","And what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Fakhitah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The mutakif (one observing itikaf) is bound to fast."
"4796","And what al-Rabi al-Muradi narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, Sufyan al-Thawri informed me, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Fakhitah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The mutakif, the mujawir (one in retreat), fasts."
"4797","And what Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Hawari narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, Amr narrated to us, Abu Fakhitah Said ibn Ilaqah informed us, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: The mujawir fasts, and the mujawir is the mutakif."
"4798","And what Abd al-Malik narrated to us, al-Humaydi narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Harb informed us that Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to him that a man said to Amr ibn Dinar: O Abu Muhammad, what is the statement of Ibn Abbas: fasting is incumbent upon the mujawir? He said: Ibn Abbas did not say it like that; he only said: The mujawir fasts. Then an objector said: So this indicates that what was narrated from Ibn Abbas in this matter is only the fasting of the mujawir by choice, not by obligation. So part of our proof against him in that was that what he mentioned is not as he mentioned it, and how could it be so, when what we know for certain is that it does not occur to anyone heart that fasting is disliked in jiwar, so that he would need this statement to be said to him to permit him to fast in jiwar; rather it is in our view in agreement with what Shubah and Hushaym narrated from Amr ibn Dinar, as we mentioned, of the obligatoriness of fasting in itikaf. Then we found from Ibn Abbas in that."
"4799","What Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Hiwari narrated to us, from al-Darawardi, Abu Suhayl ibn Malik informed me, he said: I and Ibn Shihab gathered with Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, may Allah be pleased with him, and my wife was bound to observe itikaf of three days in the Sacred Mosque, so Ibn Shihab said: There is no itikaf except with fasting. So Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz said: Is it a command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: No. He said: Is it then a command of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: No. He said: Is it then a command of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: No. He said: Is it then a command of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: No. Abu Suhayl said: So I left and found Tawus and Ata, and asked them about that. So Tawus said: Ibn Abbas used not to see any fasting incumbent upon the mutakif except what he imposes upon himself. Ata said: That is my opinion. So in this hadith is from Ibn Abbas that he used not to see any fasting upon the mutakif. And we have narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that whoever observed itikaf, fasting is incumbent upon him. So we established thereby that this chapter is of what the statements in it have counterbalanced each other, and whatever is so, it is necessary to return in it to reflective reasoning, so that it judges between those who differ in it. So we considered in that, and we found of the proof of whoever holds that itikaf may be without fasting, and of those who hold that is al-Shafii, he argues for what he said of that that we do find the mutakif entered upon by the night in which he is not fasting, while he is in itikaf, so he argued thereby for the permissibility of itikaf without fasting. So we found of the proof against him in that for his opponents in it - and they are Abu Hanifah and his companions, and Malik and his companions, and al-Thawri and his companions - that we have found itikaf is not exited by the entering of the night upon the mutakif in which his fasting is not valid, and we have found the like of that, namely that itikaf is only in the mosques in which itikaf is observed, and is not on roads nor elsewhere than mosques, and we have found the mutakif leaves the mosques for defecation and urination, so he comes to be in houses and roads in which itikaf is not valid for him, yet he is not thereby outside his itikaf, since he cannot do without that. So like that is the entering of the night upon him in which there is no fasting in his itikaf; that does not take him out of his itikaf; rather the entering of the night upon him in what we mentioned, in which he has no action, did not take him out of his itikaf, while going out from the mosques to what we mentioned is by his action, yet that was so. And when what is by his action, of what initial itikaf is not valid in, of what we mentioned, does not take him out of his itikaf, then the entering of the night upon him in which he has no action is more deserving not to take him out of his itikaf. Then we have found itikaf is only remaining in mosques, so we considered remaining in places in which remaining is an act of devotion: does it occur in a state of sanctity from remaining in them, or without sanctity from him in his remaining? So we found Mina and Arafah and Muzdalifah: remaining in them in the sanctity of hajj is devotion, and it is the remaining that has meaning, while we found remaining in them outside hajj is not so, and has no ruling by which the one remaining in them is distinguished from his remaining in other houses. So like that is remaining in mosques: when it is in its sanctity, the one remaining in it is thereby distinguished from one remaining in other houses and the like. And there is no sanctity in which what he remains in that sanctity occurs except the sanctity of fasting. So that was a proof that itikaf is only with fasting. Then an objector said: But it has been narrated from Yala ibn Umayyah that he used to sit in the mosque for an hour and count that as itikaf."
"4800","And he mentioned what Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Yala ibn Umayyah said to a companion of his: Sit, let us observe itikaf for an hour in the Sacred Mosque."
